CHAPTER I GENERAL PROVISIONS

Article 1 (Purpose) The purpose of this Act is to manage motor vehicles efficiently and ensure the performance and safety of motor vehicles by prescribing the matters regarding the registration, safety standards, self-certification, correction of manufacturing defects, checkup, maintenance, inspection of motor vehicles, and motor vehicle management business, etc., thereby contributing to public welfare.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]

Article 2 (Definitions) The terms used in this Act are defined as follows: <Amended on May 24, 2011; Dec. 18, 2012; Mar. 23, 2013; Jan. 7, 2014; Aug. 11, 2015; Dec. 29, 2015; Jan. 28, 2016; Jun. 9, 2020; Aug. 16, 2023; Feb. 13, 2024>
  1. "Motor vehicle" means an instrument manufactured for the purpose of movement on land by an engine or an instrument manufactured for the purpose of movement on land by being towed (hereinafter referred to as "towed motor vehicle"); provided, those prescribed by Presidential Decree shall be excluded herefrom;
  1-2. "Engine" means a power-generating device, such as an internal combustion engine or an electronic motor, manufactured mainly for driving motor vehicles;
  1-3. "Autonomous vehicle" means a motor vehicle which can self operate without any operation by its driver or passenger;
  1-4. "Incomplete motor vehicle" means a motor vehicle equipped with its chassis and other minimum structure and devices, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, which further requires additional manufacturing or assembling processes so that it can be used in accordance with its usage;
  1-5. "Motor vehicle manufactured by phase" means a motor vehicle manufactured by phase in order to ensure that the operation prescribed in subparagraph 2 (referring to the act of enabling a motor vehicle’s use in accordance with its usage) is enabled by using an incomplete motor vehicle;
  1-6. "Traction battery" means a battery that stores electric energy for the purpose of powering motor vehicles or a medium for storing electric energy that has functions similar to those of such battery;
  1-7. "Connected motor vehicle" means the use of means of transportation defined in subparagraph 3 of Article 2 of the National Integrated Transport System Efficiency Act, transportation facilities under subparagraph 4 of that Article, other devices, facilities, equipment, apparatus, etc., and wireless information and communications technology means a motor vehicle that transmits or receives information.
  1-8. “End-of-life battery” means a traction battery whose use has ended after being installed in an electric vehicle defined in subparagraph 3 of Article 2 of the Act on Promotion of Development and Distribution of Environment-Friendly Motor Vehicles, hybrid vehicles defined in subparagraph 5 of that paragraph, and hydrogen electric vehicles defined in subparagraph 6 of that paragraph (hereinafter referred to as “electric vehicles, etc.”);
  1-9. “Remanufactured battery” means a traction battery for electric vehicles, etc., that is manufactured and assembled from end-of-life batteries classified in the remanufacturing grade based on the results of a performance evaluation under Article 35-13, following repairs such as the replacement of parts; 
  2. "Operation" means an act of using a motor vehicle according to its usage, regardless of the transportation of people or freight;
  3. "Motor vehicle user" means the owner of a motor vehicle or a person who has been entrusted with the matters regarding operation, etc. of a motor vehicle by its owner;
  4. "Model" means any configuration, specification, and performance regarding the structure and devices of a motor vehicle;
  4-2. "Pressure-resistant container" means a container manufactured for the purpose of being installed in a motor vehicle, as prescribed in subparagraph 2 of Article 3 of the High-Pressure Gas Safety Control Act in order to use high-pressure gas as fuel (including a valve and a safety device of the container);
  4-3. "Motor vehicle cyber attack/threat" refers to acts of invading, disrupting, paralyzing, or destroying parts, devices, information and communication devices, or information systems related thereto of motor vehicles by electronic means such as hacking, computer viruses, denial of service, or electromagnetic waves, or acts of forging, altering, damaging, or leaking software, motor vehicles control information, etc. of motor vehicles and threats related thereto;
  4-4. "Motor vehicle cyber security management system" refers to a comprehensive management system that includes administrative, technical, and physical protection measures to protect motor vehicles from motor vehicle cyber attacks and threats.
  4-5. "Software" means software defined in subparagraph 1 of Article 2 of the Software Promotion Act, which is installed in a motor vehicle;
  4-6. "Software update" means altering, adding, or deleting software;
  4-7. “Pedal misapplication prevention device” means a device that detects misapplication of the accelerator pedal by the driver and controls unintended sudden acceleration;
  5. "Vehicle scrapping" means dismantling a motor vehicle and then compressing, crushing, or cutting the devices of the motor vehicle set forth in Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport so that it cannot maintain its performance, or compressing or crushing a motor vehicle without dismantling it;
  6. "Motor vehicle management business" means motor vehicle transaction business, motor vehicle maintenance business, and motor vehicle scrapping and recycling business;
  7. "Motor vehicle transaction business" means any business engaged in the sale and purchase of motor vehicles (excluding newly constructed motor vehicles and motorcycles), intermediation therein and vicarious execution of application for their registration;
  8. "Motor vehicle maintenance business" means any business engaged in the checkup and maintenance of motor vehicles (excluding motorcycles) or the tuning works; provided, those prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall be excluded herefrom;
  9. "Motor vehicle scrapping and recycling business" means any business engaged in the takeover of motor vehicles requested for scrapping (excluding motorcycles), collection of reusable parts, scrapping a motor vehicle and vicarious execution of application for deletion of registration;
  10. "Accident recording device" means any device or function that stores operation information about a motor vehicle and verifies information stored for a specific period of time before and after an accident set forth in Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, including motor vehicle collisions;
  11. "Tuning of a motor vehicle" means altering a part of the structure and devices of a motor vehicle or adding attachments to the motor vehicle; provided, this shall not apply where only software updates are made without any physical change in the structure and devices of the automobile;
  12. "Standard maintenance time" means the average time required for each maintenance work which is prepared, publicly disclosed and used by the association of the motor vehicle maintenance business entities;
  13. "Motor vehicle subject to total loss" means a motor vehicle, the condition of which is completely damaged, destroyed, or stained to make it unrepairable, or the amount of damage or loss of which and the amount of the expenses to be borne by its insurance company is at least the insurable value, which is classified as follows by the insurance company defined in Article 2 of the Insurance Business Act (hereinafter referred to as "insurance company"):
  a. Where it is classified as a stolen or lost motor vehicle;
  b. Where it is classified as a repairable motor vehicle;
  c. Where it is classified as unrepairable and subject to be scrapped;
  14. "Motor vehicle auction" means disposal of motor vehicles (excluding newly constructed motor vehicles and motorcycles), after establishing an auction place pursuant to Article 60, by the method of selling them by auction (including the auction through electronic transactions defined in subparagraph 5 of Article 2 of the Framework Act on Electronic Documents and Transactions).
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]

Article 3 (Types of motor vehicles) (1) Motor vehicles shall be classified as follows: <Amended on May 24, 2011; Mar. 23, 2013; Aug. 27, 2019; Jun. 9, 2020>
  1. Passenger vehicles: A motor vehicle suitably manufactured to transport up to 10 persons;
  2. Motor vehicles for passengers and freight: A motor vehicle suitably manufactured to transport 11 persons or more; provided, any of the following motor vehicles shall be deemed motor vehicles for passengers and freight, regardless of the number of passengers:
  a. A motor vehicle suitably manufactured to transport up to 10 persons with special equipment or facilities fitted in the motor vehicle;
  b. A front-steering motor vehicle suitably manufactured to transport up to 10 persons as a compact motor vehicle prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport;
  c. Deleted; <Aug. 27, 2019>
  3. Trucks: A motor vehicle equipped with a loading space which makes the car suitable for transporting freight and in which the gross weight for a carload of freight in the loading space is heavier than that of the passengers when all passengers, except the driver, are on board in the boarding space;
  4. Special motor vehicles: A motor vehicle suitably manufactured for the towing of other motor vehicles or for rescue work or other uses and that is neither a passenger motor vehicle, a motor vehicle for passengers and freight, nor a truck;
  5. Motorcycles: A motorcycle and other motor vehicles with a structure similar thereto, which are suitably manufactured to transport one or two persons, regardless of the size of total displacement or rated output of a motor vehicle.
  (2) The detailed criteria for classification under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, based on the criteria, such as size, structure, motor type, total displacement, or rated output of a motor vehicle. <Added on May 24, 2011; Mar. 23, 2013>
  (3) The types of motor vehicles classified in paragraph (1) may be sub-classified in accordance with the standards prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on May 24, 2011; Mar. 23, 2013>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]

Article 4 (Guidance and supervision of motor vehicle management work) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall guide and supervise any motor vehicle management work prescribed under the authority of the Special Metropolitan City Mayor, a Metropolitan City Mayor, a Special Self-Governing City Mayor, a Do Governor, or a Special Self-Governing Province Governor (hereinafter referred to as the "Mayor/Do Governor") or a Metropolitan City Mayor, a Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu (hereinafter referred to as the "head of a Si/Gun/Gu"; the head of a Gu refers to the head of an autonomous Gu) in this Act in order to establish an adequate and efficient system for motor vehicle management and to improve the efficiency of administrative work for motor vehicle management. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Oct. 24, 2017>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]

Article 4-2 (Formulation of master plans for motor vehicle policy) (1) In order to efficiently manage motor vehicles and to improve safety thereof, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall formulate and implement a master plan for motor vehicle policy (hereinafter referred to as "master plan") every five years. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (2) The master plan shall include the following: <Amended on Oct. 20, 2020; Feb. 13, 2024>
  1. Prospect for development of motor vehicle-related technologies, and promotional direction of the motor vehicle safety and management policies;
  2. Matters regarding the research and development, foundation creation and international compatibility of motor vehicle safety standards under Article 29;
  3. Matters regarding increasing safety of motor vehicles;
  4. Matters regarding a system for managing motor vehicles and protection of consumers;
  4-2. Matters regarding the formulation of the standards for inspection of connected motor vehicles to which new technology is applied, safety management, and research, development, and dissemination of technology and devices related to inspection of motor vehicles;
  5. Other matters necessary for safety and management of motor vehicles.
  (3) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport intends to formulate a master plan in accordance with paragraph (1), he or she shall first consult with the head of a relevant central administrative agency and the Mayor/Do Governor, and finalize the plan after deliberation thereon by the National Transport Committee pursuant to Article 106 of the National Transport System Efficiency Act. The same shall also apply to any modification to the master plan (excluding insignificant modifications prescribed by Presidential Decree). <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (4) Once a master plan is finalized, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall notify the head of a relevant central administrative agency and the Mayor/Do Governor thereof, and shall publicly announce it (including posting on the Internet). <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (5) Matters necessary for formulating and modifying a master plan under paragraphs (1) through (4), and other necessary matters, shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Added on May 24, 2011]

CHAPTER II MOTOR VEHICLE REGISTRATION

Article 5 (Registration) No motor vehicle (excluding motorcycles; hereafter in this Article through Article 47-12, the same shall apply) shall be operated unless it is registered in the motor vehicle register (hereinafter referred to as "register"); provided, this shall not apply where a motor vehicle is operated with temporary operation permission obtained under Article 27(1) within the permitted period. <Amended on Oct. 24, 2017; Feb. 4, 2020>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]

Article 6 (Validity of change of ownership of motor vehicles) Acquisition, loss or change of the ownership of a motor vehicle shall take effect only through registration.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]

Article 7 (Motor vehicle register) (1) The Mayor/Do Governor shall furnish and manage the register. <Amended on Jan. 6, 2015>
  (2) Where the register is fully or partially destroyed or lost, the Mayor/Do Governor shall take measures necessary for its restoration, as prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (3) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport or the Mayor/Do Governor shall take measures necessary to prevent destruction, mutilation, or other illicit leak of the register or any descriptions recorded therein and to preserve the same. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (4) A person who intends to inspect the register or to receive a certified copy or abridged copy thereof shall file an application therefor with the Mayor/Do Governor, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (5) When the Mayor/Do Governor allows a person to inspect the register or issues a certified copy or abridged copy thereof pursuant to paragraph (4), he or she may omit part of the descriptions recorded therein, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, for preventing disclosure of personal information. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (6) The register shall specify a motor vehicle’s registration number, identification number, name, main place of use, title owner, engine model, traction battery identification number (only applicable to an electric vehicle defined in subparagraph 3 of Article 2 of the Act on Promotion of Development and Distribution of Environment-Friendly Motor Vehicles), vehicle type, usage, detailed type, changed structure and device, term of validity of inspection, information on any mortgage right, and other matters required to be publicly notified. In such cases, the details to be included, documentary forms and method of statement shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Added on Jan. 6, 2015; Aug. 16, 2023>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]

Article 8 (New registration) (1) A person who intends to make a new registration of a motor vehicle shall file an application therefor (hereinafter referred to as "new registration") with the Mayor/Do Governor, as prescribed by Presidential Decree. Where a motor vehicle to be newly registered falls under any of the following cases, the Mayor/Do Governor shall confirm with a person who files an application for newly registration of a motor vehicle the fact that the purchaser has received a notice under Article 8-2 from a person who manufactures, assembles, or imports motor vehicles (including persons entrusted with the sale of motor vehicles by such person; hereinafter referred to as "motor vehicle manufacturer, seller, etc."), as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport: <Amended on Jan. 9, 2024>
  1. Where the motor vehicle of which the registration for deletion has been made due to return pursuant to Article 13(1)2;
  2. Where any malfunction, flaw, or other defects as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport has occurred to a motor vehicle before its delivery after it was released from the factory by its manufacturer (referring to the date of manufacture of the motor vehicle; hereinafter the same shall apply).
  (2) Upon receipt of an application for the new registration, the Mayor/Do Governor shall record necessary matters in the register and issue a motor vehicle registration certificate.
  (3) Where a person who manufactures, assembles, or imports motor vehicles (including persons entrusted with the sale of motor vehicles by such person; hereinafter referred to as "motor vehicle manufacturer, seller, etc.") has sold a motor vehicle, he or she shall immediately transmit information on a certificate of manufacture of a motor vehicle necessary for entering in the register to the electronic information processing organization established under Article 69, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, and shall file an application for new registration without delay on behalf of the buyer; provided, this shall not apply where a buyer himself or herself files an application for new registration, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Dec. 18, 2012; Mar. 23, 2013; Jan. 9, 2024>
  (4) Where any motor vehicle manufacturer, seller, etc. files an application for new registration under paragraph (1), he or she may collect a fee from a buyer of a motor vehicle, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]

Article 8-2 (Duty to notify motor vehicle manufacturers and sellers) (1) Where any motor vehicle manufacturer, seller, etc. sells a motor vehicle regarding which registration for deletion has been made due to return under Article 13(1)2, he or she shall notify the buyer of the fact that the relevant motor vehicle has been returned to the manufacturer and immediately transmit it to the computerized information processing system under Article 69; provided, in cases of a motor vehicle returned upon request for replacement or refund under Article 47-2, such notification that include the reason for replacement or refund shall be transmitted and such reason shall also be stated in the certificate of manufacture of the relevant motor vehicle. <Amended on Jan. 7, 2014; Jan. 9, 2024>
  (2) Where any motor vehicle manufacturer, seller, etc. sells a motor vehicle, he or she shall notify the buyer of the repair of malfunction, flaw and other defects that occurred before the delivery of the motor vehicle after it was released from the factory by its manufacturer (referring to the date of manufacture of the motor vehicle) and its current condition, and shall immediately transmit them to the electronic information processing system under Article 69. <Added on Jan. 7, 2014; Oct. 24, 2017; Jan. 9, 2024>
  [This Article Added on Dec. 18, 2012]

Article 8-3 (Provision of information on traction batteries for electric vehicles) (1) When the motor vehicle manufacturer, seller, etc. sells motor vehicles prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, such as electric vehicles, etc. defined in subparagraph 3 of Article 2 of the Act on Promotion of Development and Distribution of Environment-Friendly Motor Vehicles, he or she shall provide the purchaser with information prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, including the manufacturer, capacity, voltage, etc. of the traction battery installed in the relevant motor vehicle.
  (2) Necessary matters, such as the methods, procedure, etc. for providing information under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.
  [This Article Added on Dec. 2, 2025]

Article 9 (Rejection of new registration) The Mayor/Do Governor shall reject new registration in any of the following cases: <Amended on Jun. 9, 2009; Jan. 28, 2015; Dec. 29, 2015>
  1. Where no legitimate cause exists for the acquisition of the relevant motor vehicle or there are any false entries in an application for registration;
  2. Where any identification number or engine model of a motor vehicle prescribed in Article 22 is not indicated, or the number or model indicated is inconsistent with that of the self-certification mark of a motor vehicle prescribed in Article 30(4) or a new inspection certificate prescribed in Article 43(3);
  3. Where a person intends to register a motor vehicle for commercial use inconsistent with the license, registration, or approval, or reported details of the passenger transport service under the Passenger Transport Service Act or of the trucking transport business under the Trucking Transport Business Act;
  4. Where a person intends to make a registration, in violation of the restrictions on use of liquefied petroleum gas as fuel prescribed in Article 28 of the Safety Control and Business of Liquefied Petroleum Gas Act;
  5. Where a person intends to register a motor vehicle without certification of manufactured motor vehicles under Article 48 of the Clean Air Conservation Act and Article 31 of the Noise and Vibration Control Act, or which has used asbestos in its brake system;
  6. Where it is an incomplete motor vehicle.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]

Article 10 (Motor vehicle registration license plates) (1) A Mayor/Do Governor shall attach a motor vehicle registration number plate (hereinafter referred to as "registration number plate"), as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport; provided, where the owner of a motor vehicle or a person who files an application for registration in lieu of the owner of a motor vehicle pursuant to the main clause of Article 8(3) or the main clause of Article 12(2) intends to directly attach a registration number plate, he or she shall directly attach the registration number plate, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Feb. 20, 2024>
  (2) No registration license plate affixed under paragraph (1) shall be detached except in any of the following cases: <Amended on Nov. 15, 2022; Feb. 20, 2024>
  1. Where permission is obtained from a Mayor/Do Governor;
  2. Where a motor vehicle maintenance business entity registered under Article 53 temporarily detaches a registration license plate and seal for maintenance in the place of business, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport;
  3. Where any provisions are otherwise provided in other statutes.
  (3) Where a registration license plate is detached or illegible, the owner of a motor vehicle shall reapply with the Mayor/Do Governor for affixing of the registration license plate, as prescribed in paragraph (1). <Amended on Feb. 20, 2024>
  (4) No motor vehicle shall be operated without affixing a registration license plate under paragraphs (1) and (3); provided, this shall not apply where a temporary operation permit license plate under Article 27(2) has been affixed. <Amended on Feb. 20, 2024>
  (5) No one shall cover a registration license plate nor make it illegible, and no motor vehicle with the covered or illegible registration license plate shall be operated.
  (6) No person shall manufacture, import, sell, or offer devices to be used for shielding a registration license plate intentionally or making it illegible. <Added on May 24, 2011>
  (7) Where the registration number plate becomes invisible because it is covered by a certain device for carrying a bicycle that is affixed to a motor vehicle or by other external devices, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, the owner of the aforementioned motor vehicle shall file an application with the Mayor/Do Governor for affixing of the registration number plate for external devices, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. Paragraphs (1) through (6) shall apply mutatis mutandis to the registration number plate for external devices. <Added on May 23, 2012; Mar. 23, 2013; Jun. 9, 2020>
  (8) Where a registration license plate is returned, the Mayor/Do Governor shall destroy and discard them so that they cannot be re-used. <Amended on May 24, 2011; May 23, 2012; Feb. 20, 2024>
  (9) No person shall affix a registration number plate by any means other than affixing a registration number plate under paragraph (1) for the purpose of interfering with the business of keeping the registration number plate in custody, and no person shall operate such motor vehicle. <Added on Jan. 6, 2015; Jun. 9, 2020; Feb. 20, 2024>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]

Article 11 (Registration for modification) (1) Where any matter recorded in the register is modified (excluding cases corresponding to the registration of transfer under Article 12 and the registration for deletion under Article 13), the owner of a motor vehicle shall file with the Mayor/Do Governor an application for the registration for modification (hereinafter referred to as "registration for deletion") as prescribed by Presidential Decree; provided, this shall not apply to any modification to insignificant registered matters prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (2) Subparagraphs 3 and 4 of Article 9 shall apply mutatis mutandis to the registration for modification.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]

Article 12 (Registration of transfer) (1) A person who acquires a registered motor vehicle by transfer shall file with the Mayor/Do Governor an application for registration of transfer of ownership of the motor vehicle (hereinafter referred to as "registration of transfer"), as prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (2) Where a person who has filed for registration of motor vehicle transaction business (hereinafter referred to as "motor vehicle dealer") prescribed in Article 53 sells a motor vehicle or brokers the sale of a motor vehicle, he or she shall file an application for registration of transfer under paragraph (1), on behalf of its buyer; provided, this shall not apply where the motor vehicle dealer sells a motor vehicle or brokers the sale of the motor vehicle to another motor vehicle dealer and where the buyer files an application for registration of transfer in person, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (3) Where a person who has acquired a motor vehicle by transfer intends to re-transfer it to any third party, he or she shall apply for registration of transfer as prescribed in paragraph (1) under his or her own name prior to such transfer.
  (4) Where a person who has acquired a motor vehicle by transfer fails to file an application for registration of transfer as prescribed in paragraph (1), a transferor (referring to the owner recorded in the register as at the time of applying for registration of transfer) may apply for such registration on behalf of such transferee, as prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (5) Upon receipt of an application for registration of transfer under paragraph (4), the Mayor/Do Governor shall accept the registration, as prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (6) Where the insurance company has received an application for registration of transfer with respect to the motor vehicle subject to total loss, the Mayor/Do Governor shall accept the application only where the inspection of repair under Article 43(1)5 is completed for the relevant motor vehicle. <Added on Aug. 11, 2015>
  (7) Subparagraphs 1, 3, and 4 of Article 9 shall apply mutatis mutandis to the registration of transfer under paragraphs (1) and (4). <Amended on Aug. 11, 2015>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]

Article 12-2 (Interested party's application for issuance of motor vehicle register) Where transfer of the relevant motor vehicle is registered, an interested party on the register may file with the Mayor/Do Governor an application for perusal or issuance of the register or an abridged copy thereof, and the Mayor/Do Governor who receives such application shall comply.
  [This Article Added on May 24, 2011]

Article 13 (Registration for deletion) (1) Where a registered motor vehicle falls under any of the following cases, the owner of the motor vehicle (including any administrator of property and inheritor; hereafter in this Article the same shall apply) shall return the motor vehicle registration certificate and the registration license plate of the relevant motor vehicle, as prescribed by Presidential Decree, and shall file an application for registration for deletion (hereinafter referred to as "registration for deletion") with the Mayor/Do Governor; provided, in cases falling under subparagraph 7 or 8, the owner may file an application for registration for deletion, and in cases falling under subparagraph 9, 1 or more persons among those who jointly own the motor vehicle (any administrator of property and inheritor; hereinafter referred to as “joint owners”) may file a registration for deletion. <Amended on Oct. 24, 2017; Feb. 20, 2024; Dec. 2, 2025>
  1. Where motor vehicle scrapping is requested to a person who has filed for registration of motor vehicle scrapping and recycling business under Article 53 (hereinafter referred to as "motor vehicle scrapping and recycling business entity");
  2. Where a motor vehicle is returned to the motor vehicle manufacturer, seller, etc. (including cases where a motor vehicle is returned upon request for replacement or refund under Article 47-2);
  3. Where the age of a motor vehicle as prescribed by the Passenger Vehicle Transport Service Act has been exceeded;
  4. Where the license, registration, authorization, or report has been declared invalid or canceled under the Passenger Transport Service Act and the Trucking Transport Business Act;
  5. Where it is impracticable to restore the functions of the motor vehicle which has been destroyed by a natural disaster, traffic accident, or fire;
  6. Where a motor vehicle is exported;
  7. Where it is deemed that there remains no market value in terms of the criteria prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as the vehicle age, among the motor vehicles for which no succeeding procedures for compulsory execution have been performed even after the completion of a seizure registration made under Article 14; in such cases, where the Mayor/Do Governor accepts a request for registration for deletion from the owner of the relevant motor vehicle, he or she shall promptly notify such fact to the court or the administrative office delegating such seizure registration, and to the interested parties on the motor vehicle register;
  8. Where a motor vehicle falls under any ground prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as use for the purpose of education or research;
  9. Where 1 or more of the joint owners request scrapping of the motor vehicle under subparagraph 1, and all of the following requirements are met:
  a. Where there are special circumstances that make it difficult to obtain the consent of all joint owners regarding the scrapping and registration for deletion of a motor vehicle, such as their unknown whereabouts or loss of contact; 
  b. Where the motor vehicle is deemed to have no residual value based on criteria prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as vehicle age and mileage.
  (2) In cases falling under paragraph (1)1, a motor vehicle scrapping and recycling business entity, and in cases falling under paragraph (1)6, a person who exports motor vehicles shall file an application for registration for deletion, respectively, on behalf of the owner of the relevant motor vehicle; provided, this shall not apply where the owner of a motor vehicle files an application for registration for deletion in person, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (3) The Mayor/Do Governor may, ex officio, file an application for registration for deletion in any of the following cases: <Amended on Aug. 11, 2015; Apr. 13, 2021; Dec. 7, 2021>
  1. Where a person obligated to file an application for registration for deletion prescribed in paragraphs (1) and (2) fails to do so;
  2. Where the chassis of a motor vehicle (referring to the motor vehicle body for a motor vehicle with no chassis; hereinafter the same shall apply) is different from the one recorded in the register;
  3. Where a person continues to operate a motor vehicle, despite an order to suspend the operation of the relevant motor vehicle pursuant to Article 24-2(2) or 37(3);
  4. Where a motor vehicle is scrapped under Article 26;
  5. Where the registration has been made by fraud or other improper means.
  6. Where a person fails to comply with an order to purchase mandatory insurance referred to in Article 6(3) of the Compulsory Motor Vehicle Liability Security Act for at least one year.
  (4) Where the Mayor/Do Governor intends to file an application for registration for deletion, ex officio, prescribed in paragraph (3), he or she shall give the registered owner of the motor vehicle and interested person a notification, stating the grounds for and the scheduled date of the registration for deletion, by no later than one month before the registration for deletion; provided, this shall not apply where the appropriate owner of the motor vehicle and the interested person agree on the registration for deletion for the motor vehicle or in cases falling under paragraph (1)3 or 5 or (3)4. <Amended on Aug. 11, 2015>
  (5) Where the Mayor or Do Governor deletes registration of a motor vehicle ex officio pursuant to paragraph (3), he or she shall notify the person who has owned the motor vehicle. In such cases, the other party who receives the notification shall return the vehicle registration certificate and registration number plate of the vehicle without delay, except in cases where there are unavoidable reasons as prescribed by the Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013, Feb. 20, 2024>
  (6) Where the Mayor/Do Governor has filed an application for registration for deletion, ex officio, under paragraph (3), he or she may detain or discard the motor vehicle registration certificate and the registration license plate of the relevant motor vehicle after giving notification under paragraph (4). <Amended on Feb. 20, 2024>
  (7) Where the owner of a motor vehicle falls under any of the following cases, he or she may file an application for registration for deletion with the Mayor/Do Governor, as prescribed by Presidential Decree: <Amended on Dec. 29, 2015; Aug. 27, 2019>
  1. Where he or she has his or her motor vehicle stolen;
  2. Where he or she has his or her motor vehicle embezzled or deprived of by fraud.
  (8) A person who has filed an application for registration for deletion under paragraph (1)6 (referring to a person who applied for registration for deletion pursuant to paragraph (2), where the owner of a motor vehicle did not make an export) shall report to the Mayor/Do Governor on whether he or she has made an export, as prescribed by Presidential Decree. In such cases, where he or she has failed to export the relevant motor vehicle, he or she may request a motor vehicle scrapping and recycling business entity to scrap a motor vehicle or may apply for new registration made under Article 8. <Amended on May 24, 2011>
  (9) A person interested in a motor vehicle, for which the registration for deletion has been filed, may apply for the issuance of a certificate proving the registration for deletion to the Mayor/Do Governor.
  (10) Where a person intends to filed for re-registration of a motor vehicle, for which the registration for deletion has been made, he or she shall apply for new registration, as prescribed by Presidential Decree. In such cases, if a right of mortgage, etc. was registered in the register as of the aforementioned registration for deletion, he or she shall prove that the relevant right of mortgage, etc. has been dissolved, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Mar. 18, 2014; Jun. 9, 2020>
  (11) Where the Mayor/Do Governor can verify, by using the electronic information processing organization prescribed in Article 69 or by jointly utilizing administrative information prescribed in Article 36(1) of the Electronic Government Act, whether exporting motor vehicles is performed, a person who has filed his or her application for de-registration pursuant to paragraph (8) shall be deemed to have reported to the Mayor/Do Governor concerning whether the aforementioned exportation of motor vehicles is performed. <Added on Jan. 28, 2016>
  (12) Upon receiving an application for registration for deletion under paragraph (1)9, the Mayor/Do Governor shall register the motor vehicle for deletion if all of the following requirements are met: <Added on Dec. 2, 2025>
  1. Where the combined shares of the joint owners who have given their consent amount to at least 50/100 of the total shares held by all joint owners; 
  2. Where the whereabouts of a joint owner whose consent has not been obtained are unknown, and no objection is raised within 90 days after public notice of the application for registration for deletion, as prescribed by Presidential Decree. 
  (13) A motor vehicle owner who has requested for scrapping of the motor vehicle in accordance with paragraph (1)9, shall, upon completion of scrapping of the motor vehicle, pay the other joint owners an amount corresponding to their ownership shares, if there is any remaining balance after covering the costs incurred; provided, if the owner is unable to pay the amount corresponding to the other joint owners’ ownership shares, the motor vehicle owner shall deposit such amount in accordance with the Deposit Act. <Added on Dec. 2, 2025>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]

Article 14 (Registration for seizure) In any of the following cases, the Mayor/Do Governor shall file for registration for seizure in the register of the relevant motor vehicle, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport: <Amended on Mar. 31, 2010; Mar. 23, 2013; Jan. 6, 2015; Dec. 27, 2016>
  1. Where a request is made by a court to register seizure under the Civil Execution Act;
  2. Where a request is made by an administrative agency to register seizure under the National Tax Collection Act or the Local Tax Collection Act;
  3. Where the public institution prescribed in Article 4 of the Act on the Management of Public Institutions (hereinafter referred to as "public institution") has entrusted the affairs of the registration for seizure.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]

Article 14-2 (Affairs to be processed for release of seizure) (1) The administrative or public institution which has entrusted the affairs of registration of seizure in accordance with subparagraph 2 or 3 of Article 14 (hereinafter referred to as "institution entrusting registration of seizure") may authorize the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport to conduct, on its behalf, the affairs of receipt, settlement, entrustment of revocation of seizure with respect to the amount of State tax, local tax and administrative fines, etc. in arrears and other affairs to be processed for release of seizure.
  (2) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport has conducted de-registration of seizure, he or she shall notify the institution entrusting registration of seizure and the Mayor/Do Governor thereof, as prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Added on Jan 6, 2015]

Article 14-3 (De-registration of seizure) The Mayor/Do Governor, in receipt of the notification pursuant to Article 14-2(2), shall conduct de-registration of the seizure with respect to the relevant motor vehicle as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.
  [This Article Added on Jan 6, 2015]

Article 15 Deleted. <Apr. 15, 1999>

Article 16 (Assignment of motor vehicle registration numbers) Where new registration for a motor vehicle has been filed, the Mayor/Do Governor shall assign a registration number to the relevant motor vehicle (hereinafter referred to as "registration number"), and in the event of any ground prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as the change of usage, the Mayor/Do Governor shall change and assign the registration number.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]

Article 17 Deleted. <Apr. 15, 1999>

Article 18 (Placement of motor vehicle registration certificates) (1) Deleted. <Aug. 11, 2015>
  (2) Where a motor vehicle registration certificate is lost or indiscernible, a motor vehicle owner shall file an application for reissuance.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]

Article 19 (Issuance of registration license plates) Methods of manufacturing and issuing registration license plates under Article 10 shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Feb. 20, 2024>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]

Article 20 (Designation of registration license plate issuance agents) (1) Where a Mayor/Do Governor deems it necessary, he or she may designate a person who handles as proxy the manufacture and issuance of registration license plates under Article 19 (hereinafter referred to as "registration license plate issuance agent"), as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. In such cases, the method of designating agents and the agency period may be prescribed by Municipal Ordinance of the relevant local government. <Amended on Dec. 29, 2009; Mar. 23, 2013; Feb. 20, 2024>
  (2) Standards for facilities, equipment, etc. that the registration license plate issuance agents need to have, procedures for designation of such agents, and other necessary matters, shall be determined by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (3) The registration license plate issuance agent may receive a registration plate issuance fee as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on March 23, 2013; Feb. 20, 2024>
  (4) In the case of managing a convexity for manufacturing motor vehicle registration license plate, registration license plate issuance agents shall take necessary safety measures to prevent the theft of the relevant convexity, and shall not take out the respective convexity.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]

Article 21 (Cancellation of designation as registration license plate issuance agents) (1) Where a registration license plate issuance agent falls under any of the following cases, the Mayor/Do Governor may cancel the designation of such agent or order the suspension of business for a period of up to six months; provided, the Mayor/Do Governor shall cancel the designation in cases falling under subparagraph 1 or 10: <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Feb. 20, 2024>
  1. Where he or she has been designated by fraud or other improper means;
  2. Where he or she has failed to meet any of the standards for facilities, equipment, etc. referred to in Article 20(2);
  3. Where a convexity for manufacturing motor vehicle registration license plate is stolen or taken out, in violation of Article 20(4);
  4. Where he or she has failed to report under Article 72(1), or files a false report;
  5. Where he or she has refused, interfered with or evaded an inspection under Article 72(2), or failed to reply to questions or replied by falsity;
  6. Where he or she has accepted unjust enrichment or engaged in other improper conduct in relation to his or her business;
  7. Where it is deemed impossible for him or her to continue the business due to such causes as bad financial status;
  8. Where he or she refuses the issuance of a registration license plate without good cause;
  9. Where he or she manufactures and delivers a registration license plate, in violation of the standards concerning specification, such as the type, quality of the material, or color of a registration license plate, as publicly notified by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport;
  10. Where he or she has operated the business during the suspension period, in violation of an order to suspend business issued under this Article.
  (2) Detailed standards and procedures for taking dispositions under paragraph (1), and other necessary matters, shall be determined by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]

Article 22 (Mark of identification numbers of motor vehicles) (1) The identification number or the engine model shall be marked in a motor vehicle in accordance with Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (2) No person shall mark the identification number or engine model of a motor vehicle, other than a person who engages in the business of manufacturing or assembling motor vehicles or engines, or a person designated by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]

Article 23 (Prohibition on erasing marks) (1) No one shall erase a mark of the identification number or the engine model of a motor vehicle, nor make it indiscernible; provided, this shall not apply where approval has been obtained from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport due to an unavoidable reason and where an order has been issued under paragraph (2). <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (2) Where a motor vehicle falls under any of the following cases, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may order its owner to erase a mark of the identification number or engine model number or order to have it marked: <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  1. Where a vehicle has no mark of the identification number or engine model number, or the marking method and its system are not in compliance with Article 22(1);
  2. Where a mark of the identification number or engine model number of a motor vehicle is similar to that of another motor vehicle;
  3. Where a mark of the identification number or of the engine model number of a motor vehicle is erased or indiscernible.
  (3) Where a person intends to erase a mark or have it marked under the proviso of paragraph (1) and (2), he or she shall file an application therefor with a person who engages in the business of manufacturing or assembling motor vehicles or engines, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, or with a person designated by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. In such cases, the expenses incurred therein may be collected from the owner of the motor vehicle in accordance with Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on May 24, 2011; Mar. 23, 2013>
  (4) Necessary procedures regarding approval and orders as prescribed in the proviso of paragraph (1) and paragraph (2) shall be determined by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]

Article 24 Deleted. <Apr. 15, 1999>

Article 24-2 (Suspension of operation of motor vehicles) (1) A motor vehicle shall be operated by the motor vehicle user as defined in subparagraph 3 of Article 2.
  (2) Where a person who fails to satisfy the requirement under paragraph (1) operates a motor vehicle, without good cause, the Mayor/Do Governor or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu may order such person to suspend the operation thereof, with consent or at the request of either of the following persons: <Amended on Feb. 21, 2018>
  1. Consent or request of the owner of the motor vehicle;
  2. Request of the head of an investigation agency; provided, this shall apply only where the head of an investigation agency has confirmed the fact that a person other than a motor vehicle user defined in subparagraph 3 of Article 2 operated the motor vehicle.
  (3) Where the Mayor/Do Governor or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu orders suspension of operation of a motor vehicle pursuant to paragraph (2), he or she shall perform the following:
  1. Recording the disposition of suspension of the relevant motor vehicle’s operation in the register;
  2. Providing the Commissioner General of the National Police Agency with the information about the relevant motor vehicle, such as its motor vehicle registration number and specifications, which are necessary to prevent and investigate operation of suspended motor vehicles;
  3. Where necessary, detaining the motor vehicle’s registration license plate and notifying such fact to the Mayor/Do Governor or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu and to the motor vehicle owner;
  4. Publicly announcing the motor vehicle registration number, grounds for suspension of operation and specifications of the motor vehicle, etc. in the Official Gazette and on the website.
  (4) With respect to the motor vehicle against which the Mayor/Do Governor or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu orders suspension of operation pursuant to paragraph (2), if necessary, the Mayor/Do Governor or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu may sell it by public auction to redeem the amount of charges in arrears.
  (5) Where expert knowledge is required for the public auction under paragraph (4) or where a direct public auction is deemed inappropriate due to other extraordinary circumstances, the Mayor/Do Governor or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu may authorize the Korea Asset Management Corporation established pursuant to the Act on the Establishment of Korea Asset Management Corporation to conduct the public auction as a proxy. In such cases, the public auction shall be deemed to have been conducted by the Mayor/Do Governor or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu. <Amended on Nov. 26, 2019>
  (6) Matters regarding the consent, request or order to suspend operation and the method of detaining a registration license plate pursuant to paragraphs (2) and (3) shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.
  [This Article Added on Aug. 11, 2015]

Article 25 (Restrictions on operation of motor vehicles) (1) If the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport deems that any of the following causes or events occurs, he or she may order restrictions on the operation of motor vehicles in prior consultation with the Commissioner of the National Police Agency: <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Feb. 4, 2020>
  1. Responding to war, disaster, or other similar emergencies;
  2. Preventing or addressing heavy traffic congestion;
  2-2. If recurrent fires caused by the operation of defective motor vehicles referred to in Article 31(1) are likely to pose serious hazards to public safety;
  3. Preventing air pollution or other grounds prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (2) If the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport intends to restrict the operation of motor vehicles prescribed in paragraph (1), he or she shall publicly announce, after deliberation by a relevant cabinet meeting, the purpose, period, area, details of restrictions, types of target motor vehicles and other necessary matters in advance. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Dec. 29, 2015>
  (3) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport orders restrictions on the operation of motor vehicles under paragraph (1)2-2, the motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. under Article 30(3) or the parts manufacturer, etc. under Article 30-2(1) shall prepare measures for protecting the owners of motor vehicles, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, shall disclose such measures to the owners of motor vehicles by mail, text message to a mobile phone, etc. and shall implement the measures. <Added on Feb. 4, 2020>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]

Article 26 (Compulsory disposal of motor vehicles) (1) Any owner or possessor of a motor vehicle (including any item which has the shape similar to that of a motor vehicle; hereafter in this Article the same shall apply) shall not engage in any of the following conduct: <Amended on Aug. 27, 2019>
  1. Fixing a motor vehicle on a certain place and using it for purposes other than operation;
  2. Leaving a motor vehicle unattended on the road continuously;
  3. Leaving a motor vehicle unattended on another person's land without good cause for a period of not less than the period prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (2) If the head of a Si/Gun/Gu deems that a case falls under any of the subparagraphs of paragraph (1), he or she shall issue an order or other disposition to the owner or possessor of a motor vehicle to scrap the motor vehicle, or order him or her to take appropriate measures, such as taking the relevant motor vehicle back, etc., as determined by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, after moving the relevant motor vehicle to a specific place. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (3) Where the owner or possessor of a motor vehicle fails to comply with an order as prescribed in paragraph (2) or the owner or possessor of the relevant motor vehicle cannot be identified, the head of a Si/Gun/Gu may sell or scrap the motor vehicle, as prescribed by Presidential Decree. In such cases, the expenses incurred in selling or scrapping the motor vehicle may be collected from the owner or possessor of the relevant motor vehicle.
  (4) In the case of having a motor vehicle sold or scrapped under paragraph (3), the balance, if any, after appropriating for the expenses incurred therein, shall be paid to the owner or possessor of the relevant motor vehicle; provided, where the owner or possessor of the motor vehicle cannot be identified, the balance shall be deposited under the Deposit Act.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]

Article 26-2 (Scrapping of flooded motor vehicles subject to total loss) (1) The owner of a flooded motor vehicle subject to total loss shall request that a motor vehicle scrapping and recycling business entity scrap his or her motor vehicle, within the period prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Nov. 15, 2022>
  (2) No person shall export a flooded motor vehicle subject to total loss or a device prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport that is installed in such motor vehicle and directly related to the safe operation of motor vehicles or sell the motor vehicle or device to an exporter. <Added on Nov. 15, 2022>
  [This Article Added on Apr. 13, 2021]

Article 27 (Permission for temporary operation) (1) A person who intends to operate a motor vehicle temporarily without filing for registration of it shall obtain permission to temporarily operate the motor vehicle (hereinafter referred to as "temporary operation permission") from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport or the Mayor/Do Governor, as prescribed by Presidential Decree; provided, a person who intends to operate an autonomous vehicle for the purposes of testing or research shall satisfy the requirements for safe operation as prescribed by Decree of the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, in connection with the objects to be permitted, the devices for detecting and warning malfunction, devices for disabling various functions, areas for operating in and other matters the driver is to comply with; and shall obtain temporary operation permission from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Aug. 11, 2015>
  (2) Upon receipt of an application for temporary operation permission, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport or the Mayor/Do Governor shall grant permission and issue a temporary operation permit and a temporary operation permit license plate, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport; provided, where a person requests for a one-day temporary operation permit by fixing a restricted operations area for export purposes and where he or she deems it necessary to allow the operation of a motor vehicle without a temporary operation permit license plate affixed thereon, he or she need not issue the temporary operation permit license plate. <Amended on May 24, 2011; Mar. 23, 2013; Jun. 9, 2020>
  (3) A motor vehicle for which temporary operation permission is granted shall be operated within the limit of the purpose and period of such permission, with and the temporary operation permit license plate (excluding cases falling under the proviso of paragraph (2)) affixed to it. <Amended on May 24, 2011; Jun. 9, 2020; Feb. 20, 2024>
  (4) A person who has obtained temporary operation permission, upon the expiry of the period prescribed in paragraph (3), shall return a temporary operation permit and temporary operation permit license plate within the period, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (5) A person who has obtained temporary operation permission under the proviso of paragraph (1) shall report to the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport the matters specified by the Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, including information on operation, such as changes in main systems and functions and operation records, and information on traffic accidents, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, in order to ensure safe operation of autonomous vehicles. <Added on Oct. 24, 2017>
  (6) If necessary to verify matters reported on in accordance with paragraph (5), the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may authorize the person designated to conduct a performance test, as proxy, under Article 32(3) to investigate such matters. <Added on Oct. 24, 2017>
  (7) If an investigation conducted under paragraph (6) finds that a motor vehicle fails to meet the requirements for safe operation prescribed in the proviso of paragraph (1) or that a motor vehicle is highly likely to cause a traffic accident, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may issue an order to take corrective measures and temporarily suspend the operation of such motor vehicle; provided, if an autonomous vehicle causes a traffic accident while in operation and is found to have a safety defect, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may immediately issue an order to temporarily suspend the operation of such motor vehicle. <Added on Oct. 24, 2017>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]

Article 28 (Special cases concerning objection to registration of motor vehicles) (1) A person dissatisfied with the registration of a motor vehicle conducted by the Mayor/Do Governor may file an objection, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport at any time. <Amended on Jan. 9, 2024>
  (2) Upon receipt of an objection filed under paragraph (1), the Mayor/Do Governor shall immediately correct it if he or she deems it reasonable.
  (3) Where the Mayor/Do Governor has made a correction as prescribed in paragraph (2) or deems the objection unreasonable, he or she shall without delay notify an applicant and an interested person who is recorded in the register thereof.
  (4) A person dissatisfied with the registration of a motor vehicle may file a request for administrative appeal under the Administrative Appeals Act or institute an administrative litigation under the Administrative Litigation Act, regardless of whether an objection has been filed under paragraph (1).
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]
  [Title Amended on Jan. 9, 2024]

CHAPTER III SAFETY STANDARDS AND SELF-CERTIFICATION FOR MOTOR VEHICLES

Article 29 (Structure and devices of motor vehicles) (1) If the structure and devices of a motor vehicle as prescribed by Presidential Decree fail to meet the performance requirements and standards required for safe operation (hereinafter referred to as "safety standards for motor vehicles"), the motor vehicle shall not be operated.
  (2) Any parts, devices, or safety equipment, as prescribed by Presidential Decree, which are installed or used on a motor vehicle (hereinafter referred to as "motor vehicle parts") shall satisfy the performance requirements and standards required for safe operation (hereinafter referred to as "safety standards for parts").
  (3) Storage facilities, gas appliances, piping facilities, and other facilities to be installed for the purposes of cooking and camping with liquefied petroleum gas in any vehicle for camping prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport; and electric facilities and camping facilities therefor shall comply with the standards for safety as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Added on Aug. 11, 2015; Aug. 27, 2019>
  (4) The safety standards for motor vehicles and the safety standards for parts shall be determined by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Aug. 11, 2015>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]

Article 29-2 (Research and development for safety standards) (1) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may authorize a person designated as an agent for performance tests under Article 32(3) (hereinafter referred to as "performance testing agent") to perform research and development for safety standards for motor vehicles or parts thereof under Article 29(1) and (2), safety standards for pressure-resistant containers under Article 35-5(1), or safety-related technologies and to establish and operate a database for such research and development, if necessary. In such cases, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall contribute or subsidize such research and development and establishment and operation of a database, within the budget. <Amended on May 24, 2011; Mar. 23, 2013; Dec. 26, 2017; Feb. 13, 2024>
  (2) Deleted. <May 24, 2011>
  [This Article Added on Feb. 6, 2009]

Article 29-3 (Installation of event data recorders and provision of information) (1) A motor vehicle manufacturer, seller, etc. shall install an accident recording device in motor vehicles that meet the standards prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, such as the type of motor vehicle, purpose of use, and the number of passengers, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Feb. 13, 2024>
  (2) Where any motor vehicle manufacturer, seller, etc. sells a motor vehicle in which an accident recording device is installed pursuant to paragraph (1), he or she shall notify the buyer of the fact that the relevant motor vehicle is fitted with the accident recording device.
  (3) Where an owner of a motor vehicle or any person determined by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport demands the provision of recorded information, the motor vehicle manufacturer, seller, etc. who has installed an accident recording device pursuant to paragraph (1) shall provide each of the following information: <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Jan. 28, 2016>
  1. Information recorded in the relevant motor vehicle’s accident recording device;
  2. Where the information prescribed in subparagraph 1 is analyzed pursuant to this Act or other statutes or regulations, any reports on the findings of such analysis.
  (4) A motor vehicle manufacturer, seller, etc. shall ensure that equipment capable of extracting the information stored in event data recorders (hereinafter referred to as "event data extraction equipment") is distributed and sold on the market. <Added on Dec. 3 2024>
  (5) Matters necessary for the standards for installation of event data recorders, notification of installation, methods of providing recorded information, standards for preparing and methods of providing result reports, and distribution and sale, etc. of event data extraction equipment under paragraphs (1) through (4) shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Mar. 23 2013; Jan. 28 2016; Feb. 13 2024; Dec. 3 2024>
  (6) No person shall provide any false information recorded in an event data recorder or any analysis results thereof. <Added on Dec. 3 2024>
  [This Article Added on Dec. 18, 2012]
  [Title Amended on Dec. 3, 2024]

Article 30 (Self-certification of motor vehicles) (1) A person who intends to manufacture, assemble or import (hereinafter referred to as "manufacture, etc.") a motor vehicle (including the incomplete motor vehicle and the motor vehicle manufactured by phase; The same shall apply in this Article, Article 30-2 through 30-5, 31, 31-2, 32, 32-2 and 33) shall self-certify (hereinafter referred to as "self-certification of motor vehicles") that the model of the relevant motor vehicle meets the safety standards for motor vehicles (in cases of an incomplete motor vehicle or a motor vehicle manufactured by phase, referring to the safety standards for motor vehicles to be applied as at the time a manufacture, etc. concerned is finished), as determined by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Dec. 29, 2015>
  (2) A person who intends to make a self-certification of motor vehicles shall register facilities for the manufacture, testing, or inspection of motor vehicles, etc. with the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, as determined by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. The same shall also apply to any modification to important matters determined by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, of the registered matters. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (3) A motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. who has failed to satisfy the requirements for self-certification determined by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, such as the scale of manufacture, test facilities for safety and performance, etc., from among the persons who have made a registration prescribed in paragraph (2) (hereinafter referred to as "motor vehicle manufacturer, etc."), shall perform the self-certification of motor vehicles on the matters directly related to the safe operation of motor vehicles, which are determined by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, by undergoing technology review and safety inspections from the performance testing agent; provided, the motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. who is equipped with test facilities for safety among the requirements for self-certification capabilities may directly conduct a safety inspection, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on May 24, 2011; Mar. 23, 2013>
  (4) Where a motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. has performed the self-certification of motor vehicles prescribed in paragraph (1) or (3), he or she shall notify the specifications of the motor vehicle to the performance testing agent, and put the mark (including the date of manufacture of the motor vehicle) of self-certification of motor vehicles on the relevant motor vehicle, as determined by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Aug. 27, 2019>
  (5) Regarding the manufactured or assembled motor vehicles with their number of production not more than the number prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, a motor vehicle manufacturer or assembler may, notwithstanding the safety standards for motor vehicles prescribed in paragraph (1), conduct the self-certification of motor vehicles by the method of safety-checking, the level of which is similar to the aforementioned safety standards for motor vehicles, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. In such cases, the motor vehicle manufacturer or assembler shall conduct the self-certification of motor vehicles after undergoing the technology review and safety inspections conducted under paragraph (3). <Added on Dec. 29, 2015>
  (6) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport verifies that the facilities for the manufacture, testing, or inspection of motor vehicles registered under paragraph (2) are different from the details registered, he or she may cancel the relevant registration or order modification to matters registered. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Dec. 29, 2015>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]

Article 30-2 (Self-certification of motor vehicle parts) (1) A person who manufactures, assembles, or imports motor vehicle parts (hereinafter referred to as "parts manufacturer, etc.") shall certify that the relevant motor vehicle parts satisfy the safety standards for parts (hereinafter referred to as "self-certification of parts"), as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (2) A parts manufacturer, etc. shall file for registration of the name of the parts manufacturer, type of the motor vehicle part, etc. with the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. The same shall also apply to any modification to important matters determined by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, of the registered matters. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (3) Where any parts manufacturer, etc. has self-certified parts, he or she shall notify the performance testing agent of the specifications of parts, and place the mark of self-certification of parts on the relevant automobile part, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (4) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport confirms that the name of the parts manufacturer, type of the motor vehicle parts, etc. registered under paragraph (2) are different from the details of the registration, he or she may revoke such registration or order modification to the matters registered. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (5) Where a motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. has self-certified a motor vehicle pursuant to Article 30(1), the motor vehicle parts installed on the relevant motor vehicle shall be deemed to have undergone self-certification of parts.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]

Article 30-3 (Suspension of manufacture or sale of motor vehicles or motor vehicle parts) (1) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may order a motor vehicle manufacturer, etc., a parts manufacturer, etc., or a manufacturer, etc. of replacement parts or tuning parts, the performance and quality of which has been certified in accordance with Articles 30-5(3) and 34-3(2) to suspend the manufacture, assembly, import, or sale of the relevant motor vehicle or motor vehicle parts., if the manufacturer falls under any of the following subparagraphs; provided, in cases falling under subparagraph 1, the Minister shall suspend the manufacture, assembly, import, or sale: <Amended on May 24, 2011; Mar. 23, 2013; Jan. 7, 2014; Oct. 24, 2017; Feb. 4, 2020; Aug. 16, 2023; Feb. 13, 2024; Feb. 27, 2026>
  1. Where he or she has self-certified motor vehicles or parts or has obtained the certification of the performance and quality of replacement parts or tuning parts by fraud or other improper means;
  1-2. Where a ground exists for restricting operation under Article 25(1)2-2;
  2. Where he or she has self-certified motor vehicles not in compliance with the safety standards for motor vehicles, in violation of Article 30;
  3. Where he or she has self-certified motor vehicle parts not in compliance with the safety standards for parts, in violation of Article 30-2(1);
  3-2. Where he or she has manufactured, assembled, or imported motor vehicle parts without filing registration of the part manufacturer’s name, types of the motor vehicle parts, etc., in violation of Article 30-2(2);
  3-3. Where he or she has failed to satisfy the standards for certification of the performance and quality of replacement parts prescribed in Article 30-5(5);
  3-4. Where he or she fails to obtain certification of a cyber security management system for motor vehicles, in violation of Article 30-9(1);
  3-5. Where the certification of a cyber security management system for motor vehicles is revoked or the validity thereof is suspended pursuant to Article 30-11(1);
  3-6. Where he or she has failed to satisfy the standards for certification of tuning parts under Article 34-3(4);
  4. Where he or she has failed to comply with a corrective order issued under the main clause of Article 31(3);
  5. Where he or she has sold a motor vehicle which is manufactured differently from the details of self-certification of motor vehicles;
  6. Where he or she has sold a motor vehicle part which is different from the details of self-certification of parts;
  7. Where he or she has sold the replacement parts different from the authorized replacement parts;
  8. Where he or she has sold a tuning part different from the certified tuning parts under Article 34-3;
  9. Where he or she sells a motor vehicle manufactured, etc. differently from the details of certification of a cyber security management system for motor vehicles;
  10. Where he or she has sold electric vehicles, etc. that failed the pre-distribution safety inspection, in violation of Article 35-16(2).
  (2) In order to confirm whether a motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. or parts manufacturer, etc. falls under any of the subparagraphs of paragraph (1), the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may have a performance testing agent conduct the investigation of such fact. In such cases, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall subsidize such investigation. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (3) Where an investigation is conducted pursuant to paragraph (2), a core device or part that shall be certified by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport pursuant to Article 30-7 shall be excluded from such investigation; provided, this shall not apply to a core device or part that is deemed to have obtained safety certification under the proviso of Article 30-7(1). <Added on Aug. 16, 2023>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]
  [Title Amended on Oct. 24, 2017]

Article 30-4 (Exemption from self-certification of motor vehicles) In any of the following cases, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may exempt the self-certification of motor vehicle, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport: <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  1. Where an approval for import is exempted pursuant to the Foreign Trade Act, for a motor vehicle which is imported by bringing in as one's property from moving residency;
  2. Where a motor vehicle, which falls under any of subparagraphs 1 through 3 of Article 70, is imported as has been domestically operated;
  3. Where a motor vehicle is imported as are used in the U.S. armed forces stationed in the Republic of Korea pursuant to the Act on Temporary Special Cases concerning the Customs Act Following the Implementation of the Agreement under Article 4 of the Mutual Defense Treaty between the Republic of Korea and the United Stated of America, regarding Facilities and Areas and the Status of United States Armed Forces in the Republic of Korea;
  4. Where the Government, a local government, a motor vehicle manufacturer, or a test and research institute manufactures motor vehicles, etc. for testing or research purposes, or in other cases falling under an event or cause determined by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]

Article 30-5 (Certification of performance and quality of replacement parts) (1) A replacement part means a part that can be used in replacement of a part installed in a motor vehicle released by a motor vehicle manufacturer. <Amended on Oct. 24, 2017>
  (2) Deleted. <Jun. 10, 2022>
  (3) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may designate a person who meets the standards prescribed by Decree of the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport to authorize the person to certify the performance and quality of replacement parts. <Amended on Oct. 24, 2017>
  (4) A manufacturer, etc. of replacement parts the performance and quality of which have been certified (hereinafter referred to as "quality certified parts") by a person designated under paragraph (3) to certify the performance and quality of replacement parts (hereinafter referred to as "agency certifying replacement parts") may indicate such certification on the relevant replacement parts. <Amended on Oct. 24, 2017; Jun. 12, 2018; Feb. 20, 2024>
  (5) Matters regarding the procedures for designating the agency certifying replacement parts under paragraph (3), and the standards and methods for certifying the performance and quality of replacement parts and indication, etc. of certification under paragraph (4) shall be prescribed by Decree of the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Oct. 24, 2017>
  [This Article Added on Jan. 7, 2014]

Article 30-6 (Revocation of designation of agencies certifying replacement parts) (1) In any of the following cases, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may revoke the designation of an agency certifying replacement parts; or may order an agency certifying replacement parts to suspend his or her business operations for a period of up to six months; provided, he or she shall revoke the designation in cases falling under subparagraph 1 or 2:
  1. Where a person has obtained designation as an agency certifying replacement parts by fraud or other improper means;
  2. Where an agency certifying replacement parts certifies the performance and quality of replacement parts by fraud or other improper means;
  3. Where an agency certifying replacement parts ceases to meet the standards for designating agencies certifying replacement parts under Article 30-5(3);
  4. Where an agency certifying replacement parts certifies replacement parts not conforming to the standards for the certification of performance and quality prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport under Article 30-5(5);
  5. Where it fails to file a report under Article 72(1) or files a false report;
  6. Where it refuses, obstructs, or evades an inspection under Article 72(2), fails to respond to an inquiry, or gives a false answer;
  7. Where an agency certifying replacement parts fails to observe any other rule prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport regarding the certification of replacement parts.
  (2) Matters regarding detailed guidelines and procedures for the measures referred to in paragraph (1) and other necessary matters shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.
  [This Article Newly Amended on Oct. 24, 2017]

Article 30-7 (Safety certification of core devices) (1) Notwithstanding Article 30 and 30-2, a motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. or a parts manufacturer, etc. (hereinafter referred to as "motor vehicle or parts manufacturer, etc.") shall obtain certification (hereinafter referred to as "safety certification") of the fact that a core device or part prescribed by Presidential Decree to which a new technology, such as a traction battery, is applied (hereinafter referred to as "core device, etc.") meets the safety standards for motor vehicles under Article 29(1) and the safety standards for parts under Article 29(2) (hereinafter referred to as "safety standards for motor vehicles, etc.") from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport; provided, where a motor vehicle or parts manufacturer, etc. is deemed to have certified the fact that a core device, etc. meets the safety standards for motor vehicles, etc. under an agreement between countries, he or she shall be deemed to have obtained the safety certification of such core device, etc. (including certification of, or a report on, modification under paragraph (2)).
  (2) Where a motor vehicle or parts manufacturer, etc. intends to modify an important matter prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport that affects safety and performance, among the details of safety certification obtained under paragraph (1), he or she shall obtain the certification of such modification; provided, any modification is to be made to a minor matter, a report on the modification shall be filed with the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.
  (3) Where a motor vehicle or parts manufacturer, etc. has obtained safety certification under paragraph (1), he or she shall indicate such safety certification, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.
  (4) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may revoke safety certification granted under paragraph (1) in any of the following cases and may order a motor vehicle or parts manufacturer, etc. to suspend the manufacture, assembly, import, or sale of the relevant motor vehicle or core device, etc.; provided, in cases falling under subparagraph 1, the safety certification shall be revoked and an order shall be issued to suspend the manufacture, assembly, import, or sale of the motor vehicle or core device, etc.: <Amended on Dec. 2, 2025>
  1. Where the motor vehicle or parts manufacturer, etc. obtains the safety certification by fraud or other improper means;
  2. Where the motor vehicle or parts manufacturer, etc. fails to take the corrective measures under Article 30-8(5);
  3. Where the same defect has occurred at least the number of times prescribed by Decree of Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport within the past 2 years; provided, minor defects prescribed by Decree of Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall be excluded.
  (5) Where a motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. installs a core device, etc., the safety certification of which has been obtained by a parts manufacturer, etc. under paragraph (1), in a motor vehicle, he or she shall be deemed to have obtained the safety certification of such core device, etc.
  [This Article Added on Aug. 16, 2023]

Article 30-8 (Procedures for safety certification of core devices) (1) When a person who intends to obtain safety certification has conducted the manufacture, etc. of motor vehicles or parts (in cases of motor vehicles, referring to before self-certification of motor vehicles under Article 30), he or she shall file an application for testing whether the safety standards for motor vehicles, etc. are satisfied (hereinafter referred to as "safety performance test") with the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. In such cases, the Minister shall issue a safety certificate where he or she deems that the manufacture, etc. of a core device, etc. is conducted in conformity with the safety standards for motor vehicles, etc.
  (2) Upon receipt of an application under paragraph (1), the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may allow a performance testing agent to conduct a safety performance test on the relevant core device, etc. on his or her behalf.
  (3) A safety performance test conducted by a performance testing agent pursuant to paragraph (2) may be substituted with any of the following safety performance tests; in such cases, the performance testing agent shall confirm whether safety performance test facilities are in conformity with the standards for safety performance test facilities under paragraph (7) and may confirm a safety performance test in accordance with the standards for confirming safety performance tests, if necessary:
  1. A safety performance test conducted by a testing institution designated by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport;
  2. A safety performance test conducted by a motor vehicle or parts manufacturer, etc. equipped with the testing facilities prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.
  (4) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may inspect whether a motor vehicle or parts manufacturer, etc. conducts the manufacture, etc. of a core device, etc. in conformity with the safety standards for motor vehicles, etc. even after granting the safety certification of such core device, etc. (hereinafter referred to as "conformity inspection"). In such cases, the Minister may allow a performance testing agent to conduct a conformity inspection on his or her behalf.
  (5) Where the manufacture, etc. of a core device, etc., the safety certification of which has been granted, is found not to be in conformity with the safety standards for motor vehicles, etc. upon a conformity inspection under paragraph (4), the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may order the relevant motor vehicle or parts manufacturer, etc. to take corrective measures.
  (6) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport allows a performance testing agent to conduct a safety performance test or a conformity inspection on his or her behalf under paragraph (2) or (4), he or she shall provide the performance testing agent with subsidies to cover the expenses incurred in necessary facilities, equipment, certification, etc.
  (7) The methods and procedures for safety certification, safety performance tests, and conformity inspections of core devices, etc. under paragraphs (1) and (4); safety performance tests and conformity inspections conducted by proxy under paragraphs (2) and (4); designation of testing institutions, and the standards and procedures for confirming safety performance test facilities and safety performance tests under paragraph (3); and other necessary matters shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.
  [This Article Added on Aug. 16, 2023]

Article 30-9 (Certification of motor vehicle cyber security management system) (1) A motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. who intends to conduct self-authentication of motor vehicles prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport (hereinafter referred to as "motor vehicle subject to certification") in consideration of the type, production quantity, functions, etc. of motor vehicles shall establish a cyber security management system for motor vehicles and obtain certification from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport (hereinafter referred to as "motor vehicle cyber security management system certification").
  (2) Where a person who has obtained certification of a cyber security management system for motor vehicles intends to change any certified matter, the person shall obtain certification of modification from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport; provided, where the person intends to modify insignificant matters prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, the person shall file a report thereon with the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.
  (3) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may prescribe and publicly notify standards for certification, including administrative, technical, and physical protective measures (hereinafter referred to as "standards for certification of a cyber security management system for motor vehicles"), and other necessary matters for certification of a cyber security management system for motor vehicles.
  (4) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport establishes standards for certification of a cyber security management system for motor vehicles, the Minister shall consult the Minister of Science and ICT in advance.
  (5) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may have a performance testing agent perform the following affairs on his or her behalf in order to efficiently perform affairs concerning certification, etc. of a cyber security management system for motor vehicles under paragraphs (1) and (2); in such cases, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall contribute or subsidize expenses incurred in the construction of facilities for certification of a cyber security management system for motor vehicles, installation and examination of equipment, etc.:
  1. An examination to confirm whether a cyber security management system for a motor vehicle established by a motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. who has applied for certification of a cyber security management system for a motor vehicle conforms to the standards for certification of a cyber security management system for a motor vehicle or an examination for certification of modification under paragraph (2);
  2. Issuance and management of certificates;
  3. Other affairs deemed necessary by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport for certification of a cyber security management system for motor vehicles.
  (6) The procedures, methods, and period of validity of certification of a cyber security management system for motor vehicles and certification of modification under paragraph (2) and other necessary matters shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.
  [This Article Added on Feb. 13, 2024]

Article 30-10 (Request for submission of data related to motor vehicle cyber security management system) (1) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport deems it necessary to secure the safety and reliability of a cyber security management system for motor vehicles, the Minister may request a person who has obtained certification of a cyber security management system for motor vehicles to submit data on the establishment and operation of a cyber security management system for motor vehicles, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. In such cases, no person in receipt of a request to submit data shall refuse to submit data without good cause.
  (2) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may record and manage the data under paragraph (1) through the electronic data processing system under Article 69.
  [This Article Added on Feb. 13, 2024]

Article 30-11 (Revocation of certification of motor vehicle cyber security management system) (1) Where a person who has obtained certification of a motor vehicle cyber security management system falls under any of the following subparagraphs, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may revoke the certification or issue an order to suspend the effect thereof for a specified period not exceeding six months; provided, in cases falling under subparagraph 1 or 2, the Minister shall revoke the relevant certification:
  1. Where he or she obtains certification of a cyber security management system for motor vehicles by fraud or other improper means;
  2. Where he or she sells a motor vehicle subject to certification during the period of suspending the effect of the certification of a cyber security management system for motor vehicles;
  3. Where he or she changes any certified matter without obtaining certification for modification or fails to report any modification, in violation of Article 30-9(2);
  4. Where it fails to meet the standards for certification of a cyber security management system for motor vehicles;
  5. Where he or she refuses to submit data without good cause, in violation of Article 30-10.
  (2) The standards and procedures for revoking or suspending the effect of certification of a cyber security management system for motor vehicles under paragraph (1) and other necessary matters shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.
  [This Article Added on Feb. 13, 2024]

Article 30-12 (Reporting of motor cycle cyber attacks or threats) (1) A motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. who has obtained certification of a cyber security management system for motor vehicles pursuant to Article 30-9(1) shall immediately report such fact to the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport when an accident related to a cyber attack or threat to a motor vehicle occurs.
  (2) Where a report under paragraph (1) is about a computer security incident under Article 2(1)7 of the Act on Promotion of Information and Communications Network Utilization and Information Protection, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall share the relevant information with the Minister of Science and ICT or with the Korea Internet and Security Agency under Article 52 of the Act.
  (3) Except as provided in this Act, motor vehicle cyber attacks and threats, the "Act on Promotion of Information and Communications Network Utilization and Information Protection" shall apply.
  [This Article Added on Feb. 13, 2024]

Article 31 (Correction of manufacturing defects) (1) A motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. or a parts manufacturer, etc. (limited to cases where motor vehicle parts are sold separately from motor vehicles; hereafter the same shall apply in this Article and Articles 31-2 through 31-4 and 33), if a motor vehicle or parts manufactured by him or her has any defect prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport exists, such as failing to conform to motor vehicle safety standards or safety standards for motor vehicle parts, or impeding safety due to a problem in design, manufacture, or performance, shall disclose the fact without delay and take corrective measures by sending mail, sending text messages using mobile phones, etc. as prescribed by the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure, and Transport Ordinance from the date of learning of the fact so that the motor vehicle owner can clearly understand the fact and the corrective action plan (including the details of the manufacturing defect, parts supply plan, and implementation plan for the corrective action plan such as securing a dedicated work space; hereinafter the same shall apply in this Article); provided, economic compensation may be provided in lieu of corrective measures for any of the following defects provided in the safety standards for motor vehicles or safety standards for parts. <Amended on Dec. 29, 2009; May 24, 2011; Mar. 23, 2013, Jan. 6, 2015, Dec. 29, 2015, Feb. 4, 2020; Apr. 13, 2021; Sep. 14, 2023; Jan. 16, 2024>
  1. Overstatement of the energy consumption rate;
  2. Overstatement of the engine power;
  2-2. Excessive indication of the driving distance after a single charge of an electric vehicle (referring to an electric vehicle defined in subparagraph 3 of Article 2 of the Act on Promotion of Development and Distribution of Environment-Friendly Motor Vehicles; hereafter the same shall apply in this Article);
  3. Defects prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, similar to those prescribed in subparagraphs 1, 2, and 2-2.
  (2) The relevant motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. or parts manufacturer, etc. who intends to make an economic compensation in lieu of corrective measures in accordance with the proviso of paragraph (1) shall submit a plan therefor to the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Added on May 24, 2011; Mar. 23, 2013; Jan. 17, 2017>
  (3) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall issue an order for correction to a motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. or parts manufacturer, etc. who fails to publicize such defect or take corrective measures under the main clause of paragraph (1), as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport; provided, where a plan for economic compensation is submitted in accordance with paragraph (2), and matters not in compliance with the safety standards for motor vehicles or motor vehicle parts are deemed the defects referred to in the proviso of paragraph (1), he or she may not issue an order for correction, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on May 24, 2011; Mar. 23, 2013; Jan. 6, 2015; Jan. 17, 2017>
  (4) Where it is necessary to verify whether a motor vehicle or a motor vehicle part manufactured, etc. has any defect, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may have a performance testing agent investigate it. In such cases, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall subsidize the expenses associated with the facilities and equipment required for such investigation and expenses for the investigation, etc. <Amended on May 24, 2011; Mar. 23, 2013>
  (5) When a performance testing agent intends to conduct an investigation pursuant to paragraph (4), he or she shall give prior notice thereof to the motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. or the parts manufacturer, etc.; and the motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. or the parts manufacturer, etc. shall submit materials necessary for the investigation of defects under paragraph (4) to the performance testing agent within the period prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport; provided, this shall not apply where a report on the plan for corrective measures or the plan for economic compensation has been filed pursuant to paragraph (8) within the prescribed period. <Added on Feb. 4, 2020>
  (6) Where a motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. or a parts manufacturer, etc. fails to submit data pursuant to paragraph (5), despite falling under the requirements prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as repeated occurrences of fires in the same type of motor vehicle or accidents caused by the operation of motor vehicle devices differently from the intention of the driver, it shall be presumed that there is a defect under paragraph (1). <Added on Feb. 4, 2020; Jun. 9, 2020; Feb. 13, 2024>
  (7) If necessary for an investigation under paragraph (4), the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may order a motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. or a parts manufacturer, etc. to provide the manufacturer, etc. with relevant motor vehicles or parts. In such cases, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall pay a fair price to the motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. or the parts manufacturer, etc., as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Added on Feb. 4, 2020>
  (8) When a motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. or a parts manufacturer, etc. takes corrective measures or makes economic compensation pursuant to paragraph (1), (3), or (14), he or she shall file a report on the plan for corrective measures or the plan for economic compensation (hereinafter referred to as "plan for corrective measures, etc.") and the progress with the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on May 24, 2011; Mar. 23, 2013; Jan. 17, 2017; Feb. 4, 2020; Sep. 14, 2023>
  (9) Where a motor vehicle management business entity (including an inspector of the performance and condition of a motor vehicle; hereafter in this Article, the same shall apply) has performed the business affairs prescribed in paragraphs (1), (5), (6), and (8), it shall keep a record thereof and manage and retain such record, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on May 24, 2011; Mar. 23, 2013; Jan. 28, 2016; Nov. 15, 2022>
  (10) Where a motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. or a parts manufacturer, etc. notifies a motor vehicle owner of any facts on defective motor vehicles or motor vehicle parts and his or her plan for corrective measures pursuant to paragraph (1), he or she shall have a performance testing agent make the notification on his or her behalf. In such cases, the motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. or the parts manufacturer, etc. shall bear the costs actually incurred in making such notification. <Added on Jan. 17, 2017; Feb. 4, 2020>
  (11) Where a performance testing agent vicariously notifies to a motor vehicle owner pursuant to paragraph (10), the agent may request the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport to provide him or her with information on the motor vehicle owner. <Added on Jan. 17, 2017; Feb. 4, 2020>
  (12) If the progress of corrective measures or economic compensation reported on by a motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. or a parts manufacturer, etc. pursuant to paragraph (8) fails to meet the standards prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may order the motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. or the parts manufacturer, etc. to disclose again to the public the defects and the plan for corrective measures, etc. therefor under paragraph (1), as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Added on Feb. 4, 2020>
  (13) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may request the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment to provide him or her with materials about an inspection for confirmation of defects under Article 51(1) of the Clean Air Conservation Act, a plan for the correction of defects under Article 51(5) of that Act, and materials about the current status of correction of defective parts and the current status of defective parts, etc. under Article 53(1) of that Act, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. In such cases, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall provide materials as requested, without good cause. <Added on Feb. 4, 2020; Oct. 1, 2025>
  (14) Where any of the following performance of an electric vehicle is deteriorated after corrective measures under the main clause of paragraph (1) have been taken against the electric vehicle, the motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. or the parts manufacturer, etc. shall take corrective measures again pursuant to paragraph (1) or make economic compensation in lieu of such corrective measures: <Added on Sep. 14, 2023>
  1. Energy consumption efficiency;
  2. Driving distance after one charge.
  (15) The safety standards for motor vehicles or the safety standards for parts shall apply mutatis mutandis to the standards for deterioration of performance under paragraph (14). <Added on Sep. 14, 2023>
  (16) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may conduct an investigation to verify whether the major parts of a core device, etc., the safety certification of which has been granted under Article 30-7, have any defect. In such cases, paragraphs (4) through (7) shall apply mutatis mutandis to such investigation. <Added on Aug. 16, 2023; Sep. 14, 2023>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]
  [Title Amended on Jan. 17, 2017]

Article 31-2 (Compensation for motor vehicle owners who made self-correction) (1) Any motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. or parts manufacturer, etc. shall compensate for the expenses of correction to any of the following persons: <Amended on May 24, 2011; Jan. 17, 2017>
  1. Any motor vehicle owner who has corrected a defect after the date earlier between the date of one year before the date the motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. or the parts manufacturer, etc. discloses the defect pursuant to the main clauses of Article 31(1) and (3) and the date of commencing the investigation pursuant to Article 30-3(2) or Article 31(4) (including a person who was the previous motor vehicle owner and has corrected the defect during the period of ownership);
  2. Any motor vehicle owner who has corrected a defect after the motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. or the parts manufacturer, etc. disclosed the defect pursuant to the main clauses of Article 31(1) or (3).
  (2) Matters regarding the standards for calculating the compensation prescribed in paragraph (1), the deadline for payment, thereof the procedures for applying for compensation payment, and other matters necessary for the compensation payment shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]

Article 31-3 (Investigation into motor vehicle accidents) (1) Any performance testing agent who suspects that a motor vehicle accident prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, such as fire, was caused by a defect under Article 31(1) may conduct an investigation to find the cause of the accident (hereinafter referred to as "accident investigation"). In such cases, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall provide facilities and equipment necessary for the accident investigation and subsidies for expenses associated with the investigation.
  (2) If necessary for an accident investigation, a performance testing agent may make inquiries to the owner, operator, custodian, and other relevant persons and may request them to submit materials and preserve, lend, or purchase the relevant motor vehicles or parts.
  (3) When a person agrees to preserve, lend, or purchase motor vehicles or parts in compliance with a request under paragraph (2), the performance testing agent shall pay a fair price to such person in accordance with the guidelines prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.
  (4) In order to conduct an accident investigation in a quick and efficient manner, a performance testing agent may request such cooperation as necessary for the accident investigation including the entry to the scene of accident and the preservation of motor vehicles or parts, to the heads of relevant agencies, including the police and the fire authority. In such cases, the heads of relevant agencies shall comply with such request, without good cause.
  (5) A performance testing agent who conducts an accident investigation may request local governments, the police, the fire authority, insurance companies and premium rate calculation institutions under the Insurance Business Act, other relevant institutions, etc. to provide materials prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. The persons so requested shall comply therewith, without good cause.
  [This Article Added on Feb. 4, 2020]

Article 31-4 (Pre-sale correction of defects) (1) Where any motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. or parts manufacturer, etc. shall disclose the defects and take corrective measures accordingly pursuant to the main clause of Article 31(1), such corrective measures shall be taken before the defective motor vehicles or motor vehicle parts are sold.
  (2) Where any motor vehicle manufacturer, seller, etc. or parts manufacturer, etc. sells any motor vehicles or motor vehicle parts for which the corrective measures referred to in paragraph (1) have been taken, he or she shall notify the buyers of such fact.
  [This Article Added on Apr. 13, 2021]

Article 32 (Cross certification between countries on parts) (1) Where a motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. or a parts manufacturer, etc. requests certification of parts or devices used on motor vehicles for the reciprocal certification between countries, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may grant certification after performing tests on safety and performance of such parts or devices (hereinafter referred to as "performance test"). <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (2) Where a part or device of a motor vehicle certified under paragraph (1) fails to satisfy the standards prescribed by a cross certification agreement between countries or falls short of the performance level reached at the time of certification, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall revoke the certification. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (3) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may allow a person designated by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport and satisfying the designation criteria determined by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport to conduct performance tests, as proxy. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (4) Where a performance testing agent has conducted a performance test, he or she shall prepare a report on the evaluation results and submit it to the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (5) Where otherwise provided in the cross certification agreement between the States on the certification procedures, etc. in connection with the certification of parts or devices to be used in motor vehicles, it shall be governed by the provisions of the treaty, notwithstanding the provisions of paragraphs (1) though (4).
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]

Article 32-2 (Ex post facto management of self-certified motor vehicles) (1) Where any motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. has sold a motor vehicle after self-certification (including cases where safety certification under Article 30-7 and safety tests under Article 35-15 have been completed), he or she shall secure necessary facilities and skilled technicians and take the following measures (hereafter in this Article referred to as "ex post facto management"), as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport: <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Jul. 16, 2013; Jan. 6, 2015; Jan. 17, 2017; Oct. 24, 2017; Apr. 13, 2021; Aug. 16, 2023; Feb. 27, 2026>
  1. Gratuitously repairing any defect that occurs during the period or traveling distance determined by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport;
  2. Supplying parts necessary for the maintenance of motor vehicles until the period determined by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport;
  3. Providing the motor vehicle maintenance business entity registered pursuant to Article 53 with the technical guidelines and education necessary for the checkup, maintenance, and testing of motor vehicles; in such cases, the target trainees and methods of providing technical guidelines and education, types and methods of providing equipment or materials for maintenance and other matters necessary therefor shall be determined by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport;
  3-2. Gratuitously providing the Korea Transportation Safety Authority established under the Korea Transportation Safety Authority Act (hereinafter referred to as the "Korea Transportation Safety Authority") with a maintenance manual, a material for manufacturing a diagnostic apparatus to be used for detecting malfunctioning motor vehicles and other materials necessary for the motor vehicle inspection conducted under Article 43, the comprehensive motor vehicle inspection conducted under Article 43-2, and the inspection of compliance with motor vehicle safety standards under Article 73-2; in such cases, types of the materials to be gratuitously provided shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport;
  4. Publishing the price data of motor vehicle parts on its official website; in such cases, matters necessary for the publication of price data of motor vehicle parts, such as objects of publication, shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.
  (2) Any motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. may have the person who has filed for registration of motor vehicle maintenance business among motor vehicle management business under Article 53, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, perform the gratuitous repair under paragraph (1)1 on his or her behalf. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (3) Where a motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. performs gratuitous repairs pursuant to paragraph (1)1, he or she shall not refuse repairs on the ground that he or she uses quality-certified parts and tuning parts certified pursuant to Article 34-3; provided, this shall not apply where the motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. proves that the use of replacement parts and tuning parts is the cause of the breakdown. <Added on Dec. 29, 2015; Jun. 12, 2018; Aug. 16, 2023; Feb. 20, 2024>
  (4) Where a motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. conducts a gratuitous repair of a motor vehicle pursuant to paragraph (1)1, due to any of the causes specified by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, such as a defect stemming from the manufacturing process, the motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. shall inform the motor vehicle owner of the details of such defect and a plan for gratuitous repair by mail, text messages via mobile phones, or other means of communications, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, so that the owner of the motor vehicle can become aware of the details of the defect and the plan for gratuitous repair. <Added on Dec. 26, 2017; Oct. 20, 2020>
  (5) Where a motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. fails to perform the duty of ex post facto management (including where a person who performs the gratuitous repair prescribed in paragraph (2) as agent fails to perform the duty of the gratuitous repair), the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may order the motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. to perform such duty. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Dec. 29, 2015; Dec. 26, 2017>
  (6) Where necessary for protecting consumers in accordance with deliberation and resolution by the Deliberation Committee for Motor Vehicle Safety and Defects established under Article 47-7 (hereinafter referred to as the "Deliberation Committee for Motor Vehicles Safety and Defects") regarding matters related to the correction, etc. of manufacturing defects, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may recommend a motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. to take necessary measures, such as gratuitous repair. <Added on Jun. 10, 2022>
  [This Article Added on Feb. 6, 2009]

Article 33 (Provision of data on motor vehicles or motor vehicle parts) (1) Where a motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. or parts manufacturer, etc. sells motor vehicles or motor vehicle parts, he or she shall provide purchasers with the data on the model and use, etc. of said motor vehicles or motor vehicle parts, as determined by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (2) A motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. or parts manufacturer, etc. shall record and preserve the data on the details of purchasers, etc. which are required for the investigation conducted under Article 30-3(2) or the correction of defects prescribed in the main clauses of Article 31(1) and (3) and in paragraph (4) of the same Article, as determined by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on May 24, 2011; Mar. 23, 2013>
  (3) A motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. or parts manufacturer, etc. shall submit the following data to the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, as determined by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport: <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Jul. 16, 2013; Jan. 17, 2017; Jun. 9, 2020>
  1. Details of correction of manufacturing defects of motor vehicles or motor vehicle parts exported;
  2. Details of correction of manufacturing defects which has been made abroad, in the same type of motor vehicles or motor vehicle parts imported;
  3. Details of gratuitous checkups and repairs by the manufacturer, which have been implemented for a motor vehicle owner;
  4. Technical information data exchanged with a motor vehicle maintenance business entity who has made a registration pursuant to Article 53 for the purposes of exchange or gratuitous maintenance, etc. in connection with defective or incomplete motor vehicles or motor vehicle parts;
  5. Technical analysis data regarding the motor vehicle fire and accident investigated internally or upon request of a third party.
  (4) A performance testing agent may request a motor vehicle manufacturer, etc., a parts manufacturer, etc., or a manufacturer of the major parts of a core device, etc. to submit materials necessary for the following investigations; and the motor vehicle manufacturer, etc., the parts manufacturer, etc., or the manufacturer of the major parts of a core device, etc. so requested shall submit relevant materials to the performance testing agent, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport: <Added on Feb. 4, 2020; Aug. 16, 2023;
  1. An investigation under Article 30-3(2);
  2. An investigation under Article 31(4);
  3. An investigation under Article 31(9);
  3-2. An investigation under Article 31(16);
  4. An accident investigation under Article 31-3(1).
  (5) Where a motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. sells an incomplete motor vehicle, he or she shall provide another motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. who purchases the incomplete motor vehicle with the information, such as whether the incomplete motor vehicle satisfies its safety standards. <Added on Dec. 29, 2015; Feb. 4, 2020>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]

Article 33-2 (Evaluation of safety of motor vehicles) (1) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall evaluate the safety of the motor vehicles sold by motor vehicle manufacturers, etc. as determined by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, in order to provide consumers with data on the level of safety of motor vehicles and to encourage manufacturing of motor vehicles with higher level of safety, and publicize the results thereof. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (2) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may require a performance testing agent to perform evaluations under paragraph (1). In such cases, the Minister shall subsidize the expenses associated with the facilities, equipment, and tests for such evaluation. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]

Article 33-3 (Management of indoor air quality of newly manufactured motor vehicles) (1) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may determine and publicly notify the guidelines for indoor air quality management of newly manufactured motor vehicles, etc. sold by motor vehicle manufacturers or sellers, etc., and other necessary matters. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (2) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may investigate and publicly announce the indoor air quality of newly manufactured motor vehicles under paragraph (1) and provide necessary recommendations for the management of the motor vehicles to the motor vehicle manufacturers or sellers, etc. based upon the findings of the investigation. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (3) If deemed necessary for the management of the indoor air quality of newly manufactured motor vehicles, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may require motor vehicle manufacturers or sellers, etc. to submit necessary reports or data. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  [This Article Added on Dec. 18, 2012]

Article 33-4 (Establishment and operation of motor vehicle defects information system) In order to collect, analyze, manage, and provide data under Article 33(3) and (4), the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may establish and operate a motor vehicle defects information system, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.
  [This Article Added on Feb. 4, 2020]

Article 34 (Tuning of motor vehicles) (1) Where the owner of a motor vehicle intends to make tuning with respect to the items as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, the owner of the relevant motor vehicle shall obtain approval from the head of a Si/Gun/Gu.
  (2) A person who has obtained approval for tuning pursuant to paragraph (1) shall receive the tuning services from a motor vehicle maintenance business entity or from the motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. In such cases, the scope of tuning services to be conducted by the motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Added on Aug. 11, 2015>
  (3) Notwithstanding paragraph (1), if a motor vehicle owner intends to temporarily tune (hereinafter referred to as "temporary tuning") a motor vehicle used for campaign speeches or interviews at public places pursuant to Article 79(3) of the Public Official Election Act as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, the owner may obtain approval for temporary tuning from the head of a Si/Gun/Gu. In such cases, it shall be deemed that the automobile has undergone a tuning inspection pursuant to Article 43(1)3. <Amended on Jan. 16, 2024>
  (4) A person who approves tuning in accordance with paragraph (1) (including a person entrusted with authority for approval in accordance with Article 77(8)) has verified the safety of devices prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, such as pedal misapplication prevention devices, the tuning using such devices shall be deemed to have been approved under paragraph (1) and the tuning inspection under Article 43(1)3. <Added on Jun. 16, 2026>
  (5) Matters regarding the standards and procedures for approval for items subject to approval under paragraph (1) and the standards, procedures, and period of validity for approval for temporary tuning under paragraph (3), and the standards and procedures for safety verification under paragraph (4) shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Added on Jan. 16, 2024; Jun. 16, 2026>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Jan. 7, 2014]

Article 34-2 (Ensuring safety of tuned motor vehicles) (1) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may enforce each of the following in order to ensure the safety of tuned motor vehicles: <Amended on Apr. 7, 2020>
  1. Investigation into, and research and development of, equipment in order to ensure safety of the tuned motor vehicles;
  1-2. Development of human resources with professional techniques or skills for tuning motor vehicles (hereinafter referred to as "professional human resources for tuning motor vehicles") and development and distribution of tuning-related training programs;
  2. Deleted; <Aug. 16, 2023>
  3. Other matters deemed necessary by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.
  (2) Deleted. <Aug. 16, 2023>
  (3) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall, when entrusting the affairs under paragraph (1)1 and 1-2 to the Korea Transportation Safety Authority in accordance with Article 77(6), make contributions or provide support for expenses necessary to perform the affairs within the budget. <Added on Jun. 16, 2026>
  (4) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may, within the budget, subsidize part or all of the costs incurred by a motor vehicle owner for any type of tuning under Article 34(1) deemed necessary to secure the safety of the motor vehicle, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Added on Jun. 16, 2026>
  [This Article Added on Jan. 7, 2014]

Article 34-3 (Certification of motor vehicle tuning parts) (1) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may grant certification (hereinafter referred to as "certification of tuning parts") of the performance and quality of parts for tuning motor vehicles (hereinafter referred to as "tuning parts").
  (2) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may designate an institution, corporation, or organization with expertise in tuning as a certification agency (hereinafter referred to as "agency certifying tuning parts") to have it certify tuning parts.
  (3) A manufacturer, etc. of tuning parts may indicate on the turning parts the fact that he or she obtains the certification of the tuning parts from an agency certifying tuning parts under paragraph (2).
  (4) Matters regarding the standards and methods for, and indication of, certification of tuning parts, and the procedures and standards for designation of agencies certifying tuning parts under paragraphs (1) through (3), and other matters shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.
  [This Article Added on Aug. 16, 2023]

Article 34-4 (Revocation of designation of agencies certifying tuning parts) (1) Where an agency certifying tuning parts falls under any of the following cases, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may revoke the designation of such agency or order it to suspend its business operations for a specified period not exceeding 6 months; provided, in cases falling under subparagraph 1 or 2, the designation shall be revoked:
  1. Where it certifies turning parts by fraud or other improper means;
  2. Where it obtains designation as an agency certifying tuning parts by fraud or other improper means;
  3. Where it certifies tuning parts not in conformity with the standards for certification of tuning parts prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport under Article 34-3(4);
  4. Where it ceases to meet the standards for designation of agencies certifying tuning parts under Article 34-3(4);
  5. Where it fails to file a report under Article 72(1) or files a false report;
  6. Where it refuses, obstructs, or evades an inspection under Article 72(2), fails to respond to an inquiry, or gives a false answer;
  7. Where it fails to observe the matters prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport in relation to the certification of tuning parts.
  (2) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport revokes the designation of an agency certifying tuning parts or orders such agency to suspend its business operations pursuant to paragraph (1), he or she may allow a performance testing agent to conduct business affairs regarding the certification of tuning parts on behalf of the agency.
  (3) Matters necessary for the detailed standards, procedures, etc. for the administrative dispositions specified in paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.
  [This Article Added on Aug. 16, 2023]

Article 34-5 (Software update by motor vehicle manufacturers) (1) Where a motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. performs software update (hereinafter referred to as "updating") (including providing software that performs the update to motor vehicle users, motor vehicle maintenance business entities, etc.; hereinafter the same shall apply), the following matters shall be observed:
  1. To ensure normal operation of all devices and functions of a motor vehicle even after the update is made;
  2. To ensure that the structure and devices related to the relevant update of a motor vehicle conform to the safety standards for motor vehicles even after the update has been made;
  3. To ensure that updates are conducted safely and protected from cyberattacks and threats on motor vehicles;
  4. To provide information on the relevant update to motor vehicle users before and after the update is conducted;
  5. To record and keep the details and history of updated information, and prevent any damage, loss, forgery, or alteration of the relevant information;
  6. Other matters as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport as necessary for safe and smooth update.
  (2) When a motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. intends to make an update related to the safe operation of motor vehicles prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, the manufacturer, etc. shall submit in advance to the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport data attesting that the details, methods, etc. thereof are in compliance with the subparagraphs of paragraph (1).
  (3) Where a motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. who has performed self-certification of motor vehicles pursuant to Article 30(3) intends to submit data under paragraph (2) to the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, he or she shall obtain verification of matters prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport from a performance testing agent.
  (4) A motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. shall submit to the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport the records of updates and data on the details thereof, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.
  (5) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may manage the data under paragraph (2) and (4) through the electronic data processing system under Article 69.
  (6) Paragraphs (1) through (5) shall not apply to the following motor vehicles.
  1. A motor vehicle operated within the period of permission after obtaining permission to temporarily operate the motor vehicle under Article 27(1);
  2. A motor vehicle for which special cases under Article 70 are recognized;
  3. Motor vehicles prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, which are deemed to require separate management due to the purpose or characteristics of operation.
  (7) Data to be submitted pursuant to paragraphs (2) and (4), methods of submission, etc. shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.
  [This Article Added on Feb. 13, 2024]

Article 34-6 (Investigation of appropriateness of updates) (1) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport deems it necessary to ensure the safety of updates, he or she may require a performance testing agent to investigate the appropriateness of the details and methods of updates and the actual status of management. In such cases, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall contribute or subsidize expenses incurred in conducting investigations.
  (2) Where a performance testing agent conducts an investigation under paragraph (1), he or she may inspect facilities, equipment, automobiles, books, documents, or other things related to the update of the person subject to the investigation, or may ask questions to relevant persons.
  (3) Article 72(3) and (4) shall apply mutatis mutandis to the procedures, etc. for investigations under paragraph (1). In such cases, "inspection" shall be deemed as "investigation", and "public official" as "performance test agent", respectively.
  (4) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport deems that an update has not been performed properly or is likely to be the case as a result of an investigation conducted under paragraph (1), etc., he or she may issue a corrective order to a person who has performed the update or intends to do so, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.
  (5) A person in receipt of a corrective order under paragraph (4) shall report a plan for corrective measures and the progress thereof to the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.
  [This Article Added on Feb. 13, 2024]

Article 34-7 (Support for operation and management of connected motor vehicles) For the safe and smooth operation and management of connected motor vehicles, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may allow motor vehicle manufacturers, etc. and persons engaged in business related to motor vehicles prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport to use the following facilities, equipment, services, etc.
  1. A cooperative autonomous driving system established pursuant to Article 21 of the Act on Promotion of and Support for Commercialization of Autonomous Driving Vehicles;
  2. A cooperative autonomous driving system established pursuant to Article 22 of the Act on Promotion of and Support for Commercialization of Autonomous Driving Vehicles;
  3. A cooperative driving certification management center established and operated pursuant to Article 27 of the Act on Promotion and Support of Commercialization of Autonomous Vehicles and autonomous cooperative driving certification service, etc.;
  4. Other facilities, equipment, services, etc. prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport as necessary for the safe and smooth operation and management of connected motor vehicles.
  [This Article Added on Feb. 13, 2024]

Article 35 (Prohibition of unauthorized dismantlement or manipulation of motor vehicles) (1) Except in any of the following cases, no person shall dismantle or manipulate a device prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport from a motor vehicle [limited to operating a device that limits the maximum speed of a motor vehicle or a steering device that assists the driver (a device that is added to the steering device that is primarily responsible for determining the direction of travel and assists the driver in steering; hereinafter the same shall apply)]. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Jan. 7, 2014; Dec. 26, 2017; Jan. 9, 2024; Feb. 13, 2024>
  1. Where he or she intends to conduct the checkup or maintenance of the motor vehicle or to make tuning;
  2. Where he or she intends to conduct automobile scrapping;
  3. Where it is to be used for educational and research purposes, or falls within the grounds prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.
  (2) No one shall arbitrarily alter, install, add, or delete software that may affect the safe operation of motor vehicles. <Added on Feb. 13, 2024>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]
  [Title amended on Dec. 26, 2017; Feb. 13, 2024]

CHAPTER III-2 SPECIAL CASES CONCERNING LOW SPEED ELECTRIC MOTOR VEHICLES

Article 35-2 (Safety standards for low speed electric motor vehicles) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may determine safety standards for motor vehicles different from those prescribed in Article 29(1) for a motor vehicle which does not exceed the maximum speed and gross weight determined by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport (hereinafter referred to as "low speed electric motor vehicle"), from among the electric motor vehicles which use electric energy as their power source. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  [This Article Added on Dec. 29, 2009]

Article 35-3 (Designation of areas for operation of low speed electric motor vehicles) (1) At his or her discretion or at the request of a person who intends to operate a low speed electric motor vehicle, the head of a Si/Gun/Gu may designate an area for operation of a low speed electric motor vehicle (hereinafter referred to as "operation area"), or may modify or revoke such designation from among the roads where permissible maximum speed is 60 kilometers per hour after consultation with the relevant chief of police in light of traffic safety and flow; provided,, considering the progressive direction of the low speed electric motor vehicle, where passage of the low speed electric motor vehicle is deemed impossible in certain section of an area without driving through a road where permissible maximum speed is more than 60 kilometers per hour, the head of a Si/Gun/Gu may designate, among those roads where permissible maximum speed is 80 kilometers per hour, the shortest road necessary for passing the aforementioned section as the operation area thereof. <Amended on Aug. 11, 2015>
  (2) A low speed electric motor vehicle shall not be operated on a road other than an operation area; provided, where the checkup, testing, etc. of a low speed electric motor vehicle is prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, such motor vehicle may be operated with permission from the head of a Si/Gun/Gu. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (3) Matters necessary for the designation of an operation area and application for operation of a low speed electric motor vehicle shall be determined by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  [This Article Added on Dec. 29, 2009]

Article 35-4 (Public notice of operation areas) (1) The head of a Si/Gun/Gu who designates an operation area, or modifies or revokes such designation (hereinafter referred to as "designating authority") shall publicly notify the following matters, and the details thereof shall be made available for the public's perusal in advance. The application of subparagraph 1 shall be limited to revocation of an operation area: <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Jun. 9, 2020>
  1. Location of an operation area and section of the road thereof;
  2. Matters regarding traffic safety, such as installation of a safety sign;
  3. Other matters prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.
  (2) Where the designating authority publicly notifies an operation area, it shall notify the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport of the details thereof. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (3) The designating authority may install the following facilities for a motor vehicle operator to easily recognize an operation area: <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  1. A road sign showing an operation area or an area prohibited from operation;
  2. Other facilities prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport for safe operation.
  (4) Where the designating authority intends to revoke designation of an operation area, public notice thereof shall be provided 90 days before the date of revocation of such designation.
  (5) Procedures for publicly notifying or disclosing an operation area for the public's perusal shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  [This Article Added on Dec. 29, 2009]

CHAPTER III-3 SAFETY MANAGEMENT FOR PRESSURE-RESISTANT CONTAINERS AND END-OF-LIFE BATTERIES

Article 35-5 (Safety standards for pressure-resistant containers) (1) A pressure-resistant container shall satisfy the performance requirement and standards necessary for the safe operation of motor vehicles (hereinafter referred to as "safety standards for pressure-resistant containers").
  (2) The safety standards for pressure-resistant containers shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  [This Article Added on May 24, 2011]

Article 35-6 (Inspections of pressure-resistant containers) (1) A person who manufactures, repairs, or imports pressure-resistant containers (hereinafter referred to as "manufacturer, etc. of pressure-resistant containers") shall undergo a test conducted by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport (hereinafter referred to as "test for pressure-resistant containers") before sale and use of the pressure-resistant containers; provided, the test for pressure-resistant containers may be, either partially or fully, omitted as to the pressure-resistant containers determined by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (2) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall destroy the pressure-resistant containers that have failed the test for pressure-resistant containers, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (3) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall carve or mark necessary matters on the pressure-resistant containers that have passed the test for pressure-resistant containers, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (4) Where a motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. intends to perform self-certification of motor vehicles pursuant to Article 30(1), he or she shall use pressure-resistant containers that have passed the test for pressure-resistant containers.
  (5) No person shall display the pressure-resistant containers subject to the test for pressure-resistant containers pursuant to the main clause of paragraph (1), which have failed the test, for the purpose of transferring, renting, using, or selling them.
  (6) Any person who has manufactured, repaired, or imported the pressure-resistant containers for which the test for pressure-resistant containers is fully omitted pursuant to the proviso of paragraph (1) shall inform the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport thereof, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (7) Types of the test for pressure-resistant containers and other necessary matters shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  [This Article Added on May 24, 2011]

Article 35-7 (Installation tests of pressure-resistant containers) (1) A motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. who intends to install a pressure-resistant container which has passed the test for pressure-resistant container under Article 35-6(1) shall, before performing the self-certification of motor vehicles, undergo the test for installation safety conducted by the performance testing agent (hereinafter referred to as "installation test for pressure-resistant container") as to the pressure-resistant containers and the gas facilities necessary for the connection thereof; provided, in the case of motor vehicles fueled by liquefied petroleum gas, the installation test for pressure-resistant container may be substituted by self-certification of motor vehicle after the pressure-resistant container is installed in the motor vehicle which has passed the test for pressure-resistant container.
  (2) After the performance testing agent conducts the installation test for pressure-resistant container and finds that the criteria for the installation test for pressure-resistant container are met, he or she shall issue an inspection certificate of the installation of pressure-resistant container, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (3) The standards, methods, procedures and other matters necessary for the installation test for pressure-resistant container shall be determined by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  [This Article Added on May 24, 2011]

Article 35-8 (Re-examination of pressure-resistant containers) (1) An owner of a motor vehicle in which a pressure-resistant container is installed shall undergo the installation test for pressure-resistant container conducted by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport after completing the tuning for the installation of a pressure-resistant container pursuant to Article 34 and 43(1)3 or pursuant to the main clause of Article 35-7(1), or shall undergo the test for the pressure-resistant container conducted by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport according to the following classification (hereinafter referred to as "re-examination of pressure-resistant container") under the direction of a person who tests a motor vehicle as proxy pursuant to Article 44(1) (hereinafter referred to as "motor vehicle testing agent") after performing the self-certification of motor vehicle pursuant to the proviso of Article 35-7(1); provided, in the case of motor vehicles fueled by liquefied petroleum gas, the re-examination of pressure-resistant container may be substituted by the regular inspection under Article 43(1)2 or the comprehensive inspection under Article 43-2(1): <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Jan. 7, 2014; Jun. 9, 2020>
  1. Regular inspection of pressure-resistant container: A test regularly conducted every time the period determined by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport elapses;
  2. On-demand inspection of pressure-resistant container: A test conducted in such cases as occurrence of damage, occurrence of damage to carving or mark made after the test for pressure-resistant containers, a change to the types of high-pressure gas to be charged, or occurrence of other causes as determined by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.
  (2) A motor vehicle testing agent shall destroy the pressure-resistant containers which have failed the re-examination of pressure-resistant container, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (3) A motor vehicle testing agent shall carve or mark necessary matters on the pressure-resistant containers which have passed the re-examination of pressure-resistant container as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (4) No person shall display the motor vehicles subject to the re-examination of pressure-resistant container under paragraph (1) which have failed the re-examination of pressure-resistant container, for the purpose of transferring, renting, using, or selling the motor vehicles.
  (5) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may subsidize expenses incurred in the installation of facilities necessary for the re-examination of pressure-resistant containers, purchase of equipment, etc. by a motor vehicle testing agent, as prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (6) The standards, period, procedures, and other matters necessary for the re-examination of pressure-resistant containers shall be determined by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  [This Article Added on May 24, 2011]

Article 35-9 (Suspension of manufacture or sale of pressure-resistant containers) (1) Where a manufacturer, etc. of pressure-resistant containers falls under any of the following cases, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may order suspension of the manufacture, import, or sale of the relevant pressure-resistant containers; provided, in cases falling under subparagraph 1, the manufacture, import, or sale of the pressure-resistant containers shall be suspended: <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  1. Where he or she has undergone the test for pressure-resistant container by fraud or other improper means;
  2. Where he or she has failed to comply with the order under Article 35-10(2) and (3).
  (2) In order to ascertain as to whether a manufacturer, etc. of pressure resistant containers falls under any subparagraph of paragraph (1), the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may require a performance testing agent to conduct an investigation. In such cases, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall subsidize the expenses associated with such investigation. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  [This Article Added on May 24, 2011]

Article 35-10 (Measures to prevent pressure-resistant containers from safety risks) (1) If deemed necessary for the safety management of pressure-resistant containers, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may require a performance testing agent to collect the pressure-resistant containers and inspect them. In such cases, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall subsidize the expenses associated with conducting the inspection by the performance testing agent. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (2) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport deems that the findings of an inspection under paragraph (1) reveal any significant defect in the pressure-resistant containers, such as gas-leakage, as determined by Presidential Decree, he or she may order the manufacturer, etc. of the pressure-resistant containers to recall or exchange them or to refund the prices paid therefor, or publish such fact (hereinafter referred to as "recall, etc."). <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (3) Notwithstanding paragraph (2), where it is discovered that pressure-resistant containers have any significant and obvious defect likely to risk public safety as determined by Presidential Decree, such as an explosion accident, and there is an urgent need for such measures as urgent recall, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may order the manufacturer, etc. of the pressure-resistant containers to make recall, etc. without conducting an inspection under paragraph (1). <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (4) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport deems that the safety risk has occurred or is likely to occur from pressure-resistant containers under paragraph (2) or (3), he or she may order suspension of or restriction on the use of motor vehicles equipped with the relevant pressure-resistant containers, or may order the disuse of the high pressure gas left inside the pressure-resistant containers. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (5) Where an order is issued under paragraph (4) to maintain public safety with no clear fault on the part of a person who holds ownership of or rights to a motor vehicle, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall provide fair compensation for the loss, as prescribed by Presidential Decree; provided, this shall not apply to natural disasters, war or other force majeure events. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (6) The method for collecting pressure-resistant containers, the procedures and methods for recall, etc., the procedures for suspension of or restriction on the use of motor vehicles, and other matters pursuant to paragraphs (1) through (4) shall be determined by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  [This Article Added on May 24, 2011]

Article 35-11 (Provision of data on pressure-resistant containers) (1) A manufacturer, etc. of pressure-resistant containers shall provide the purchaser with the data on the type, usage, etc. of pressure-resistant containers, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport when selling pressure-resistant containers. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (2) A manufacturer, etc. of pressure-resistant containers shall record and keep the data on the details of the purchaser, etc. which are required for the inspection conducted under Article 35-9(2) or for the order issued under Article 35-10(2) through (4), as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (3) A manufacturer, etc. of pressure-resistant containers shall submit to the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport the data on the correction of manufacturing defects of pressure-resistant containers exported, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, and on gratuitous checkup by the manufacturer, etc. and the details, etc. of repairs, which have been notified to the owners and performed accordingly. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  [This Article Added on May 24, 2011]

Article 35-12 (Relationship to other statutes) With respect to the matters regarding registration, safety management, inspection, re-examination, purchase of insurance, etc. of pressure-resistant containers which are not provided for in this Act, the provisions of the High-Pressure Gas Safety Control Act, the Safety Control and Business of Liquefied Petroleum Gas Act, and the Urban Gas Business Act shall apply; provided, the following provisions shall not apply: <Amended on Jan. 28, 2015>
  1. Articles 17, 18, 20, 24, 40, 42 and 43 of the High-Pressure Gas Safety Control Act;
  2. Articles 44 and 73 of the Safety Control and Business of Liquefied Petroleum Gas Act;
  3. Articles 15 and 50 of the Urban Gas Business Act.
  [This Article Added on May 24, 2011]

Article 35-13 (Performance evaluation of end-of-life batteries) (1) The owner of an electric vehicle, etc. (where a motor vehicle scrapping and recycling business entity receives a request for scrapping from the owner of an electric vehicle, etc., in accordance with Article 13(1)1, referring to that motor vehicle scrapping and recycling business entity) shall, when removing the end-of-life battery from the electric vehicle, etc., undergo an evaluation of the performance and safety of the end-of-life battery (hereinafter referred to as “performance evaluation”) conducted by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport before such removal; provided, this shall not apply when the owner of an electric vehicle returns the battery of the relevant motor vehicle to the head of a local government in accordance with Article 8 of the Addenda to the Clean Air Conservation Act (Act No. 17797).
  (2) Notwithstanding the main clause of paragraph (1), in any of the following cases, the person prescribed in the relevant subparagraph shall undergo the performance evaluation; in such cases, he or she may undergo the performance evaluation after removing the end-of-life battery from the electric vehicle, etc.:  
  1. Where a traction battery is collected for remedy of manufacturing defects under Article 31(1) or (3): A motor vehicle manufacturer, etc.;
  2. Where a traction battery is collected for gratuitous repair under Article 32-2(1)1: A motor vehicle manufacturer, etc.;
  3. Where the relevant traction battery is no longer used in an electric vehicle, etc., due to a motor vehicle accident, etc.: A motor vehicle maintenance business entity or a motor vehicle scrapping and recycling business entity registered in accordance with Article 53(1); 
  4. Where the relevant traction battery is no longer used in an electric vehicle, etc. because the owner of electric vehicle, etc. has replaced the traction battery: The owner of the electric vehicle, etc.
  (3) When conducting a performance evaluation in accordance with paragraphs (1) and (2), the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall classify end-of-life batteries into the following grades, based on the performance and safety standards established in consultation with the Minister of Trade, Industry and Resources and the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment:
  1. Where it is suitable for use as a traction battery in electric vehicles, etc.: The remanufacturing grade; 
  2. Where it is suitable for use as a battery for storing electric energy or a medium for storing electric energy that has functions similar to those of such battery, other than use as a traction battery of electric vehicles, etc.: The reuse grade; 
  3. Where it cannot be used even as a battery for storing electric energy or a medium for storing electric energy that has functions similar to those of such battery: The recycling grade.
  (4) No person shall conduct any of the following acts:
  1. Forging or altering performance evaluation results;
  2. Using it for a purpose other than the purpose of the grade classified in accordance with paragraph (3) (excluding where a remanufacturing-grade battery is used as a reuse-grade or recycling-grade battery, or a reuse-grade battery is used as a recycling-grade battery).
  (5) Matters necessary for the specific criteria, procedures, etc. for performance evaluations under paragraphs (1) through (3) shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.
  [This Article Added on Feb. 27, 2026]

Article 35-14 (Designation and revocation of performance evaluation agent) (1) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may designate the Korea Transportation Safety Authority as an entity which conducts performance evaluation on the Minister‘s behalf (hereinafter referred to as "performance evaluation agent") and have it conduct performance evaluation and notify the results thereof.
  (2) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may, in any of the following cases, revoke the designation of a performance evaluation agent; or order suspension of all or part of the affairs of the performance evaluation agent for a period not exceeding 6 months:
  1. Where he or she has accepted unjust enrichment in relation to the affairs or engaged in other improper conduct;
  2. Where he or she has failed to meet the standards for facilities, equipment, etc. of the performance evaluation agent under paragraph (3);
  3. Where it is deemed inappropriate for him or her to continue the services due to the bad financial status, etc.;
  4. Where he or she fails to observe the matters prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport in relation to the performance evaluation.
  (3) Matters necessary for the standards for facilities, equipment, etc., the procedure for designation of performance evaluation agents, and other relevant matters shall be prescribed by Decree of Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.
  [This Article Added on Feb. 27, 2026]

Article 35-15 (Registration of remanufactured battery business entities and safety management thereof) (1) A person who intends to manufacture or assemble remanufactured batteries from end-of-life batteries from electric vehicles, etc. (hereinafter referred to as “remanufactured battery business entity”) shall register as a parts manufacturer, etc., in accordance with Article 30-2(2).
  (2) A remanufactured battery business entity shall, before selling or renting remanufactured batteries, conduct a safety verification test (hereinafter referred to as "safety test") to verify compliance with standards prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, and shall provide the results thereof to the person conducting the pre-distribution safety inspection under Article 35-16(1), as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. In such cases, Articles 30(1), 30-2(1), and 30-7(1) shall not apply.
  (3) Where a remanufactured battery is found to have been sold or rented in noncompliance with the standards under the former part of paragraph (2), the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may order the relevant remanufactured battery business entity to take corrective measures.
  (4) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may revoke the registration under paragraph (1) in any of the following cases and may order the remanufactured battery business entity to suspend the manufacture, assembly, import, sale, or rental of remanufactured batteries; provided, in cases falling under subparagraph 1, the Minister shall revoke such registration and order the remanufactured battery business entity to suspend the manufacture, assembly, import, sale, or rental of remanufactured batteries:
  1. Where it has conducted the safety test by fraud or other improper means;
  2. Where it has failed to take the corrective measures under paragraph (3).
  [This Article Added on Feb. 27, 2026]

Article 35-16 (Safety inspection of electric vehicles equipped with remanufactured batteries) (1) Where intending to install remanufactured batteries in electric vehicles, etc. for sale or operation, the following persons shall undergo a safety inspection (hereinafter referred to as “pre-distribution safety inspection”) conducted by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport:
  1. Where intending to sell electric vehicles, etc. equipped with remanufactured batteries: Motor vehicle manufacturers, etc. that intend to sell electric vehicles, etc.;
  2. Where intending to operate electric vehicles, etc. after replacing traction batteries with remanufactured batteries: The owners of electric vehicles, etc.
  (2) No person shall sell or operate electric vehicles, etc. that have failed the pre-distribution safety inspection; provided, this shall not apply where electric vehicles, etc. are operated for the purpose of undergoing a safety inspection under paragraph (1)2.
  (3) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may have a performance testing agent conduct pre-distribution safety inspections on the Minister‘s behalf.
  (4) Any person conducting pre-distribution safety inspections shall verify the results of the safety tests under Article 35-15(2).
  (5) The owner of electric vehicles, etc. installed with remanufactured batteries shall undergo regular safety inspections of remanufactured batteries conducted by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport; provided, where the owner has undergone a regular inspection under Article 43(1)2, he or she shall be deemed to have undergone a regular safety inspection. 
  (6) The standards, methods, and procedures for pre-distribution safety inspection, and other necessary matters shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.
  [This Article Added on Feb. 27, 2026]

Article 35-17 (Handling of end-of-life batteries) (1) The following person who intends to store, transport, etc. end-of-life batteries shall meet the standards regarding facilities, equipment, and other matters prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport:
  1. A motor vehicle scrapping and recycling business entity; 
  2. A manufacturer, etc. of a motor vehicle;
  3. A motor vehicle maintenance business entity registered in accordance with Article 53(1);
  4. A remanufactured battery business entity; 
  5. A trucking transport business entity which has obtained permission in accordance with Article 3 of the Trucking Transport Business Act.
  (2) A person falling under each subparagraph of paragraph (1) shall store, transport, etc. end-of-life batteries in compliance with the handling and management methods prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.
  [This Article Added on Feb. 27, 2026]

Article 35-18 (Establishment and operation of traceability system for end-of-life batteries) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may establish and operate a traceability system, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, to systematically manage history information on the production of traction batteries, performance evaluation results, installation of remanufactured batteries, etc.
  [This Article Added on Feb. 27, 2026]

Article 35-19 (Provision of information on end-of-life batteries) (1) A performance evaluation agent or performance testing agent may request information necessary for performing the following affairs, such as information regarding battery diagnostic systems, performance, and characteristics, from manufacturers, etc. of motor vehicles or remanufactured battery business entities; in such cases, a person who has received such request shall provide the information unless there is good cause not to do so:
  1. Performance evaluation;
  2. Vicarious execution of pre-distribution safety inspection under Article 35-16(3); 
  3. Verification of the results of safety tests under Article 35-16(4) (limited to cases of vicarious execution of pre-distribution safety inspections).
  (2) Necessary matters, such as the type of information to be provided, methods, etc. for providing information under paragraph (1), shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.
  [This Article Added on Feb. 27, 2026]

CHAPTER IV CHECKUP AND MAINTENANCE OF MOTOR VEHICLES

Article 36 (Maintenance of motor vehicles) A user of a motor vehicle who intends to have the motor vehicle repaired shall do so within the scope allowed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Dec. 18, 2012]

Article 36-2 (Regular checkup of trucks) (1) The owner of a motor vehicle used in a trucking transport service defined in subparagraph 3 of Article 2 of the Trucking Transport Business Act, whose maximum load capacity and gross vehicle weight meet the standards prescribed by Decree Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport (hereinafter referred to as “commercial freight or special motor vehicles”), shall undergo a regular checkup if the relevant motor vehicle has exceeded the vehicle age prescribed by Decree Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.
  (2) Where it is deemed that a commercial freight or special motor vehicle does not comply with safety standards or poses a risk to safe operation based on the results of the regular checkup under paragraph (1), the owner of the relevant commercial freight or special motor vehicle shall have the vehicle repaired within the period prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.
  (3) Matters necessary for the items, methods, procedure, validity period, postponement, etc. of regular checkups under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.
  [This Article Added on Dec. 2, 2025]

Article 37 (Orders for checkup and maintenance) (1) The head of a Si/Gun/Gu may issue an order for checkup, maintenance, inspection, or restoration to the original state, to an owner of any of the following motor vehicles, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport; provided, the head of a Si/Gun/Gu shall respectively order restoration to the original state and the provisional checkup under Article 43(1)4 in cases of subparagraph 2; a pre-distribution safety checkup in cases of subparagraph 2-2, a regular checkup under Article 36-2(1) in cases of subparagraph 2-3; a regular checkup under Article 43(1)2 or a comprehensive checkup under Article 43-2 in cases of subparagraph 3; a provisional checkup under Article 43(1)4 in cases of subparagraph 4 or 5: <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Jan. 7, 2014; Jan. 17, 2017; Mar. 28, 2023; Dec. 2, 2025; Feb. 27, 2026>
  1. A motor vehicle deemed to have failed to satisfy the safety standards for motor vehicles or to have difficulty in safe operation;
  2. A motor vehicle which has been tuned without approval prescribed in Article 34;
  2-2. A electric vehicle, etc. that has not undergone a pre-distribution safety inspection in accordance with Article 35-16(1); 
  2-3. A commercial freight or special motor vehicle that has not undergone a regular checkup under Article 36-2(1);
  3. A motor vehicle which has not undergone a regular inspection conducted under Article 43(1)2 or a comprehensive motor vehicle inspection conducted under Article 43-2;
  4. A motor vehicle for business involved in any of the serious traffic accidents prescribed in Article 19(2) of the Passenger Transport Service Act or Article 19(1)11 and 32(1)12 of the Trucking Transport Business Act;
  5. A motor vehicle deemed to have difficulty in safe operation in accordance with the standards prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport due to a natural disaster, fire, or flood.
  (2) Where the head of a Si/Gun/Gu intends to give an order for checkup, maintenance, inspection, or restoration to the original state as prescribed in paragraph (1), he or she shall fix a period therefor, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. In such cases, he or she may simultaneously order suspension of operation of the relevant motor vehicle. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Jan. 17, 2017>
  (3) Where the owner of a motor vehicle falling under paragraph (1)2-3 and 3 fails to comply with a checkup or inspection order under paragraph (1) for at least 1 year, the head of a Si/Gun/Gu shall order the suspension of the operation of the motor vehicle. In such cases, Article 24-2(3) through (5) shall apply mutatis mutandis to matters to be complied with by the head of a Si/Gun/Gu. <Amended on Apr. 13, 2021; Dec. 2, 2025; Feb. 27, 2026>
  (4) Matters necessary for the issuance of an order to suspend operation under the former part of paragraph (3), matters to be complied with under the latter part of that paragraph, etc. shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Apr. 13, 2021>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]

Article 38 Deleted. <Apr. 15, 1999>

Article 39 Deleted. <Apr. 15, 1999>

Article 40 (Precision tests for machines and apparatuses) (1) A person who manufactures and assembles, or imports and sells machines and apparatuses used for the checkup, maintenance, or inspection of a motor vehicle and a person who uses them (hereinafter referred to as "manufacturers, etc. of machines and apparatuses") shall have them undergo a precision test conducted by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. The same shall also apply to any modification of the structure and devices of machines and apparatuses. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (2) Standards and procedures for testing machines and apparatuses subject to a precision test as prescribed in paragraph (1), and other necessary matters, shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]

Article 41 Deleted. <Apr. 15, 1999>

Article 42 Deleted. <Apr. 15, 1999>

CHAPTER V INSPECTIONS OF MOTOR VEHICLES

Article 43 (Inspections of motor vehicles) (1) An owner of a motor vehicle (in cases falling under subparagraph 1, referring to an expectant of a new registration) shall have his or her motor vehicle inspected by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, according to the following classification: <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Jan. 7, 2014; Aug. 11, 2015>
  1. New inspection: Inspection conducted for a new registration;
  2. Regular inspection: Inspection regularly conducted at a fixed interval after a new registration;
  3. Inspection of tuning: Inspection conducted when a motor vehicle has been tuned under Article 34;
  4. Provisional inspection: Inspection conducted when required by this Act or an order issued under this Act or by an application from an owner of a motor vehicle;
  5. Repair inspection: Inspection conducted on a motor vehicle subject to total loss after repair.
  (2) When the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport performs an inspection of a motor vehicle under paragraph (1) (hereinafter referred to as "motor vehicle inspection"), he or she shall check whether the structure and devices of the relevant motor vehicle meet the inspection standards prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport (hereinafter referred to as "inspection standards for motor vehicles"), and whether the identification number, engine model, etc. are the same as the details of the data recorded in an electronic information processing organization under Article 69, and after the motor vehicle inspection, shall notify the owner of the motor vehicle of the results thereof, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. In such cases, separate inspection standards shall be determined for commercial motor vehicles and non-commercial motor vehicles. <Amended on Dec. 29. 2009; Dec. 18, 2012; Mar. 23, 2013; Apr. 13, 2021>
  (3) Where a motor vehicle has passed an inspection conducted under paragraph (2), the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall take measures according to the following classifications: <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Jan. 7, 2014; Apr. 13, 2021>
  1. New inspection: Issuing a new inspection certificate;
  2. Regular inspection, inspection of tuning, provisional inspection, or repair inspection: Recording inspections on the register.
  (4) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport deems that an owner of a motor vehicle is unable to submit his or her motor vehicle for inspections under paragraph (1)2 through 4 due to a natural disaster or other unavoidable causes or events, he or she may extend the period or postpone the inspection of the motor vehicle. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (5) Where a motor vehicle with the mark of self-certification of motor vehicle under Article 30(4) is newly registered (excluding a motor vehicle which is newly registered after its registration is revoked), it shall be deemed newly inspected under paragraph (1)1.
  (6) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport has performed a regular inspection pursuant to paragraph (1)2 (hereinafter referred to as "regular inspection"), he or she shall record and keep the scene and results of the inspection with an electronic information processing organization established under Article 69 until the period prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (7) No person shall manipulate or alter, or have another person manipulate or alter, any value set in a machine or instrument used for inspections of motor vehicles according to the inspection standards for motor vehicles or any value measured by a machine or instrument. <Added on Dec. 26, 2017>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]

Article 43-2 (Comprehensive motor vehicle inspections) (1) A motor vehicle owner who has registered in the area of exhaust gas precision inspection (hereinafter referred to as "precise inspection") of operating motor vehicles under Article 63(1) of the Clean Air Conservation Act and certain diesel motor vehicle owners under Article 26(1) of the Special Act on the Improvement of Air Quality in Air Control Zones shall undergo a comprehensive motor vehicle inspection (hereinafter referred to as "comprehensive inspection") which combines a regular inspection, a precise inspection of exhaust gas conducted under Article 63(1) of the Clean Air Conservation Act, or an exhaust gas inspection of specific diesel motor vehicle (hereinafter referred to as "specific diesel automobile inspection") under Article 26(2) of the Special Act on the Improvement of Air Quality in Air Control Zones, which is jointly conducted on the following matters by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport and the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment. Where a motor vehicle has undergone a comprehensive inspection, it shall be deemed to have completed a regular inspection, precise inspection, and specific diesel motor vehicle inspection: <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Apr. 2, 2019; Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. Common fields of conducting sensory inspection (inspection for confirming the status of motor vehicles by human sensory organs) and functional inspection to confirm the identity of motor vehicle and operating condition of the devices related to exhaust gas, etc.;
  2. Motor vehicle safety inspection;
  3. Motor vehicle exhaust gas precise inspection.
  (2) Matters regarding the procedures, target, the term of validity, postponement, etc. for comprehensive inspections shall be prescribed by Joint Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport and the Ministry of Climate, Energy and Environment (hereinafter referred to as "Joint Decree"). <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Oct. 1, 2025>
  (3) Article 43(2), (3), (4), (6), and (7); the proviso, with the exception of the subparagraphs, of Article 76; and subparagraph 12 of Article 76 shall apply mutatis mutandis to comprehensive inspection. <Amended on Dec. 26, 2017>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]

Article 43-3 (Research and development relating to motor vehicle inspection) (1) Where it is necessary to research and develop technology and equipment related to motor vehicle inspection or to develop such technology and equipment in order to formulate inspection standards for motor vehicles to which new technology is applied, such as environment-friendly motor vehicles (referring to environment-friendly motor vehicles defined in subparagraph 2 of Article 2 of the Act on Promotion of Development and Distribution of Environment-Friendly Motor Vehicles) and autonomous vehicles, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may entrust a motor vehicle inspection agent prescribed in Article 44 with such research and development or dissemination.
  (2) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport entrusts research and development or dissemination under paragraph (1), he or she may fully or partially subsidize expenses incurred therein within the budget.
  [This Article Added on Oct. 20, 2020]

Article 44 (Designation of motor vehicle inspection agents) (1) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may designate the Korea Transportation Safety Authority as a motor vehicle inspection agent to conduct motor vehicle inspections and notification of the results thereof on his or her behalf. <Amended on Dec. 29, 2009; Mar. 23, 2013; Oct. 24, 2017>
  (2) Standards for facilities, equipment, etc. and the procedures for designation of a motor vehicle inspection agent, and other necessary matters, shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on May 24, 2011; Mar. 23, 2013>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]

Article 44-2 (Designation of motor vehicle comprehensive inspection agents) (1) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may designate the Korea Transportation Safety Authority as a comprehensive inspection agent (hereinafter referred to as "comprehensive inspection agent") to conduct general inspection on his or her behalf (including notification of the results thereof). <Amended on Dec. 29, 2009; Mar. 23, 2013; Oct. 24, 2017>
  (2) Matters necessary for the standards for facilities, equipment and human resources, and the business scope of a comprehensive inspection agent shall be determined by Joint Ministerial Decrees.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]

Article 45 (Designation of designated maintenance business entities) (1) If deemed necessary for efficiently conducting regular inspections, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may designate those who have secured the prescribed facilities and skilled technicians among motor vehicle maintenance business entities as designated maintenance business entities and require them to provide regular inspection services (including notification of the results thereof); provided, in areas subject to a precise inspection under any subparagraph of Article 63(1) of the Clean Air Conservation Act, the Minister may allow a designated maintenance business entity for comprehensive inspection under Article 45-2 to conduct regular inspection without designating a maintenance business entity. <Amended on Dec. 29, 2009; Mar. 23, 2013>
  (2) A motor vehicle maintenance business entity who intends to be designated as a maintenance business entity under paragraph (1) (hereinafter referred to as "designated maintenance business entity") shall file an application for designation with the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, satisfying the standards for the facilities and skilled technicians prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. The same shall also apply to any modification to important matters determined by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, among matters regarding designation; provided, a motor vehicle maintenance business entity who intends to modify any matter other than the important matters determined by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall report thereon to the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Aug. 27, 2019>
  (3) Standards for facilities and skilled technicians, procedures for designation, and the scope of inspection services, etc. for a designated maintenance business entity, and other necessary matters, shall be determined by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (4) The proviso, with the exception of the subparagraphs, of Article 76 and subparagraph 12 of the same Article shall apply mutatis mutandis to a designated maintenance business entity.
  (5) A designated maintenance business entity whose designation as a designated maintenance business entity under Article 45-3(1) has been revoked, or a person who applies for the designation of a designated maintenance business entity from the same place of business shall not be eligible for designation as the designated maintenance business entity unless five years have passed since the revocation of its designation. <Amended on Apr. 13, 2021>
  (6) If a designated maintenance business entity receives an application for regular inspection from a motor vehicle owner, he or she shall check whether the relevant motor vehicle meets the inspection standards for motor vehicles as provided for in Article 43(2).
  (7) A designated maintenance business entity shall not have another person conduct regular inspection under his or her name.
  (8) Where a designated maintenance business entity suspends or closes all or part of his or her business, he or she shall report thereon to the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport; provided, the same shall not apply where he or she has reported suspension or closure of motor vehicle management business in accordance with Article 55(4). <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]

Article 45-2 (Designation of designated maintenance business entities for comprehensive inspections) (1) If deemed necessary for efficiently performing comprehensive inspections, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may designate those who have secured the prescribed facilities and skilled technicians from among motor vehicle maintenance business entities as designated maintenance business entities of motor vehicle comprehensive inspection (hereinafter referred to as "designated maintenance business entity for comprehensive inspection") after consultation with the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment and may require them to conduct comprehensive inspection (including notification of the results thereof). <Amended on Dec. 29, 2009; Mar. 23, 2013; Oct. 1, 2025>
  (2) A motor vehicle maintenance business entity who intends to be designated as a designated maintenance business entity for comprehensive inspection shall file an application for designation with the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, as prescribed by Joint Ministerial Decrees. The same shall also apply to any modification to important matters determined by Joint Ministerial Decrees, among matters regarding designation; provided, a motor vehicle maintenance business entity who intends to modify any matter other than the important matters determined by Joint Ministerial Decrees shall report thereon to the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Added on Aug. 27, 2019>
  (3) Matters regarding the standards for facilities, equipment and human resources of the designated maintenance business entity for comprehensive inspection, the procedures for designation, and the scope of inspection services shall be determined by Joint Ministerial Decrees. <Amended on Aug. 27, 2019>
  (4) Article 45(4) through (8) shall apply mutatis mutandis to a designated maintenance business entity for comprehensive inspection. <Amended on Aug. 27, 2019>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]

Article 45-3 (Revocation of designation) (1) If a motor vehicle inspection agent, a comprehensive inspection agent, a designated maintenance business entity, or a designated maintenance business entity for comprehensive inspection falls under any of the following cases, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may revoke such designation or order the suspension of all or part of the business for a period of up to 6 months; provided, where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport orders revocation of the designation as a comprehensive inspection agent or designated maintenance business entity for comprehensive inspection or suspension of affairs, he or she shall consult with the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment; in cases falling under subparagraph 1, 15 or 18, the designation shall be revoked: <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Jan. 6, 2015; Dec. 26, 2017; Aug. 27, 2019; Jan. 30, 2024; Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. Where he or she has been designated by fraud or other improper means;
  2. Where he or she has accepted unjust enrichment in relation to the affairs or engaged in other improper conduct;
  3. Where it is deemed inappropriate for him or her to continue the services due to the bad financial status, etc.;
  4. Where he or she has prepared a false motor vehicle inspection sheet without conducting inspection or has prepared a motor vehicle inspection sheet inconsistent with the results of inspection;
  5. Where he or she has performed an inspection with machines and apparatuses not submitted to a precision test pursuant to Article 40(1) or machines and apparatuses which have unconfirmed accuracy;
  6. Where he or she has performed an inspection by omitting any inspection items required for inspection of the structure and devices of a motor vehicle pursuant to Article 43(2) or 43-2(3);
  7. Where he or she has failed to take measures in response to the results of inspection pursuant to Article 43(3) (including cases applied mutatis mutandis in Article 43-2(3));
  8. Where he or she has failed to film or fraudulently filmed the inspection and results of the inspection (including where he or she has failed to take pictures of the front and back sides of the motor vehicle including its registered license plate, or where he or she takes pictures of the motor vehicle with certain parts covered), in violation of Article 43(6) (including cases applied mutatis mutandis in Article 43-2(3));
  8-2. Where he or she manipulates or alters, or has another person manipulate or alter, any value set in a machine or instrument used for inspections of motor vehicles according to the inspection standards for motor vehicles or any value measured by a machine or instrument, in violation of Article 43(7) (including cases applied mutatis mutandis in Article 43-2(3));
  9. Where he or she performs an inspection at a place other than the inspection facilities designated under Article 45(1) or 45-2(1);
  10. Where he or she falls short of the designation standards for facilities, equipment, etc. as prescribed in Article 45(2) or 45-2(3);
  10-2. Where he or she has failed to apply for a modification to designation under the latter part of Article 45(2) or has failed to make a report, in violation of the proviso of that paragraph;
  11. Where he or she has performed an inspection beyond the scope of inspection services or beyond his or her capacity in terms of skilled technicians under Article 45(3) or 45-2(3);
  12. Where he or she has had another person provide the inspection services under his or her name, in violation of Article 45(7) (including cases applied mutatis mutandis in Article 45-2(4));
  12-2. Where he or she has failed to apply for a modification to designation under the latter part of Article 45-2(2) or has failed to make a report, in violation of the proviso of that paragraph;
  13. Where he or she has had a person who is not an inspection technician conduct inspection as prescribed in Article 46(1);
  14. Where he or she has failed to comply with the order for dismissal of an inspection technician or suspension of duty as prescribed in Article 46(2);
  15. Where his or her registration of motor vehicle management business has been revoked under Article 66;
  16. Where he or she has failed to make a report as prescribed in Article 72(1) or has made a false report;
  17. Where he or she refuses, interferes with or evades an inspection under Article 72(2), fails to answer a question, or gives a false answer to any question;
  18. Where he or she has provided the inspection services during the suspension period of services in violation of the order for suspension of services under this Article.
  (2) Where the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport has canceled or suspended motor vehicle inspection by a motor vehicle testing agent, a comprehensive inspection agent or a designated maintenance business entity, etc. pursuant to paragraph (1), the Minister shall have the electronic information processing organization referred to in Article 69 record and manage the status thereof. <Added on Jan. 6, 2015>
  (3) Where a designated maintenance business entity or a designated maintenance business entity for comprehensive inspection has his or her designation revoked in any of the cases specified by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, among the cases specified in paragraph (1), the Mayor/Do Governor or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu shall inform such fact to the owners of motor vehicles that underwent a regular or comprehensive inspection not longer than six months ago, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Added on Dec. 26, 2017>
  (4) Detailed standards, procedures, and management methods for dispositions under paragraphs (1) and (2), and other necessary matters, shall be determined by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport (referring to Joint Ministerial Decrees in cases of a comprehensive inspection agent and a designated maintenance business entity for comprehensive motor vehicle inspection; hereafter in Article 46 and 46-2, the same shall apply). <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Jan. 6, 2015; Dec. 26, 2017; Apr. 13, 2021>
  [This Article Added on Feb. 6, 2009]

Article 46 (Duties of inspection technician) (1) Matters necessary regarding the classification and duties of skilled technicians (hereinafter referred to as "inspection technicians") such as a performance evaluation agent, a motor vehicle inspection agent, a comprehensive inspection agent, a designated maintenance business entity, and a designated maintenance business entity for comprehensive inspection designated in accordance with Articles 35-14(1), 44(1), 44-2(1), 45(1), and 45-2(1) shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Jan. 30, 2024; Feb. 27, 2026>
  (2) If an inspection technician fall under any of the following subparagraphs, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall order the relevant performance evaluation agent, motor vehicle inspection agent, comprehensive inspection agent, designated maintenance business entity, or designated maintenance business entity for comprehensive inspection to dismiss or suspend an inspection technician from his or her duties for a certain period of time, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport; provided, if the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport has a comprehensive inspection agent or a designated maintenance business entity for comprehensive inspection dismiss or suspend his or her duties as an inspection technician, he or she shall consult the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment: <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Aug. 27, 2019; Apr. 13, 2021; Jan. 30, 2024; Oct. 1, 2025; Feb. 27, 2026>
  1. Where he or she has accepted unjust enrichment in relation to the affairs or engaged in other improper conduct;
  2. Where he or she has prepared a false motor vehicle inspection sheet without performing the inspection or has prepared a motor vehicle inspection sheet inconsistent with the results of inspection;
  2-2. Where he or she has conducted an evaluation by omitting some of the evaluation items required for a performance evaluation under Article 35-13(1) or (2);
  2-3. Where he or she has failed to notify the results of the performance evaluation under Article 35-14(1);
  3. Where he or she has performed an inspection with machines and apparatuses not submitted to a precision test pursuant to Article 40(1) or machines and apparatuses which have unconfirmed accuracy;
  4. Where he or she has performed an inspection by omitting any inspection items required for inspection of the structure and devices of a motor vehicle pursuant to Article 43(2) or 43-2(3);
  5. Where he or she has failed to take measures in response to the results of inspection pursuant to Article 43(3) (including cases applied mutatis mutandis in Article 43-2(3));
  6. Where he or she has failed to record the scene and results of the inspection, in violation of Article 43(6) (including cases applied mutatis mutandis in Article 43-2(3)) or recorded by falsity;
  7. Where he or she has performed an inspection at a place other than the inspection facilities designated under Article 45(1) or 45-2(1);
  8. Where he or she has performed an inspection beyond the scope of inspection services or beyond his or her capacity in terms of skilled technicians under Article 45(3) or 45-2(3);
  9. Where he or she fails to receive any education on motor vehicle inspections without good cause, in violation of Article 46-2(1).
  (3) No inspection technician who has been dismissed pursuant to paragraph (2) shall be appointed as inspection technician unless one year has elapsed from the date on which he or she is dismissed. <Added on Jan. 6, 2015; Apr. 13, 2021; Jan. 30, 2024>
  (4) The Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall authorize the electronic information processing organization under Article 69 to manage the status of the inspection technicians who were dismissed or suspended, etc. pursuant to paragraph (2). <Added on Jan. 6, 2015; Jan. 30, 2024>
  (5) Detailed standards and procedures for issuing orders under paragraphs (2) and (4), methods of management, and other necessary matters shall be determined by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Jan. 6, 2015>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]
  [Title Amended on Jan. 30, 2024]

Article 46-2 (Training of inspection technician) (1) Inspection technicians shall regularly receive training on motor vehicle inspections (hereinafter referred to as "training of inspectors") conducted by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport according to their classification for at least a certain period of time. <Amended on Jan. 30, 2024>
  (2) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall create and manage the records regarding whether inspection technicians have completed education for inspectors, in an electronic information processing organization established under Article 69. <Amended on Jan. 30, 2024>
  (3) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may designate specialized educational institutions for providing training for inspectors, if necessary.
  (4) Matters necessary for targets, content, methods, cycle, and timing of training for inspectors, standards and procedures for designating specialized educational institutions under paragraph (3), the revocation of such designation, etc. shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.
  [This Article Added on Apr. 13, 2021]
  [Title Amended on Jan. 30, 2024]

Article 47 (Verification of taximeters) (1) A person who manufactures, repairs, imports, or uses a taxi fare meter (hereinafter referred to as "taximeter") shall have it verified by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (2) If deemed necessary, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may designate a specialized institution authorized to verify a taximeter (hereinafter referred to as "specialized taximeter verification institution") and may require such institution to conduct verification on his or her behalf under paragraph (1), as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (3) No one shall manufacture, repair, import, or use a taximeter nor sell it nor intermediate its transaction without verification as prescribed in paragraph (1) or (2).
  (4) Article 40 and 45(2) and (3) shall apply mutatis mutandis to specialized taximeter verification institutions.
  (5) Where a specialized taximeter verification institution falls under any of the following cases, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may revoke such designation or order suspension of all or part of its business for a period of up to six months; provided, the cases falling under subparagraph 1 or 8 shall be subject to the revocation of such designation: <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  1. Where he or she has been designated by fraud or other improper means;
  2. Where he or she has accepted unjust enrichment in relation to the affairs or engaged in other improper conduct;
  3. Where it is deemed inappropriate for him or her to continue the services due to the bad financial status, etc.;
  4. Where he or she fails to meet any of the designation standards for facilities, equipment, etc. as prescribed in Article 45(2) which shall apply mutatis mutandis pursuant to paragraph (4);
  5. Where he or she has performed an inspection with machines and apparatuses not submitted to a precision test pursuant to Article 40(1) or machines and apparatuses which have unconfirmed accuracy;
  6. Where he or she has failed to make a report as prescribed in Article 72(1) or has made a false report;
  7. Where he or she refuses, interferes with or evades an inspection under Article 72(2), fails to answer a question, or gives a false answer to any question;
  8. Where he or she has provided the verification services during the suspension period of services in violation of the order of suspension for services under this Article.
  (6) Detail standards and procedures for taking administrative dispositions pursuant to paragraph (5) and other necessary matters shall be determined by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]

CHAPTER V-II REPLACEMENT OF, OR REFUND FOR, MOTOR VEHICLES

Article 47-2 (Requirements for replacement of, or refund for, motor vehicles) (1) If a motor vehicle self-certified and sold in the domestic market by a motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. meets all the following requirements, its owner (excluding transport business entities under the Passenger Transport Service Act or the Trucking Transport Business Act who own at least two motor vehicles for business use) may claim replacement of such motor vehicle with a new motor vehicle or the refund of the price paid for such motor vehicle from a motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. within two years from the date of delivery:
  1. A motor vehicle sold under a written contract providing as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport that the motor vehicle can be replaced with a new motor vehicle, assuring refund of the purchase price, etc. upon the occurrence of a defect;
  2. A motor vehicle with doubtful safety, with a significantly deteriorated economic value, or unusable, due to a structural or mechanical defect referred to in Article 29(1);
  3. A motor vehicle falling under either of the following cases, where one year has not passed since the motor vehicle was delivered to its owner (if the mileage exceeds 20,000 kilometers, the period shall be deemed to have lapsed):
  a. Where a motor vehicle has been repaired at least twice by the relevant motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. (including persons to whom repair work has been entrusted by the motor vehicle manufacturer, etc.), due to a defect that occurred with the same symptom in the structure or any of the devices specified by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, such as its motor, power transmission system, steering system, or brake system, but the identical defect reoccurs; provided, included herein shall be cases where a motor vehicle has been repaired at least once and the total period of repair exceeds 30 days;
  b. Where a motor vehicle has been repaired at least three times by the relevant motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. (including persons to whom repair work has been entrusted by the motor vehicle manufacturer, etc.), due to a defect that occurred with the same symptom in any structure or device, other than the structure or any of the devices specified in item a, but the identical defect reoccurs; provided, included herein shall be cases where a motor vehicle has been repaired at least once and the total period of repair exceeds 30 days.
  (2) If a defect with the same symptom reoccurs after a motor vehicle falling under paragraph (1) has been repaired once in the case of paragraph (1)3a or twice in the case of paragraph (1)3b, the owner of such motor vehicle (hereinafter referred to as "owner of the defective motor vehicle") shall notify the relevant motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. thereof, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.
  [This Article Newly Amended on Oct. 24, 2017]

Article 47-3 (Presumption of defects) Any defect discovered within 1 year from the date a motor vehicle falling under Article 47-2(1) is delivered to the owner of the defective motor vehicle shall be presumed to have existed at the time of delivery. <Amended on Feb. 13, 2024>
  [This Article Newly Amended on Oct. 24, 2017]

Article 47-4 (Application for arbitration for replacement or refund) (1) At the request of the owner of a defective motor vehicle, the Deliberation Committee for Motor Vehicle Safety and Defects shall commence arbitration proceedings for replacement or refund of the purchase price (hereinafter referred to as "arbitration for replacement or refund") where all the following requirements are met; in such cases, matters necessary for the methods, etc. of filing an application for arbitration for replacement or refund shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport: <Amended on Jun. 10, 2022>
  1. Where the relevant motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. accepted the rules on arbitration for replacement or refund under Article 47-7(2)1b (hereinafter referred to as "rules on arbitration for replacement or refund"), in advance, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport;
  2. Where the owner of the defective motor vehicle accepted the rules on arbitration for replacement or refund, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, at the time of concluding the relevant sale and purchase contract or filing an application for replacement or refund.
  (2) A motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. who accepted the rules on arbitration for replacement or refund in advance shall inform the purchaser of its acceptance of the rules on arbitration for replacement or refund at the time of selling a motor vehicle.
  (3) The owner of a defective motor vehicle or the relevant motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. may appoint any of the following persons as an agent; in such cases, the authority of an agent shall be clarified in writing: <Added on Jun. 10, 2022>
  1. Where the owner of the defective motor vehicle or the relevant motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. is a corporation (including an organization, etc. under Article 13(2) of the Framework Act on National Taxes), an executive officer or employee of such corporation;
  2. The legal representative, the spouse, a lineal ascendant or descendant, or a sibling of the owner of the defective motor vehicle;
  3. Where the owner of the defective motor vehicle is a lessor defined in the Specialized Credit Finance Business Act, a lessee of the relevant motor vehicle;
  4. An attorney-at-law.
  (4) If an application for arbitration for replacement or refund filed under paragraph (1) is defective, the Deliberation Committee for Motor Vehicle Safety and Defects shall order the applicant to correct such defect within a reasonable period. If the applicant fails to do so within the specified period, the Deliberation Committee for Motor Vehicle Safety and Defects shall reject the application. <Amended on Jun. 10, 2022>
  (5) If deemed necessary for rendering an arbitral award for replacement or refund, the Deliberation Committee for Motor Vehicle Safety and Defects may request the relevant motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. and the owner of the defective motor vehicle to do the following; and may request a performance testing agent to inspect the relevant motor vehicle to locate defects; in such cases, matters necessary for such inspection shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport: <Amended on Jun. 10, 2022>
  1. Submission of materials necessary for arbitration;
  2. Provision of the defective motor vehicle by its owner;
  3. Other matters deemed necessary by the competent arbitral tribunal under Article 47-9 for rendering an arbitral award for replacement or refund.
  [This Article Newly Amended on Oct. 24, 2017]

Article 47-5 (Effects of arbitral awards) (1) An arbitral award for replacement or refund is equally as binding as a final court judgment on the relevant motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. and for the owner of the relevant defective motor vehicle.
  (2) Article 36 of the Arbitration Act shall apply mutatis mutandis to any appeal against an arbitral award for replacement or refund; and to the cancellation of an arbitral award for replacement or refund.
  (3) Except as otherwise provided in this Act, the Arbitration Act shall apply mutatis mutandis to the procedures for arbitration for replacement or refund.
  [This Article Newly Amended on Oct. 24, 2017]

Article 47-6 (Methods for replacement or refund upon arbitral awards) (1) Where a motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. delivers a new motor vehicle in replacement of a defective motor vehicle to its owner in compliance with an arbitral award for replacement or refund, such motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. shall not claim the return of the benefits derived from the ownership, operation, etc. of the defective motor vehicle from the owner of the defective motor vehicle.
  (2) Where a defective motor vehicle is replaced with a new motor vehicle in compliance with an arbitral award for replacement or refund, the taxes and public charges prescribed by Presidential Decree, including acquisition tax for the new motor vehicle, shall be deemed paid at the time the owner of the defective motor vehicle initially purchased the defective motor vehicle. <Amended on Jun. 9, 2020>
  (3) Where a motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. refunds a purchase price in compliance with an arbitral award for replacement or refund, such motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. shall meet the standards determined by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport in making a refund to the owner of the defective motor vehicle.
  (4) Even where a motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. is obligated to deliver a new motor vehicle in replacement to the owner of a defective motor vehicle according to an arbitral award for replacement or refund, the motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. may choose to refund the purchase price on any of the grounds prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, including where replacement is impossible due to the termination of manufacture or other similar event.
  [This Article Newly Amended on Oct. 24, 2017]

Article 47-7 (Establishment of Committee for Deliberation on Safety and Defects of Motor Vehicles) (1) The Committee for Deliberation on Safety and Defects of Motor Vehicles shall be established within the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport in order to conduct arbitration proceedings, etc. for replacement or refund; and to deliberate on matters related to the correction, etc. of manufacturing defects.
  (2) The functions of the Deliberation Committee for Motor Vehicle Safety and Defects are as follows:
  1. The following functions related to arbitration for replacement of, or refund for, motor vehicles:
  a. Arbitration for replacement or refund;
  b. Establishment and amendment of rules on arbitration for replacement and refund;
  2. Deliberation on the following matters in connection with correction, etc. of manufacturing defects:
  a. Issuance of orders to suspend the manufacture, assembly, import, or sale of motor vehicles under Article 30-3(1);
  b. Investigations into self-certification, etc. under Article 30-3(2);
  c. Substitution of corrective measures and issuance of orders for correction under Article 31(3);
  d. Investigations into manufacturing defects under Article 31(4);
  e. Compensation for motor vehicle owners who made self-correction under Article 31-2;
  f. Evaluation of the level of safety of motor vehicles under Article 33-2;
  g. Other matters specified by Presidential Decree as those related to correction of manufacturing defects, gratuitous repair, etc.;
  3. Establishment, amendment, and repeal of rules on the operation, etc. of the Deliberation Committee for Motor Vehicle Safety and Defects.
  (3) In order to efficiently perform the functions specified in the subparagraphs of paragraph (2), the Deliberation Committee for Motor Vehicle Safety and Defects may establish a sectoral committee or subcommittee, as prescribed by Presidential Decree. In such cases, a resolution of a sectoral committee or subcommittee shall be deemed the resolution of the Deliberation Committee for Motor Vehicle Safety and Defects. <Added on Jun. 10, 2022>
  [This Article Newly Amended on Oct. 24, 2017]

Article 47-8 (Organization and operation of Committee for Deliberation on Safety and Defects of Motor Vehicles) (1) The Committee for Deliberation on Safety and Defects of Motor Vehicles shall be comprised of up to 100 members, including one chairperson, and its members shall be appointed or commissioned by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport from among any of the following persons: <Amended on Jun. 10, 2022; Feb. 20, 2024>
  1. A current or former judge, public prosecutor, or attorney-at-law serving for at least 10 years;
  2. A person who has served in a university or college as at least an associate professor, majoring in law;
  3. A person who has served in a university, a college, or an officially accredited research institute as at least an associate professor or equivalent position, majoring in a field related to motor vehicles;
  4. A person who has served as at least a Grade-IV public official with work experience in a field related to motor vehicles;
  5. A person who has served in a public institution under Article 4 of the Act on the Management of Public Institutions with work experience of at least 10 years in a field related to motor vehicles;
  6. A person who has been engaged in a motor vehicle-related role for at least 10 years as a professional engineer or master craftsman qualified under Article 10 of the National Technical Qualifications Act;
  7. A person who has served in a public institution under Article 4 of the Act on the Management of Public Institutions; or in a consumer protection agency for consumer protection for at least 10 years;
  8. A current or former executive officer of a consumer organization registered under Article 29 of the Framework Act on Consumers;
  9. A current or former executive officer of a business entity or a trade association related the manufacture, etc. of motor vehicles;
  10. A person who has majored in the field of information and communications technology, cyber security, or software, who is or was an associate professor or higher or a position equivalent thereto at a university or officially recognized research institute.
  (2) Each member shall hold office for a term of 2 years and may be reelected for a second time. <Amended on Dec. 2, 2025>
  (3) Except in any of the following cases, a committee member who is not a public official shall not be dismissed from office against his or her will: <Amended on Jun. 10, 2022; Dec. 2, 2025>
  1. Where a committee member falls under Article 47-10(1);
  1-2. Where a committee member fails to recuse himself or herself under Article 47-10(5) although he or she is subject to exclusion on any ground specified in the subparagraphs of Article 47-10(2);
  1-3. Where a committee member has violated the obligation to maintain confidentiality under Article 47-13; 
  2. Where a committee member is deemed too seriously incompetent to perform his or her duties, due to prolonged mental or physical incapacity;
  3. Where a committee member shall be dismissed on any of other grounds specified by Presidential Decree, such as a breach of any of his or her official duties.
  (4) The chairperson shall be appointed by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport from among the committee members.
  (5) A majority of the members of the Deliberation Committee for Motor Vehicle Safety and Defects shall constitute a quorum, and any resolution thereof shall require the concurring vote of a majority of those present.
  (6) The Committee for Deliberation on Safety and Defects of Motor Vehicles shall take minutes of a meeting regarding the following matters. If deemed necessary, the proceeding of a meeting may be recorded by stenography or by an audio or video recording system: <Added on Feb. 4, 2020>
  1. The date, time, and place of the meeting;
  2. Members who are present at the meeting;
  3. Details of deliberation and resolutions adopted.
  (7) The minutes of a meeting taken pursuant to paragraph (6) (excluding minutes of a meeting of an arbitral tribunal under Article 47-9) shall be made available to the public; provided, the information that meets the requirements prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as trade secret of a motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. or a parts manufacturer, etc. and personal information defined in subparagraph 1 of Article 2 of the Personal Information Protection Act, need not be made available to the public. <Added on Feb. 4, 2020>
  (8) Other matters necessary for the organization, operation, etc. of the Deliberation Committee for Motor Vehicle Safety and Defects shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Feb. 4, 2020>
  [This Article Newly Amended on Oct. 24, 2017]

Article 47-9 (Organization and operation of arbitral tribunals) (1) Arbitration by the Deliberation Committee for Motor Vehicle Safety and Defects shall be conducted by an arbitral tribunal comprised of three members.
  (2) Members of an arbitral tribunal (hereinafter referred to as "arbitrators") shall be appointed by the committee chairperson for each case from among the members of the Deliberation Committee for Motor Vehicle Safety and Defects; but the members whom the parties agree to select shall be appointed as arbitrators.
  (3) Arbitrators shall endeavor to conduct arbitration proceedings in an expeditious, impartial, and economical manner; and the parties to such arbitration proceedings shall participate conscientiously, based on mutual trust and understanding.
  (4) The chief of an arbitral tribunal shall be selected by agreement among arbitrators.
  (5) A meeting of an arbitral tribunal shall be convened by the chief of the arbitral tribunal, who shall notify the relevant parties of the fact that the meeting is to be held, including the following information, no later than seven days before the first meeting on the case at issue is held; provided, where convening a meeting is urgently required or there exists any unavoidable reason, such notification may be given by the day immediately before the meeting is held: <Amended on Jun. 10, 2022>
  1. The date, time, and place of the meeting;
  2. The major careers of the members to be present at the meeting;
  3. The proceedings for filing a request for a challenge, appointing an agent, submitting materials related to disputes, etc.
  (6) All arbitrators of an arbitral tribunal shall constitute a quorum for a meeting of the arbitral tribunal; and any resolution thereof shall require the concurring vote of a majority of the arbitrators.
  [This Article Newly Amended on Oct. 24, 2017]

Article 47-10 (Disqualification as members of Committee for Deliberation on Safety and Defects of Motor Vehicles) (1) No person falling under any subparagraph of Article 33 of the State Public Officials Act shall be qualified for membership of the Deliberation Committee for Motor Vehicle Safety and Defects.
  (2) A member of the Deliberation Committee for Motor Vehicle Safety and Defects shall be excluded from performing his or her duties in the relevant case, in any of the following cases: <Amended on Feb. 4, 2020; Jun. 10, 2022>
  1. If a committee member or the current or former spouse of the committee member is a party to the dispute brought before the Committee (hereinafter referred to as "case at issue") or is involved in the case at issue as a joint right-holder or joint obligor with any of the parties to the case at issue;
  2. If a committee member is the current or former relative of any party to the case at issue;
  3. If a committee member has made any statement or has provided expert opinion regarding the case at issue;
  4. If a committee member serves or has served as the representative of any party to the case at issue;
  5. If a committee member was involved in an action or omission that gave rise to the case at issue;
  6. If a committee member is a current or former employee of the motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. against whom the case at issue is brought;
  7. If the corporation or organization to which a committee member belongs has provided advisory services, research services, or other services with regard to the case at issue (including cases where a committee member belonged to such corporation or organization during the latest three years);
  8. If a committee member has provided advisory services, research services, or other services during the latest two years in relation to the motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. against whom the case at issue is brought;
  9. If a committee member or the corporation or organization to which the committee member belongs has concluded, with the motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. against whom the case at issue is brought, a contract for entrustment with sale, parts supply, or gratuitous repair by proxy under Article 32-2(2) during the latest two years;
  10. If the corporation or organization to which a committee member belongs and the motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. against whom the case at issue is brought have been in the same business group under the Monopoly Regulation and Fair Trade Act or in a parent-subsidiary relationship under the Commercial Act during the latest two years.
  (3) If any ground for exclusion exists, the chairperson may decide whether or not to exclude a committee member, ex officio or at the request of any party to the case at issue. <Amended on Jun. 10, 2022>
  (4) If the circumstances indicate that it would be impracticable to expect impartiality from a committee member in performing his or her duties, any party may file a request for a challenge to the committee member with the chairperson, who shall decide whether or not to accept the request without referring the request to the Deliberation Committee for Motor Vehicle Safety and Defects if he or she finds that the request is well-founded; provided, if it is not reasonable for the chairperson to make a decision as the circumstances indicate that it would be impracticable to expect impartiality from the chairperson in performing his or her duties or for any other reason, the Deliberation Committee for Motor Vehicle Safety and Defects shall decide whether or not to accept the request following its resolution. <Amended on Jun. 10, 2022>
  (5) If a committee member is subject to exclusion on any ground specified in the subparagraphs of paragraph (2), such member shall recuse himself or herself from performing his or her duties in the case at issue, and if the member is subject to exclusion on any ground specified in paragraph (4), he or she may recuse himself or herself from performing his or her duties in the case at issue. <Amended on Jun. 10, 2022>
  (6) Paragraphs (1) through (5) shall apply mutatis mutandis to personnel of the organization involved in arbitration proceedings for the operation and administrative processing of the Deliberation Committee for Motor Vehicle Safety and Defects.
  (7) Upon receipt of a challenge under paragraph (4), an arbitral tribunal shall suspend arbitration proceedings until it makes a decision on the challenge.
  [This Article Newly Amended on Oct. 24, 2017]

Article 47-11 (Re-deliberation on results of deliberation by Committee for Deliberation on Safety and Defects of Motor Vehicles) (1) In any of the following cases, the Deliberation Committee for Motor Vehicle Safety and Defects may re-deliberate on a resolution adopted through deliberation on the correction, etc. of manufacturing defects under Article 47-7(2)2:
  1. If a request for re-deliberation is made by a party to the case, an interested person, a whistleblower, or other person based on objective facts;
  2. If the chairperson of the Committee recognizes that any material fact that is likely to affect the deliberation was omitted or that the findings from investigation have a serious defect;
  3. If the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport determines that the case at issue is an important matter and makes a request for re-deliberation.
  (2) In order for the Deliberation Committee for Motor Vehicle Safety and Defects to change the results of original deliberation and resolution through re-deliberation under paragraph (1), a resolution shall be adopted by the attendance of a majority of current members of the Committee and a concurring vote of at least 2/3 of those present. If the Committee fails to adopt such resolution by a concurring vote of at least 2/3 of those present, the initial deliberation and resolution shall become a final decision by the Deliberation Committee for Motor Vehicle Safety and Defects.
  (3) Except as provided in paragraphs (1) and (2), the procedures and methods for re-deliberation by the Deliberation Committee for Motor Vehicle Safety and Defects and matters necessary for the notification of results shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Added on Feb. 4, 2020]
  [Previous Article 47-11 moved to Article 47-12 <Feb. 4, 2020>]

Article 47-12 (Entrustment of operation and administrative processing of Committee for Deliberation on Safety and Defects of Motor Vehicles) (1) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may entrust the Korea Transportation Safety Authority with part of administrative tasks for the operation and administrative processing of the Deliberation Committee for Motor Vehicle Safety and Defects, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. The organization, human resources, etc. for performing administrative tasks of the Deliberation Committee for Motor Vehicle Safety and Defects, as entrusted by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport in such cases, shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Oct. 24, 2017>
  (2) Where part of administrative tasks is entrusted under paragraph (1), the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may pay contributions to the Korea Transportation Safety Authority to cover the expenses incurred in the operation and administrative processing of the Deliberation Committee for Motor Vehicle Safety and Defects. <Amended on Oct. 24, 2017>
  [This Article Newly Amended on Oct. 24, 2017]
  [Moved from Article 47-11 <Feb. 4, 2020>]

Article 47-13 (Prohibition on disclosure of confidential information) A person who is or has been a member the Committee for Deliberation on Safety and Defects of Motor Vehicles shall not divulge any confidential information learned in the course of performing his or her duties.
  [This Article Added on Dec. 2, 2025]

CHAPTER VI MANAGEMENT OF MOTORCYCLES

Article 48 (Reporting on use of motorcycles) (1) A person who intends to acquire and use a motorcycle determined by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport (hereinafter referred to as "motorcycle") shall report on its use (hereinafter referred to as "report on use") to the head of a Si/Gun/Gu and shall be assigned a motorcycle number, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on May 24, 2011; Mar. 23, 2013; Sep. 14, 2023>
  (2) Upon receipt of a report on use of a motorcycle under paragraph (1), the head of a Si/Gun/Gu shall designate the number of the motorcycle, enter the necessary matters in the register of motorcycles, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, and issue a certificate of completion of report on the use of motorcycles (hereinafter referred to as "certificate of completion of report on the use of motorcycles") <Added on Sep. 14, 2023>
  (3) Where there are matters to be changed as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, from among the matters to be reported on under paragraph (1), or where a motorcycle is disused, an owner of the motorcycle shall report thereon to the head of a Si/Gun/Gu. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Sep. 14, 2023>
  (4) A person who has acquired the ownership of a motorcycle reported on under paragraph (1) by transfer shall report on the transfer of ownership thereof to the head of a Si/Gun/Gu, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport; provided, where an owner who has acquired the motorcycle by transfer has failed to report such transfer of ownership, a transferor may report thereon, in lieu of such transferee, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Sep. 14, 2023>
  (5) Where a person who sells a motorcycle to a consumer sells a newly manufactured, assembled, or imported motorcycle, he or she sells such motorcycle, he or she shall immediately transmit the manufacturing information necessary for preparing the register of motorcycles to the computerized information processing organization under Article 69, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Added on Sep. 14, 2023>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]
  [Title Amended on Sep. 14, 2023]

Article 49 (Obligation to affix motorcycle license plates) (1) No motorcycle shall be operated without a motorcycle license plate, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, affixed to the part of its rear to be readily visible. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Jun. 9, 2020>
  (2) Upon receipt of a report on use, the head of a Si/Gun/Gu shall attach a two-wheeled motor vehicle license plate to the relevant two-wheeled motor vehicle, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport; provided, where a person who reports the use of a two-wheeled motor vehicle intends to directly attach a license plate of a two-wheeled motor vehicle, he or she may allow the person to do so, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Sep. 14, 2023; Feb. 20, 2024>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]

Article 50 (Structure and devices of motorcycles) (1) No motorcycle shall be operated if its main structure and devices fail to meet the safety standards.
  (2) Matters regarding the scope of and the safety standards for the main structure and devices as prescribed in paragraph (1) shall be determined by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]

Article 51 (Inspections of motorcycles) (1) The owner of a motorcycle (in cases falling under subparagraph 1, referring to a person scheduled to file a report on use) shall undergo an inspection of the relevant motorcycle conducted by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport according to the following classifications (hereinafter referred to as "inspection of a motorcycle"):
  1. Inspection of use of a motorcycle: Inspection conducted where a motorcycle intends to be reused again after filing a report on the use of the motorcycle, the disuse of which has been reported pursuant to Article 48(3);
  2. Regular inspection of a motorcycle: Inspection regularly conducted at a fixed interval after a report on use;
  3. Inspection of tuning of motorcycles: Inspection conducted where a motorcycle is tuned pursuant to Article 34 which is applied mutatis mutandis in Article 52;
  4. Provisional inspection of a motorcycle: Inspection conducted when required by this Act or an order issued under this Act or by an application from an owner of a motorcycle.
  (2) When the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport conducts an inspection of a motorcycle, the Minister shall, jointly with the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment, verify whether the structure and devices of the relevant motorcycle meet the inspection standards prescribed by Joint Decree (hereinafter referred to as "inspection standards for motorcycles") for the following motorcycles: <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. A common field in which the verification of the identity of motorcycles and of the operating conditions of exhaust gas-related devices, etc. are conducted as sensory tests and functional tests;
  2. Motorcycle safety inspection;
  3. Inspection of exhaust gases and noise and vibration of motorcycles.
  (3) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport has conducted an inspection of a motorcycle, he or she shall notify the owner of the motorcycle of the results thereof, as prescribed by Joint Ministerial Decrees.
  (4) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall take the following measures against a motorcycle that has passed an inspection for motorcycles:
  1. Inspection of the use of a motorcycle: Issuance of a certificate of inspection of the use of a motorcycle;
  2. Regular inspection of a motorcycle, a tuning inspection of a motorcycle, or a temporary inspection of a motorcycle: to record the fact of inspection in the certificate of report on use of a motorcycle.
  (5) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport deems that an owner of a motorcycle is unable to submit his or her motorcycle for inspections under paragraph (1)2 through 4 due to a natural disaster or other unavoidable causes or events, he or she may extend the period or postpone the inspection of the motorcycle.
  (6) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport has conducted a regular inspection of motorcycles, he or she shall record and keep the scene of the inspection and the results thereof in the electronic information processing organization under Article 69 by the period prescribed by Joint Ministerial Decrees.
  (7) No person shall manipulate or alter, or have another person manipulate or alter, any value set in a machine or instrument used for inspections of motorcycles according to the inspection standards for motorcycles or any value measured by a machine or instrument.
  (8) Necessary matters concerning the subject matters, frequency, methods, procedures, items, validity period, etc. of motorcycle inspections shall be prescribed by Joint Ministerial Decrees.
  [This Article Added on Sep. 14, 2023]

Article 51-2 (Designation of motorcycle comprehensive inspection agents) (1) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may designate the Korea Transportation Safety Authority as a person who conducts inspections of motorcycles on behalf of the Korea Transportation Safety Authority (hereinafter referred to as "motorcycle inspection agent") and have him or her conduct inspections of motorcycles and notify the results thereof on behalf of the Korea Transportation Safety Authority.
  (2) Matters necessary for the standards for facilities, equipment, and the procedure for designation of motorcycle inspection agents shall be prescribed by Joint Ministerial Decrees.
  [This Article Added on Sep. 14, 2023]

Article 51-3 (Designation of designated maintenance business entity for motorcycles) (1) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport deems it necessary for efficiently conducting regular inspections of motorcycles, he or she may designate a person who has secured certain facilities, equipment, and technical personnel to conduct a regular inspection of motorcycles (including notification of the results thereof).
  (2) Any corporation or organization that wishes to obtain designation pursuant to paragraph (1) shall submit an application for designation to the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport after meeting the standards for facilities, equipment, and skilled technicians prescribed by Joint Ministerial Decrees.
  (3) Where a person who has obtained designation pursuant to paragraph (1) (hereinafter referred to as "designated maintenance business entity for motorcycles") intends to modify important matters prescribed by Joint Ministerial Decrees from among the designated matters, he or she shall file an application for designation of modification with the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport; provided, where he or she intends to modify matters excluding important matters prescribed by Joint Ministerial Decrees, he or she shall file a report thereon with the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.
  (4) Necessary matters for procedures for the designation of a designated maintenance business entity for motorcycles, the scope of inspection affairs, etc. shall be prescribed by Joint Ministerial Decrees.
  (5) A designated maintenance business entity whose designation as a designated maintenance business entity for motorcycles under Article 51-4(1) has been revoked, or a person who applies for the designation of a designated maintenance business entity from the same place of business shall not be eligible for designation as the designated maintenance business entity for motorcycles unless five years have passed since the revocation of its designation.
  (6) Upon receipt of an application for regular inspection of a motorcycle from a motorcycle owner, a designated maintenance business entity for motorcycles shall check whether the relevant motorcycle meets the inspection standards for motorcycles.
  (7) A designated maintenance business entity of motorcycles shall not have another person conduct regular inspection of motorcycles under his or her name.
  (8) Where a designated maintenance business entity of motorcycles suspends or closes the operation, he or she shall report to the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, as prescribed by Ministerial Decree of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.
  [This Article Added on Sep. 14, 2023]

Article 51-4 (Revocation of designation of motorcycle inspection agents) (1) Where a motorcycle inspection agent or a designated maintenance business entity of motorcycles falls under any of the following cases, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may revoke such designation or order suspension of all or part of its business for a period of up to six months; provided, the cases falling under subparagraph 1 or 18 shall be subject to the revocation of such designation:
  1. Where he or she has been designated by fraud or other improper means;
  2. Where he or she has accepted unjust enrichment in relation to the affairs or engaged in other improper conduct;
  3. Where it is deemed inappropriate for him or her to continue the services due to the bad financial status, etc.;
  4. Where he or she has prepared a false motorcycle inspection sheet without performing the motorcycle inspection or has prepared a motorcycle inspection sheet inconsistent with the results of motorcycle inspection;
  5. Where he or she fails to take measures against the results of an inspection of a motorcycle under Article 51(4);
  6. Where he or she has failed to film or fraudulently filmed the inspection and results of the inspection (including where he or she has failed to take pictures of the front and back sides of the motorcycle including its registered license plate, or where he or she takes pictures of the motorcycle with certain parts covered), in violation of Article 51(6);
  7. Where he or she has manipulated or altered, or have another person manipulate or alter, any value set in a machine or instrument used for inspections of motorcycles according to the inspection standards for motorcycles or any value measured by a machine or instrument;
  8. Where an inspection is conducted by omitting some of the inspection items necessary for the inspection of a motorcycle under Article 51(8);
  9. Where he or she has performed an inspection at a place other than the inspection facilities designated under Article 51-3(1);
  10. Where he or she has failed to meet any of the standards for facilities, equipment, etc. referred to in Article 51-3(2);
  11. Where it conducts an inspection beyond the inspection capability or scope of inspection by skilled technicians under Article 51-3(2) or (4) of that Article;
  12. Where he or she fails to file an application for designation of modification or fails to report such designation, in violation of Article 51-3(3);
  13. Where he or she allows another person to conduct regular inspections of motorcycles in his or her name, in violation of Article 51-3(7);
  14. Where he or she has had a person who is not a skilled technician conduct inspection under Article 51-5(1);
  15. Where he or she has failed to comply with the order for dismissal of a skilled technician or suspension of duty under Article 51-5(2);
  16. Where he or she has failed to make a report as prescribed in Article 72(1) or has made a false report;
  17. Where he or she refuses, interferes with or evades an inspection under Article 72(2), fails to answer a question, or gives a false answer to any question;
  18. Where he or she has provided the inspection services during the suspension period of services in violation of the order for suspension of services under this Article.
  (2) Where the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport has canceled or suspended motorcycle inspection agency by a motorcycle inspection agent or a designated motorcycle maintenance business entity pursuant to paragraph (1), the Minister shall have the electronic information processing organization referred to in Article 69 record and manage the status thereof.
  (3) Where a designated maintenance business entity of motorcycles or a designated maintenance business entity of motorcycles has his or her designation revoked in any of the cases specified by Joint Ministerial Decrees, among the cases specified in paragraph (1), the Mayor/Do Governor or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu shall inform such fact to the owner of a motorcycle that underwent a regular inspection of motorcycles not longer than six months ago, as prescribed by Joint Ministerial Decrees.
  (4) Detailed criteria, procedures, and other necessary matters for making dispositions referred to in paragraphs (1) and (2) shall be prescribed by Joint Ministerial Decrees.
  [This Article Added on Sep. 14, 2023]

Article 51-5 (Duties of skilled technicians of motorcycle inspection) (1) Matters necessary for the classification, duties, etc. of skilled technicians to be employed by motorcycle inspection agents and designated maintenance business entities for motorcycles, shall be prescribed by Joint Ministerial Decrees.
  (2) Where a skilled technician referred to in paragraph (1) fall under any of the following cases, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may order the relevant motorcycle inspection agent or designated motorcycle maintenance business entity to dismiss the relevant motorcycle inspection agent or to suspend his or her duties for a specified period, as prescribed by Joint Ministerial Decrees:
  1. Where he or she has accepted unjust enrichment in relation to the affairs or engaged in other improper conduct;
  2. Where he or she has prepared a false motorcycle inspection sheet without performing the motorcycle inspection or has prepared a motorcycle inspection sheet inconsistent with the results of motorcycle inspection;
  3. Where he or she fails to receive any education on motorcycle inspections without good cause, in violation of Article 46-2(1) which is applied mutatis mutandis in Article 52;
  4. Where he or she fails to take measures against the results of an inspection of a motorcycle under Article 51(4);
  5. Where he or she fails to record or keep the scene and results of the inspection of a motorcycle in violation of Article 51(6), or falsely records or keeps such scene and results, in violation of Article 51(6);
  6. Where an inspection is conducted by omitting some of the inspection items necessary for the inspection of a motorcycle under Article 51(8);
  7. Where he or she has performed an inspection at a place other than the inspection facilities designated under Article 51-3(1);
  8. Where it conducts an inspection beyond the inspection capability or scope of inspection by skilled technicians under Article 51-3(2) or (4) of that Article.
  (3) No skilled technician dismissed pursuant to paragraph (2) shall be appointed as a skilled technician under paragraph (1) within one year from the date of such dismissal.
  (4) The Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall authorize the electronic information processing organization under Article 69 to manage the status of the skilled technicians who were dismissed or suspended, etc. pursuant to paragraph (2).
  (5) Detailed criteria, procedures, methods of management, and other necessary matters for orders referred to in paragraphs (2) and (4) shall be prescribed by Joint Ministerial Decrees.
  [This Article Added on Sep. 14, 2023]

Article 52 (Application mutatis mutandis to motorcycles) The provisions of Articles 7 (excluding matters regarding specifying traction battery identification numbers under paragraph (6)), 9, 10(5) (including cases applied mutatis mutandis in Article 10(7)), 13(3) through (7), 18, 20, 22, 23, 26, 28, 29, 30, 30-2 through 30-8, 31, 31-2 through 31-4, 32, 32-2(1)4, 32-2(5) (limited to failures to comply with the duties on ex post facto management under Article 32-2(1)4), 32-2(6), 33, 33-2, 34, 34-2 through 34-4, 35(1), 37 , 43-3 , 46-2 and shall apply mutatis mutandis to a motorcycle. In such cases, the "Mayor/Do Governor" shall be deemed as the "head of a Si/Gun/Gu"; "register", as "register of motorcycles"; "new registration" as "report of use", and "motor vehicle" as "motorcycle", "motor vehicle registration certificate" as "certificate of completion of report on the use of motorcycles", "registration license plate", "motorcycle license plate", "safety standards for motor vehicle" as "safety standards for motorcycles", and "safety standards for parts" as "safety standards for motorcycles", "motor vehicle self-authentication" as "self-authentication of motorcycles", "parts self-certification" as "self-authentication of motorcycles", and "motor vehicle manufacturer, etc." as "manufacturer, etc. of motorcycles", "parts manufacturer, etc." as "parts manufacturers for motorcycles", "motor vehicle technology review and safety inspection" as "actual measurement confirmation of motorcycles", "motor vehicle inspection" as "motorcycle inspection", "regular inspection" as "regular inspection of motorcycles", "temporary inspection" as "temporary inspection of motorcycle", "inspection technicians" as "technical personnel to be equipped by motorcycle inspection agents and motorcycle designated maintenance business entities", and "motor vehicle owner" as "motorcycle owner". <Amended on May 24, 2011; May 23, 2012; Jan. 7, 2014; Aug. 11, 2015, Oct. 24, 2017; Feb. 4, 2020, Apr. 13, 2021, Aug. 16, 2023, Sep. 14, 2023, Jan. 9, 2024, Jan. 30, 2024, Feb. 13, 2024>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]

CHAPTER VII MOTOR VEHICLE MANAGEMENT BUSINESS

Article 53 (Registration of motor vehicle management business) (1) A person who intends to run a motor vehicle management business shall have it registered with the head of a Si/Gun/Gu, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. The same shall also apply where the person intends to modify the registered matters; provided, this shall not apply to any modification to minor registered matters as prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (2) The motor vehicle management business as prescribed in paragraph (1) may be subdivided, as prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (3) Matters regarding the standards and procedures, etc. for a motor vehicle management business registration as prescribed in paragraph (1) shall be determined by ordinance of the Seoul Special Metropolitan City, a Metropolitan City, a Special Self-Governing City/Do (including a Special Self-Governing Province) or a city of a population of at least 500,000 persons within the scope prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. In such cases, a person who intends to engage in the motor vehicle transaction business in an autonomous Gu with a population of at least 500,000 within the Seoul Special Metropolitan City and a Metropolitan City shall satisfy the standards for registration as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Aug. 11, 2015>
  (4) In determining the ordinance pursuant to paragraph (3), factors such as traffic conditions, environmental pollution, surrounding conditions and other local circumstances may be taken into account. <Amended on Aug. 11, 2015>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]

Article 53-2 (Payment of monetary awards) The Special Metropolitan City Mayor, a Metropolitan City Mayor, a Special Self-Governing Mayor, a Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu may grant a monetary award to a person who has reported or charged any persons falling under subparagraphs 1, 13, and 14-2 of Article 79, subparagraphs 1 and 5-3 of Article 80, subparagraph 7-2 of Article 81, or Article 84(4)20, (excluding when a motor vehicle owner reports in a case falling under subparagraph 1 of Article 79, subparagraph 1 of Article 80, and subparagraph 7-2 of Article 81), as prescribed by ordinance of the relevant local government. <Amended on Aug. 11, 2015; Oct. 24, 2017; Feb. 4, 2020; Dec. 7, 2021; Feb. 20, 2024>
  [This Article Added on Jan. 7, 2014]

Article 54 (Grounds for disqualification) (1) None of the following persons shall engage in motor vehicle management business. In the case of a corporation, the same shall also apply if its executive officer falls under any of the following: <Amended on Aug. 11, 2015; Jan. 28, 2016>
  1. A person under adult guardianship or under limited guardianship;
  2. A person who has been declared bankrupt and not yet been reinstated;
  3. A person in whose case one year has not elapsed since the revocation of registration of his or her motor vehicle management business prescribed in this Act (excluding cases where the registration is withdrawn for falling under subparagraph 1 or 2);
  4. A person in whose case two years have not passed since the complete execution of or exemption from a sentence for imprisonment with labor or heavier punishment for violation of this Act;
  5. A person under suspension of execution after having been sentenced to the suspension of execution of a sentence for imprisonment with labor or heavier punishment for violation of this Act.
  (2) Where a person who has made the registration for the motor vehicle management business prescribed in Article 53 (hereinafter referred to as "motor vehicle management business entity") falls under any ground prescribed in paragraph (1), the head of a Si/Gun/Gu shall revoke the relevant registration; provided, the same shall not apply where an executive officer of a corporation who falls under paragraph (1) is replaced within three months.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]

Article 55 (Reporting on transfer or acquisition of motor vehicle management business) (1) A person who intends to transfer or acquire a motor vehicle management business shall report thereon to the head of a Si/Gun/Gu, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (2) Where a corporation who operates the motor vehicle management business intends to merge with another corporation, he or she shall report thereon to the head of a Si/Gun/Gu.
  (3) A person who acquires a motor vehicle management business or a corporation surviving a merger shall succeed to the rights and obligations of the motor vehicle management business entity.
  (4) Where a motor vehicle management business entity suspends or closes all or part of the business, he or she shall report thereon to the head of a Si/Gun/Gu.
  (5) Where a motor vehicle management business entity who has reported pursuant to paragraph (4) is designated as a maintenance business entity or designated maintenance business entity for comprehensive inspection, he or she shall be deemed to have reported on suspension or closure of the relevant designated business. In such cases, the head of a Si/Gun/Gu shall notify the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport thereof. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]

Article 56 (Orders for improving business) (1) Where deemed necessary for the sound development of the motor vehicle management business, the head of a Si/Gun/Gu may issue any of the following orders to a motor vehicle management business entity, as prescribed by Presidential Decree: <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Oct. 24, 2017>
  1. Relocation of a place of business;
  2. Improvement of facilities or operation.
  3. Adjustment of commission or a fee;
  4. Other matters prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport for the sound development of the motor vehicle management business.
  (2) If necessary for preventing loss to motor vehicle dealers and motor vehicle owners, the head of a Si/Gun/Gu may order an online information provider registered under Article 65-2(1) for motor vehicles for sale to take the following measures, as prescribed by Presidential Decree: <Added on Oct. 24, 2017>
  1. Improvement of a place of business, computer systems, or operation;
  2. Improvement of terms and conditions of use;
  3. Other matters prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport to prevent loss to motor vehicle dealers and motor vehicle owners.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]

Article 57 (Prohibited acts for motor vehicle management business entities) (1) No motor vehicle management business entity shall engage in any of the following acts: <Amended on Dec. 18, 2012>
  1. Allowing another person to operate the business under his or her own name (including subcontracting in the form of entrustment, delegation, outsourcing, etc. for the whole or part of the business);
  2. Leasing part or all of the place of business to another person or allowing the person to occupy and use the place of business;
  3. Offering and accepting unjust enrichment, or engaging in other unlawful act with regard to the relevant business;
  4. Rejecting a user's request without good cause in relation to the relevant business;
  5. Forcibly selling a product or service not requested by a user regarding the relevant business, demanding compensation in return for a work not requested by a user, or soliciting clients or customers for business purposes.
  (2) No motor vehicle maintenance business entity or the motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. under Article 34(2) shall tune a motor vehicle nor tune it inconsistent with what was approved, except where he or she obtains approval from the head of a Si/Gun/Gu under Article 34 (including cases applied mutatis mutandis in Article 52). <Amended on Jan. 7, 2014; Aug. 11, 2015>
  (3) No motor vehicle dealer (including the servants and employees thereof) shall engage in any of the following conducts: <Amended on Dec. 30, 2013; Jun. 10, 2022>
  1. Intermediating motor vehicle transactions upon receipt of a request for the intermediation of motor vehicle transactions from a person who is not the owner recorded on the register; provided, the same shall not apply where he or she has been asked to intermediate motor vehicle transactions by the person authorized to act in connection with the motor vehicle transactions by the motor vehicle owner listed on the register;
  2. Any of the following wrongful labeling or advertising of a motor vehicle for which he or she intends to engage in intermediation for the sale or purchase:
  a. Labeling or advertising of the motor vehicle for which he or she intends to engage in intermediation for the sale or purchase although it is impossible to conduct any actual transaction as such motor vehicle does not exist;
  b. False or exaggerated labeling or advertising of the motor vehicle for which he or she intends to engage in intermediation for the sale or purchase in terms of the history, price, etc. of such motor vehicle;
  c. Any other labeling or advertising, the content of which is prescribed by Presidential Decree as may undermine motor vehicle transaction business practices or to cause any damage to the buyer of the motor vehicle.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]
  [Title Amended on Aug. 11, 2015]

Article 57-2 (Prohibition of collecting or intermediating scrapped motor vehicles) (1) No person, other than a motor vehicle scrapping and recycling business entity, shall engage in any of the following acts: <Amended on Jun. 10, 2022; Nov. 15, 2022>
  1. Collecting or purchasing to collect motor vehicles to be scrapped for business purposes or engaging in intermediation of such motor vehicles for a motor vehicle scrapping and recycling business entity;
  2. Labeling or advertising any act falling under subparagraph 1.
  (2) No person, other than a motor vehicle dealer, shall label or advertise a motor vehicle for sale or a motor vehicle for which he or she intends to engage in intermediation for the sale or purchase, for business purposes. <Added on Jun. 10, 2022>
  (3) Any person who intends to label or advertise, via the Internet, a motor vehicle owned by another person for the purpose of selling such motor vehicle or intermediating the sales thereof shall obtain the prior consent of the owner of the motor vehicle. <Added on Dec. 2, 2025>
  [This Article Added on Aug. 11, 2015]
  [Title Amended on Dec. 2, 2025]

Article 58 (Obligations of motor vehicle management business entities to give notice and conduct management) (1) Where a motor vehicle dealer (including his or her employees engaged in dealing) intermediates the sale and purchase of a motor vehicle, he or she shall give written notice of the following matters to the buyer of the relevant motor vehicle prior to concluding a sales contract in accordance with Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport: <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Dec. 30, 2013; Jan. 6, 2015; Oct. 24, 2017; Dec. 26, 2017; Feb. 13, 2024>
  1. Details of a checkup (including photographs taken at the scene of checkup and prepared within 120 days from the date of checkup) of the performance and condition of the structure and devices of the relevant motor vehicle (hereinafter referred to as "checkup of the performance and condition of a motor vehicle") by any of the persons specified by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport;
  2. Any registration of seizure and mortgage;
  3. A fee or charge received under Article 65(1);
  4. Where the buyer wishes, details of the inspection or appraisal of price of the relevant motor vehicle.
  (2) A person who intends to conduct a checkup on the performance and condition of a motor vehicle shall meet the facilities, equipment, and qualification requirements prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport for each place of business and shall file a report thereon with the head of a Si/Gun/Gu, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. The same shall also apply where any modification is to be made to an important matter prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport among the matters reported or where the person intends to close a place of business. <Amended on Dec. 26, 2017; Nov. 15, 2022>
  (3) Upon receipt of a report under the former part of paragraph (2) or a report on modification under the latter part of that paragraph, the head of a Si/Gun/Gu shall examine the details of such report and accept the report if it conforms to this Act. <Added on Nov. 15, 2022>
  (4) Where a motor vehicle dealer (including the servants and employees thereof) advertises motor vehicles using the Internet, he or she shall post the history of such motor vehicles, seller information, and other necessary matters, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Added on May 24, 2011; Mar. 23, 2013; Jun. 10, 2022; Nov. 15, 2022>
  (5) A motor vehicle maintenance business entity shall meet the following requirements: <Amended on May 24, 2011; May 23, 2012; Mar. 23, 2013; Jan. 7, 2014; Jan. 28, 2016; Nov. 15, 2022; Feb. 20, 2024>
  1. Deleted; <Jan. 6, 2015>
  2. He or she shall inform the customer of new parts (unused parts including parts ordered and manufactured by the automobile manufacturer or replacement parts including quality-certified parts), secondhand parts, recycled parts or replacement parts pursuant to Article 30-5, etc. necessary for the maintenance when the customer requests maintenance services so that the customer may make an informed choice;
  3. Where providing maintenance services using a secondhand part or a recycled part, he or she shall check whether the relevant part has any problem;
  4. He or she shall disclose the standard maintenance time via the Internet, printed materials or other methods of disclosure, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport;
  5. With respect to the major maintenance works prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, he or she shall post in his or her place of business the hourly labor charges and the standard maintenance time so that they are readily visible to the person who has requested the maintenance works;
  6. He or she shall issue an estimate and a detailed statement of checkup and maintenance services and inform the details of ex post facto management to a customer who requests maintenance services under the procedures prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport;
  7. He or she shall provide ex post facto management services, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport;
  8. He or she shall not prepare and issue a misleading estimate and a misleading detailed statement of checkup and maintenance services.
  (6) A motor vehicle scrapping and recycling business entity shall comply with the following requirements: <Amended on Jan. 28, 2016; Nov. 15, 2022; Jan. 30, 2024; Feb. 20, 2024>
  1. Where the motor vehicle scrapping and recycling business entity receives a request to scrap a motor vehicle from a motor vehicle owner or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu, he or she shall acquire the relevant motor vehicle, its motor vehicle registration certificate, registration license plate and the seal, and issue a document certifying the fact thereof, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport;
  1-2. Where the motor vehicle scrapping and recycling business entity receives a request to scrap a motor vehicle, he or she shall scrap the relevant motor vehicle except for exporting the motor vehicle or a device prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport or selling it to an exporter;
  2. Where the motor vehicle scrapping and recycling business entity receives a request to scrap a motor vehicle, he or she shall discard the registration certificate and registration license plate of the relevant motor vehicle to make them unusable;
  3. The entity shall ensure that his or her employees engaged in motor vehicle scrapping and recycling business display their identity, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport;
  4. The entity shall require its employees engaged motor vehicle scrapping and recycling business to receive education on matters to be observed in the motor vehicle scrapping and recycling, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport;
  5. Other matters prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport for the motor vehicle scrapping and recycling.
  (7) Deleted. <Jan. 28, 2016>
  (8) A person who conducts a checkup on the performance and condition of a motor vehicle pursuant to paragraph (2) (hereinafter referred to as "motor vehicle performance and condition checkup provider") shall comply with the following: <Added on Nov. 15, 2022>
  1. He or she shall provide the details of a checkup on the performance and condition of a motor vehicle (including the photographs taken during the checkup) to a person who requests such checkup, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport;
  2. He or she shall neither conduct a false checkup of the performance and condition of a motor vehicle nor provide any details different from those of the actual checkup;
  3. He or she shall complete education related to a checkup on the performance and condition of a motor vehicle on a regular basis, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.
  (9) Where a motor vehicle management business entity (including a motor vehicle performance and condition checkup provider; hereafter in this Article, the same shall apply) has performed the business affairs prescribed in paragraphs (1), (5), (6), and (8), it shall keep a record thereof and manage and retain such record, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on May 24, 2011; Mar. 23, 2013; Jan. 28, 2016; Nov. 15, 2022>
  (10) A motor vehicle management business entity shall transmit the matters prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, out of the information recorded, managed, and retained pursuant to paragraph (9), as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, to the electronic information processing organization established under Article 69. <Added on Dec. 18, 2012; Mar. 23, 2013; Nov. 15, 2022>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]
  [Title Amended on Nov. 15, 2022]

Article 58-2 (Exemplary business entities) (1) The head of a Si/Gun/Gu may designate a motor vehicle management business entity with outstanding business performance as an exemplary business entity pursuant to the designation criteria determined by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (2) The head of a Si/Gun/Gu shall without delay revoke designation of a person designated as an exemplary business entity under paragraph (1) where such person fails to meet any of the designation criteria, or is subjected to an administrative disposition under Article 66.
  (3) Matters regarding procedures for designating exemplary business entities and the revocation thereof under paragraphs (1) and (2) shall be determined by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]

Article 58-3 (Liability for damages by motor vehicle management business entities) (1) Where any property loss has occurred to a motor vehicle dealer because a motor vehicle dealer fails to give a notice under each subparagraph of Article 58(1) or gives a false notice when selling or brokering the sale of a motor vehicle, the motor vehicle dealer shall pay the relevant damages. Where any property loss has occurred to the motor vehicle dealer because a motor vehicle performance and condition checkup provider provides false or erroneous information on the checkup of performance and condition to the motor vehicle dealer, the motor vehicle dealer may claim indemnity for such loss from the motor vehicle performance and condition checkup provider. <Amended on Jan. 6, 2015; Oct. 24, 2017; Dec. 26, 2017; Oct. 20, 2020; Nov. 15, 2022>
  (2) A motor vehicle dealer shall subscribe to guaranty insurance, participate in mutual aid under Article 68-17, or make a deposit before commencing business, as prescribed by Presidential Decree, to guarantee liability for damage under paragraph (1). <Amended on Mar. 28, 2023>
  (3) No deposit made under paragraph (2) shall be recovered within one year after the date the motor vehicle dealer closes his or her business or deceases.
  (4) Where the sale, or brokerage of the sale, of a motor vehicle is completed, a motor vehicle dealer shall explain to the parties to the relevant transaction the following matters regarding guarantee for liability for damage, and shall issue a copy of the relevant certificate (including the relevant certificate regarding the guarantee liability of a motor vehicle performance and condition checkup provider under Article 58-4(2); hereafter in this Article and Article 84(4)23, the same shall apply) or shall transmit the relevant certificate in electronic format: <Amended on Oct. 24, 2017; Feb. 4, 2020; Oct. 20, 2020; Mar. 28, 2023>
  1. Amount of guarantee;
  2. Names of the insurance company, the person who conducts mutual aid business, and the depository organization and their addresses;
  3. Period of guarantee.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]

Article 58-4 (Guarantee liability of motor vehicle performance and condition checkup provider) (1) A motor vehicle performance and condition checkup provider shall provide guarantee for the results of the checkups of performance and condition, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.
  (2) A motor vehicle performance and condition checkup provider shall buy insurance indemnifying the guarantee liability under paragraph (1).
  (3) Matters regarding the category, coverage, procedures, etc. of the insurance under paragraph (2) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Newly Amended on Oct. 24, 2017]
  [Previous Article 58-4 moved to Article 58-5 <Oct. 24, 2017>]

Article 58-5 (Qualifications for motor vehicle price inspectors or appraisers) Any of the following persons may inspect or appraise prices of motor vehicles pursuant to Article 58(1)4:
  1. A professional engineer in mechanical field under Article 3 of the Professional Engineers Act who has completed the training on motor vehicle price inspection or appraisal as prescribed by Presidential Decree;
  2. A person who has obtained qualification as at least the craftsman motor vehicles maintenance, and also holds a license for motor vehicle diagnosis or appraisal officially accredited by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport pursuant to Article 2 of the Framework Act on Qualifications.
  [This Article Added on Jan 6, 2015]
  [Moved from Article 58-4; previous Article 58-5 moved to Article 58-6 <Oct. 24, 2017>]

Article 58-6 (Revocation of purchase and sales agreements) (1) A motor vehicle purchaser who has concluded a purchase and sales agreement through a motor vehicle dealer’s dealing or intermediation of the sale may revoke such agreement within 30 days from the date of delivery of the motor vehicle in cases falling under subparagraph 1 or 2 and within 90 days from the date of delivery of the motor vehicle in cases falling under subparagraph 3: <Amended on Mar. 28, 2023>
  1. Where the relevant motor vehicle’s mileages and records of accidents are different from the details of the notice given pursuant to Article 58(1)1;
  2. Where a notice is not given or a false notice is given in connection with the matter prescribed in Article 58(1)1 (excluding the fact that the relevant motor vehicle is flooded) or the matter prescribed in Article 58(1)2;
  3. Where the fact that the relevant motor vehicle is flooded is different from the details of the notice given under Article 58(1)1 or where a false notice of such fact is given among the matters specified in Article 58(1)1 or a notice of such fact is not given.
  (2) Where a motor vehicle purchaser has revoked a purchase and sales agreement pursuant to paragraph (1), he or she shall promptly return the motor vehicle to the motor vehicle dealer.
  (3) A motor vehicle dealer shall return the money already received to the motor vehicle purchaser at the time the motor vehicle is returned under paragraph (2).
  [This Article Added on Jan. 28, 2016]
  [Moved from Article 58-5 <Oct. 24, 2017>]

Article 59 (Management of motor vehicles for sale) (1) In any of the following cases, a motor vehicle dealer shall report to the head of a Si/Gun/Gu, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport; provided, the same shall not apply where a motor vehicle is displayed at an auction house under Article 60: <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  1. Where a motor vehicle for sale is displayed at a place of business;
  2. Where a motor vehicle for sale is sold;
  3. Where a motor vehicle for sale is not sold and returned to its owner.
  (2) A motor vehicle dealer shall comply with the following: <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Dec. 26, 2017>
  1. Managing a motor vehicle for sale displayed at a motor vehicle dealer's place of business as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport;
  2. Preparing a register for managing motor vehicle transactions and keeping said register until the period prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport;
  3. Ensuring that his or her employees engaged in the sale of motor vehicles display their identity, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport;
  4. Ensuring that his or her employees engaged in the sale of motor vehicles complete educational courses on rules, etc. on the sale of motor vehicles, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport;
  5. Other matters prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport to protect the rights and interests of motor vehicle buyers.
  (3) No motor vehicle dealer shall ask a motor vehicle performance and condition checkup provider to fraudulently check the performance and condition of the structure, devices, etc. of the motor vehicle. <Added on Oct. 24, 2017>
  (4) No motor vehicle dealer shall employ any of the following persons as his or her employee: <Added on Feb. 13, 2024>
  1. A person in whose case two years have not passed since the complete execution of or exemption from a sentence for imprisonment with labor or heavier punishment for violation of Article 58(1)1 by arranging the sale or purchase of a motor vehicle by failing to notify the fact of flooding among the details of a checkup on the performance and condition of a motor vehicle or by making a false notification, in violation of and the execution of such sentence is terminated a person for whom two years have not elapsed since the date on which the execution is deemed completed) or exempted;
  2. A person under suspension of execution after having been sentenced to the suspension of execution of a sentence for imprisonment with labor or heavier punishment for violation of Article 58(1)1 by failing to notify the fact of flooding among the details of a checkup on the performance and condition of a motor vehicle or by arranging the sale or purchase of a motor vehicle.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]

Article 60 (Establishment and operation of motor vehicle auction houses) (1) A motor vehicle dealer or an association comprised of a group of motor vehicle dealers under Article 67 may open and operate a motor vehicle auction house (hereinafter referred to as "auction house"), by preparing certain standards for facilities and human resources therefor, and obtaining approval thereof from the Mayor/Do Governor, if it is deemed necessary to ensure reasonable prices of motor vehicles for sale, better adjustment of demand and supply, fostering and development of the motor vehicle management business and order in sales practices. The same shall also apply to any modification to the approved matters; provided, the same shall not apply to any modification to insignificant matters prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Jan. 28, 2016>
  (2) Matters regarding standards for approval in terms of facilities and human resources of an auction house, etc. under paragraph (1) and procedures for approval, etc. shall be determined by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (3) A person who opens and operates an auction house (hereinafter referred to as "founder") shall comply with the following:
  1. He or she shall conduct checkup and inspection on the registered matters and the safety and performance conditions of a motor vehicle which is an object of auction, etc. and notify the results thereof to a person who intends to participate in the auction;
  2. He or she shall not violate this Act or an order or a disposition issued under this Act.
  (4) Matters regarding the standards for checkup and inspection on a motor vehicle which is an object of auction under paragraph (3)1 and the notification method of checkup results, etc. shall be determined by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (5) An auction house under this Act shall not be governed by the provisions concerning an auction house or a market as prescribed by other statutes.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]

Article 61 Deleted. <Apr. 15, 1999>

Article 62 (Participation in auction) Auction participants shall pay a deposit to a founder to guarantee the payment of price of successful bid for a motor vehicle, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]

Article 63 (Refusal to take over auctioned motor vehicles) (1) Where a successful bidder refuses or neglects to take over an auctioned motor vehicle within the agreed-upon period without good cause, a founder shall keep the motor vehicle for a specified period at the expense of the relevant successful bidder or urge him or her to take it over.
  (2) A founder may put an auctioned motor vehicle up again at auction, where a successful bidder fails to take the auctioned motor vehicle over or there is any other unavoidable reason after keeping the auctioned motor vehicle for a specified period or urging the successful bidder to take it over under paragraph (1).
  (3) Losses caused by re-auction, etc. as prescribed in paragraph (2) shall be borne by the successful bidder as prescribed in paragraph (1).
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]

Article 64 (Appointment of persons in charge of checkup and maintenance) (1) A motor vehicle maintenance business entity shall appoint a person in charge of checkup and maintenance who will deal with matters concerning the checkup and maintenance of a motor vehicle (hereinafter referred to as "person in charge of maintenance") and shall report thereon to the head of a Si/Gun/Gu. The same shall also apply to the dismissal of a person in charge of maintenance.
  (2) Where a person in charge of maintenance violates this Act or any order or disposition issued under this Act, the head of a Si/Gun/Gu may order the relevant motor vehicle maintenance business entity to dismiss the person in charge of maintenance. In such cases, the dismissed person shall not be re-appointed as a person in charge of maintenance, unless six months have passed since such dismissal.
  (3) Where a person in charge of maintenance fails to report flooding or makes a false report to a motor vehicle maintenance business entity, the head of a Si/Gun/Gu may order the relevant motor vehicle maintenance business entity to suspend his or her duties for a certain period, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Feb. 13, 2024>
  (4) Matters necessary for the qualifications, duties, etc. of persons in charge of maintenance under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Feb. 13, 2024>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]

Article 64-2 (Maintenance technology training) (1) A motor vehicle maintenance business entity may require skilled technicians prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, such as a person in charge of maintenance, to receive education on motor vehicle maintenance (hereinafter referred to as "maintenance technology education") conducted by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.
  (2) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may designate a public institution or a specialized organization related to maintenance as a specialized educational institution, if necessary to provide maintenance technology education.
  (3) Where a specialized educational institution designated pursuant to paragraph (2) provides education, it shall comply with matters prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, such as issuing a certificate of completion of education, preparing and keeping records of education, etc.
  (4) Matters necessary for the details and methods of maintenance technology education, standards and procedures for designation of specialized educational institutions under paragraph (2), cancellation of designation, etc. shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.
  [This Article Added on Jan. 30, 2024]

Article 65 (Fees for motor vehicle management business entities) (1) A motor vehicle management business entity may collect a fee or charge, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (2) A motor vehicle scrapping and recycling business entity shall pay to the owner of the relevant motor vehicle, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, the balance after deducting the cost for motor vehicle scrapping from the estimated value of a motor vehicle for scrapping; provided, where the cost for motor vehicle scrapping exceeds the estimated value of the motor vehicle, he or she may collect the expense in excess, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (3) Where there is a difference between the fee or charge collected in advance from the person in receipt of a motor vehicle for application for the registration of transfer of ownership of the motor vehicle under the main clause of Article 12(2) and the actual expense incurred in filing an application for the registration of transfer of ownership, the motor vehicle dealer shall notify the transferee of the fact within 30 days from the date when such application is filed, and shall return the full amount of difference. <Added on Dec. 30, 2013>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]

Article 65-2 (Registration of providing online information on motor vehicles for sale) (1) Any person who intends to provide motor vehicle dealers with the information under paragraph (3) on motor vehicles for sale through a website (including an application used in mobile phones; hereafter in this Article the same shall apply), so that motor vehicle dealers can purchase motor vehicles from the owners of motor vehicles (excluding corporations; hereafter in this Article the same shall apply), shall meet the standards for registration prescribed by Presidential Decree; and shall be registered with the head of a Si/Gun/Gu. The same shall also apply to any modification to the important matters prescribed by Presidential Decree, among the registered matters.
  (2) A person who intends to be registered under paragraph (1) shall file an application with the head of a Si/Gun/Gu, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.
  (3) A person registered under paragraph (1) (hereinafter referred to as "online information provider for motor vehicles for sale") shall provide information on the following matters with regard to motor vehicles for sale through a website:
  1. Mileage of each motor vehicle;
  2. Photographs of the interior and exterior of each motor vehicle, taken as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport;
  3. The information specified by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, among the information on management of motor vehicle records under Article 69-2;
  4. Purchase price offered for each motor vehicle by a motor vehicle dealer and delivery method.
  (4) An online information provider for motor vehicles for sale shall preserve records of the mileage and registration number of each motor vehicle, the price offered by a motor vehicle dealer for the motor vehicle, the final purchase price, and the registration number of the motor vehicle management business of the motor vehicle dealer, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.
  (5) No online information provider for motor vehicles for sale shall provide online information on motor vehicles for sale to any person other than motor vehicle dealers.
  (6) Article 54 shall apply mutatis mutandis to the grounds for disqualification of an online information provider for motor vehicles for sale. In such cases, "motor vehicle management business" shall be construed as "providing information on motor vehicles for sale online"; "Article 53" as "Article 65-2""; and "person who has filed for the registration of the motor vehicle management business (hereinafter referred to as "motor vehicle management business entity")" as "online information provider for motor vehicles for sale".
  [This Article Newly Amended on Oct. 24, 2017]

Article 66 (Revocation or suspension of business) (1) Where a motor vehicle management business entity falls under any of the following cases, the head of a Si/Gun/Gu may revoke registration of the relevant business or may order such business entity to suspend all of part of the business for a period of up to 6 months; provided, where the business entity falls under subparagraph 1, 12d (limited to cases of intentionally or by negligence failing to notify flooding or falsely notifying it), 15, or 16, the head of a Si/Gun/Gu shall revoke registration of the business: <Amended on May 24, 2011; Dec. 18, 2012; Mar. 23, 2013; Dec. 30, 2013; Jan. 7, 2014; Jan. 6, 2015; Aug. 11, 2015; Jan. 28, 2016; Oct. 24, 2017; Dec. 31, 2018; Dec. 22, 2020; Jun. 10, 2022; Nov. 15, 2022; Feb. 13, 2024; Feb. 20, 2024; Dec. 2, 2025>
  1. Where he or she has filed a registration by fraud or other improper means;
  2. Where he or she fails to commence the business within six months after registration or where a business entity discontinues the business for at least one year;
  3. Where he or she has modified any matter registered without having such modification registered, in violation of the latter part of Article 53(1);
  4. Where he or she fails to meet the registration standards prescribed in Article 53(3) or to comply with the conditions prescribed in paragraph (4) of the same Article;
  5. Where he or she has transferred, acquired, or merged the registered motor vehicle management business without reporting such, in violation of Article 55(1);
  6. Where he or she fails to comply with a business improvement order, as prescribed in Article 56(1);
  7. Where he or she violates any of the subparagraphs of Article 57(1);
  8. Where he or she fails to keep a record and to manage and retain such record, in violation of Article 58(9);
  9. Where he or she fails to subscribe guaranty insurance, in violation of Article 58-3(2); or has commenced the motor vehicle transaction business without depositing a bond;
  10. Where he or she has collected a fee or charge in excess of that prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, in violation of Article 65(1);
  11. Where it is deemed inappropriate for him or her to continue the business due to unsound business operation, a deterioration of the financial structure, or other grounds;
  12. Where a motor vehicle dealer falls under any of the following cases:
  a. Where he or she has sold a motor vehicle or conducted brokerage for sale and purchase of a motor vehicle, but fails to apply for registration of transfer, in violation of Article 12(2);
  b. Where he or she has intermediated a motor vehicle transaction upon receipt of a request therefor from a person who is not the owner recorded on the register, in violation of Article 57(3)1;
  c. Where he or she has placed wrongful labels and advertisements, in violation of Article 57(3)2;
  d. Where he or she has failed to make a notification or made a false notification, in violation of Article 58(1)1 or 2;
  e. Where he or she has undergone the performance and condition checkup as prescribed in Article 58(1) by a person who fails to meet the standards for facilities, equipment and qualification, in violation of paragraph (2) of the same Article;
  f. Where he or she has not returned to the motor vehicle purchaser the payment for such motor vehicle received, in violation of Article 58-6(3);
  g. Where he or she has failed to file a report or filed a false report, in violation of Article 59(1);
  h. Where he or she has failed to comply with the matters prescribed in Article 59(2);
  13. Where a motor vehicle maintenance business entity falls under any of the following cases:
  a. Where he or she has falsified the results of a regular checkup under Article 36-2(1); 
  b. Where he or she has used machines and apparatuses not yet submitted to a precision test for the checkup or maintenance work of a motor vehicle, in violation of Article 40(1);
  c. Where he or she has conducted a checkup or maintenance work at a place other than a business place registered under Article 53(1); provided, the same shall not apply to any emergency measures for any malfunctioning motor vehicles, to any checkup and maintenance work in any island area, which is not connected to the mainland without any motor vehicle maintenance business entity;
  d. Where he or she has performed maintenance work on a motor vehicle in excess of the scope of the subdivided motor vehicle maintenance business work, in violation of Article 53(2);
  e. Where he or she has tuned a motor vehicle without obtaining approval under Article 34 (including cases applied mutatis mutandis in Article 52), in violation of Article 57(2), or has tuned the motor vehicle inconsistently with what was approved for him or her;
  f. Deleted; <Nov. 15, 2022>
  g. Where he or she has failed to comply with any matter prescribed in Article 58(5);
  h. Where he or she has failed to appoint a person in charge of inspection and maintenance or failed to report the appointment or dismissal of a person in charge of inspection and maintenance, in violation of Article 64(1);
  i. Where he or she has failed to comply with an order to dismiss a person in charge of inspection and maintenance, in violation of Article 64(2);
  j. Where a person fails to transmit or falsely transmits the fact of flooding among matters prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, which is transmitted to an electronic data processing system pursuant to Article 58(10), despite the fact that the requester for maintenance has notified the flooding or the person in charge of maintenance has reported the flooding.
  14. Where a motor vehicle scrapping and recycling business entity falls under any of the following cases:
  a. Where he or she has failed to file an application for registration for deletion of a motor vehicle which was requested for scrapping, in violation of Article 13(2);
  b. Where he or she has scrapped a motor vehicle at a place other than a place for scrapping business registered under Article 53(1);
  c. Where he or she has scrapped a motor vehicle requested to be scrapped under Article 58(6)1, whose identification number, etc. is different from the details recorded on the motor vehicle registration certificate;
  d. Where he or she has failed to scrap a motor vehicle requested to be scrapped, in violation of Article 58(6)1-2;
  e. Where he or she has failed to discard the registration certificate and registration license plate of a motor vehicle requested to be scrapped to make them unusable, in violation of Article 58(6)2;
  f. Where he or she has failed to pay to the owner of a motor vehicle in violation of Article 65(2), the balance from the estimated value of a motor vehicle for scrapping after deducting the cost for motor vehicle scrapping;
  15. Where he or she has operated the business during the period of suspension, in violation of an order for business suspension issued under this Article;
  16. Where a motor vehicle management business entity reports a business closure to the head of a tax office having jurisdiction over the place of tax payment, or his or her business registration made under Article 8(9) of the Value-Added Tax Act, Article 168 of the Income Tax Act or Article 111 of the Corporate Tax Act is canceled.
  (2) Where the motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. prescribed in Article 34(2) tunes a motor vehicle, in violation of Article 57(2), without approval pursuant to Article 34 or tunes differently from what was approved for under the same Article, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may revoke registration of his or her business; or may order him or her to fully or partially suspend his or her business within a specified period of up to six months. <Added on Aug. 11, 2015>
  (3) If necessary for ascertaining whether a motor vehicle management business entity meets the requirements for revocation of registration under paragraph (1)16, the head of a Si/Gun/Gu may request the head of the competent tax office to provide information on whether the motor vehicle management business entity has closed its business. The head of the competent tax office so requested shall provide information on whether the business of the motor vehicle management business entity has been closed under Article 36(1) of the Electronic Government Act. <Added on Aug. 9, 2017>
  (4) In any of the following cases, the head of a Si/Gun/Gu may revoke registration as an online information provider for motor vehicles for sale; or may order an online information provider for motor vehicles for sale to fully or partially suspend his or her business operations for a specified period of up to six months; provided, in cases falling under subparagraph 1, the registration shall be revoked: <Added on Oct. 24, 2017>
  1. Where he or she has filed a registration by fraud or other improper means;
  2. Where an online information provider for motor vehicles for sale fails to comply with an improvement order issued under Article 56(2);
  3. Where an online information provider for motor vehicles for sale ceases to meet any of the standards for registration under Article 65-2(1);
  4. Where an online information provider for motor vehicles for sale fails to preserve records of the mileage and registration number of a motor vehicle, the price offered by a motor vehicle dealer for the motor vehicle, the final purchase price, and the registration number of the motor vehicle management business of the motor vehicle dealer, in violation of Article 65-2(4);
  5. Where an online information provider for motor vehicles for sale has provided online information on motor vehicles for sale to any person other than motor vehicle dealers, in violation of Article 65-2(5).
  (5) Where a motor vehicle performance and condition checkup provider falls under any of the following cases, the head of a Si/Gun/Gu may order such inspector to close his or her place of business or to suspend all or part of his or her business for each place of business for a specified period of up to six months; provided, in cases falling under subparagraph 1 or 2, the head of the Si/Gun/Gu shall order the inspector to close his or her place of business: <Added on Nov. 15, 2022>
  1. Where he or she files a report by fraud or other improper means;
  2. Where he or she conducts his or her business during the period of business suspension, in violation of an order to suspend the business;
  3. Where he or she fails to commence his or her business within six months after the head of the Si/Gun/Gu has received a report or a person who has filed a report discontinues his or her business for at least one year;
  4. Where he or she ceases to meet the requirements for reporting prescribed in Article 58(2);
  5. Where he or she fails to comply with the matters specified in Article 58(8);
  6. Where he or she fails to keep a record and to manage and retain such record, in violation of Article 58(9);
  7. Where he or she fails to fulfill the guarantee liability for the details of a checkup on performance and condition, in violation of Article 58-4(1);
  8. Where he or she conducts a checkup on the performance and condition of a motor vehicle without buying insurance, in violation of Article 58-4(2).
  (6) Matters necessary for the criteria and procedures for taking the administrative dispositions prescribed in paragraphs (1), (2), (4), and (5) shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Aug. 11, 2015; Aug. 9, 2017; Oct. 24, 2017; Nov. 15, 2022>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]

Article 67 (Establishment of trade associations) (1) Motor vehicle management business entities may establish an association or a union (hereinafter referred to as "association, etc.") comprised of motor vehicle management business entities according to the classification prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, upon obtaining authorization from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport or the Mayor/Do Governor, if deemed necessary for the sound development of the motor vehicle management business and establishment of its order. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (2) An association, etc. shall be established as a juristic person.
  (3) In order to establish an association, etc., at least 1/5 of the members or those qualified for the membership of the association, etc. shall propose the establishment, prepare the articles of association with the consent of at least 1/3 of the members or those qualified for the membership of the association at the inaugural general meeting, and apply for authorization for such establishment. <Amended on Jan. 17, 2012>
  (4) An association, etc. shall perform the following: <Amended on May 24, 2011; Mar. 23, 2013>
  1. Deleted; <May 24, 2011>
  2. Deleted; <May 24, 2011>
  3. Deleted; <May 24, 2011>
  4. Autonomous guidance for motor vehicle management business entities;
  5. Conducting tasks entrusted by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport or the Mayor/Do Governor and complying with his or her instructions;
  6. Affairs necessary for the common development of the motor vehicle management business entities of the association, etc.
  (5) Matters regarding the articles of association or the guidance and supervision, etc., of an association, etc., shall be determined by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport or municipal ordinance. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (6) Except as provided in this Act, the provisions of the Civil Act governing an incorporated association shall apply mutatis mutandis to the association, etc. <Amended on Jun. 9, 2020>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]

Article 68 (Federation) (1) Associations, etc. may establish a federation of such associations, etc. as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, upon obtaining authorization from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, for the purpose of achieving the common goals. <Amended on May 24, 2011; Mar. 23, 2013>
  (2) The Federation shall perform the following affairs; provided, the business referred to in subparagraph 2 shall be performed by the Federation, etc. where no Federation is established: <Added on May 24, 2011; Mar. 23, 2013; Jan. 30, 2024>
  1. Investigations and research to improve the facilities of the motor vehicle management business and to develop relevant technology;
  2. Education and training of managers and employees;
  3. Investigations into, and research on, the systems of commissions and fees;
  4. Conducting tasks entrusted by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport and complying with the instructions from the Minister;
  5. Management and supervision of performance of affairs of associations, etc.;
  6. Affairs necessary for promoting the motor vehicle management business.
  (3) Article 67(2), (3), (5) and (6) shall apply mutatis mutandis to the federation established under paragraph (1). <Amended on May 24, 2011>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]

CHAPTER VII-2 INTERNATIONAL COMPATIBILITY OF MOTOR VEHICLE SAFETY STANDARDS

Article 68-2 (International compatibility of motor vehicle safety standards) (1) To ensure the compatibility of Korean safety standards for motor vehicles, motor vehicle parts, and pressure-resistant containers with the corresponding international standards (hereinafter referred to as "international compatibility of motor vehicle safety standards, etc."), the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall research and analyze international standards, and formulate and implement a plan for international cooperation on the relevant information, technology, etc. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (2) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall provide administrative or financial support to the companies, institutions, organizations related to the safety standards for motor vehicles, motor vehicle parts, and pressure-resistant containers in their international cooperation activities. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  [This Article Added on May 24, 2011]

Article 68-3 (Formulation of master plan for international compatibility) (1) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall formulate and implement a master plan for international compatibility of motor vehicle safety standards, etc. (hereinafter referred to as "master plan for international compatibility"). <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (2) The following matters shall be included in the master plan for international compatibility:
  1. Current status and conditions of international compatibility of motor vehicle safety standards, etc.;
  2. Objectives and phased promotional strategy for international compatibility of motor vehicle safety standards, etc.;
  3. Matters regarding research and development on international compatibility of motor vehicle safety standards, etc.;
  4. Matters regarding raising and management of financial resources necessary for international compatibility of motor vehicle safety standards, etc.;
  5. Matters regarding the promotional or collaborative system for international compatibility of motor vehicle safety standards, etc.;
  6. Other matters necessary for international compatibility of motor vehicle safety standards, etc.
  (3) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport intends to formulate a master plan for international compatibility, he or she shall hear from the head of a relevant central administrative agency and have the plan undergo deliberation by the National Transport Commission under Article 106 of the National Transport System Efficiency Act before finalizing such plan. The same shall also apply to any proposed modification to the existing master plan (excluding any modification to insignificant matters prescribed by Presidential Decree). <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  [This Article Added on May 24, 2011]

Article 68-4 (Designation and operation of dedicated institution) (1) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may designate a dedicated institution in order to efficiently conduct professional technology reviews and measures for improvement, necessary for ensuring international compatibility of motor vehicle safety standards, etc. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (2) A dedicated institution shall conduct the following duties: <Amended on Apr. 13, 2021>
  1. Research and analysis to develop and increase the effectiveness of motor vehicle safety standards, etc.;
  2. Research and analysis of systems and policies in relation to motor vehicle safety standards, etc. adopted in the Republic of Korea and abroad;
  3. Review to assess technical feasibility and safety required for improving the international compatibility of motor vehicle safety standards, etc.;
  4. Assistance for international cooperation and operation of overseas offices to accommodate the international standards in relation to motor vehicle safety;
  5. Training of experts to improve the international compatibility of motor vehicle safety standards, etc.;
  6. Other duties deemed necessary by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport for improving the international compatibility of motor vehicle safety standards, etc.
  (3) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may make contributions or provide subsidiaries to a dedicated institution under paragraph (1) for expenses incurred in performing the duties under the subparagraphs of paragraph (2) within the budget. <Added on Apr. 13, 2021>
  (4) Where a person designated under paragraph (1) falls under any of the following, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may revoke such designation or fully or partially suspend his or her duties for a specified period not exceeding six months; provided, in cases falling under subparagraph 1, such designation shall be revoked: <Added on Apr. 13, 2021>
  1. Where he or she has been designated by fraud or other improper means;
  2. Where he or she fails to meet the designation standards under paragraph (5).
  (5) Matters necessary for the designation of a dedicated institution under paragraph (1) and the standards, procedures, etc. for the revocation of designation and the suspension of duties under paragraph (4) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Added on Apr. 13, 2021>
  [This Article Added on May 24, 2011]
  [Title Amended on Apr. 13, 2021]

Article 68-5 (Research and development regarding international compatibility of motor vehicle safety standards) (1) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may conduct the following projects for international compatibility of motor vehicle safety standards, etc.: <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  1. Research and development, and transfer and dissemination, of the technology for international compatibility of motor vehicle safety standards, etc.;
  2. Formulation and revision of Korean motor vehicle safety standards relating to international compatibility of motor vehicle safety standards, etc.;
  3. International cooperation and exchanges for international compatibility of motor vehicle safety standards, etc.;
  4. Support for strengthening technology competitiveness of small and medium enterprises for international compatibility of motor vehicle safety standards, etc.
  (2) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may have any of the following persons to conduct the projects referred to in paragraph (1); provided, the Minister shall subsidize research and development, within the budget: <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  1. A government-invested research institute established under the Act on the Establishment, Operation and Fostering of Government-funded Research Institutes;
  2. A motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. and parts manufacturer, etc.;
  3. A performance testing agent;
  4. A motor vehicle-related research institution which is an incorporated foundation under the Civil Act or other statutes;
  5. A university or graduate school established under the Higher Education Act or under the Special Act on Establishment and Management of Foreign Educational Institutions in Free Economic Zones and Jeju Free International City;
  6. Other motor vehicle-related research institutions prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Added on May 24, 2011]

Article 68-6 (Management of motor vehicles with new technology) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may take necessary measures as prescribed in a mutual recognition agreement or free trade agreement, etc. between the States with regard to the importation and exportation of the motor vehicles, motor vehicle parts or equipment, which are manufactured, etc. by applying new technologies or features. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  [This Article Added on May 24, 2011]

Article 68-7 (Training of experts) (1) In order to systematically train experts necessary for developing motor vehicle technology, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may provide a person who conducts any of the following projects with administrative and financial support: <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  1. Training of motor vehicle-related experts in such areas as machinery, electricity, electronics, etc.;
  2. Development and dissemination of motor vehicle-related educational programs;
  3. Other projects prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport in order to train motor vehicle-related experts.
  (2) Where necessary to provide support for the projects in paragraph (1), the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may designate an agency or organization, etc. relating to the training of motor vehicle-related experts as a collaborative institution. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  [This Article Added on May 24, 2011]

Article 68-8 (Pilot projects) (1) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport deems it necessary to facilitate the research on, and development of, and the use and dissemination of motor vehicle technology, he or she may conduct a pilot project, as determined by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (2) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may provide a person who participates in a pilot project under paragraph (1) with administrative, financial and technological support. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  [This Article Added on May 24, 2011]

CHAPTER VII-3 ESTABLISHMENT OF MOTOR VEHICLE SERVICE MULTIPLE COMPLEXES

Article 68-9 (Formulation of national master plan for development of motor vehicle service multiple complexes) (1) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may formulate a national master plan for the development of a motor vehicle service industrial multiple complex necessary to collectively establish or foster the facilities concerning motor vehicles and related commercial or cultural facilities for motor vehicles’ registration, sales, inspection, distribution of parts, exhibition and advertisement (hereinafter referred to as "motor vehicle service multiple complex").
  (2) The national master plan for the motor vehicle service multiple complex under paragraph (1) shall include the following matters:
  1. Direction of implementing efficient development of the motor vehicle service multiple complex;
  2. Inspection and analysis of the status of facilities relating to major motor vehicle service;
  3. Analysis on the demand for development of the motor vehicle service multiple complex and location thereof;
  4. Rough estimation of project costs required for the establishment of the motor vehicle service multiple complex;
  5. Other matters prescribed by Presidential Decree, as required for development and vitalization of the motor vehicle service multiple complex.
  (3) Matters regarding the procedures, etc. for the formulation and modification of the national master plan for the motor vehicle service multiple complex pursuant to paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Added on Jan 6, 2015]

Article 68-10 (Designation and development of motor vehicle service multiple complexes) (1) The Mayor/Do Governor may designate a motor vehicle service multiple complex to promote the convenience of users of motor vehicle-related services and to facilitate sound development of motor vehicle-related business. In such cases, even where the head of a Si/Gun/Gu requests designation of a necessary motor vehicle service multiple complex, the Mayor/Do Governor may also designate the same as requested.
  (2) Article 4, 5, and 7 through 10 of the Urban Development Act shall apply mutatis mutandis to the procedures for formulation, designation and revocation of a development project for the motor vehicle service multiple complex; Article 12 of the Urban Development Act shall apply mutatis mutandis to the entrusted development project for the motor vehicle service multiple complex; Article 17 through 19 of the Urban Development Act shall apply mutatis mutandis to the preparation, authorization, public notice of an implementation plan for the motor vehicle service multiple complex and deemed authorization or permission upon formulating and authorizing the implementation plan; and Article 28 through 32, 32-2, 32-3, 33 through 36, 36-2, and 37 through 49 of the Urban Development Act shall apply mutatis mutandis where implementing the business of the motor vehicle service multiple complex in the method of land substitution. In such cases, the term "urban development zone" as "motor vehicle service multiple complex", the term "development plan for urban development zone" as "development plan for the motor vehicle service multiple complex", the term "implementation plan for urban development project" shall be construed as "implementation plan for the motor vehicle service multiple complex", and the term "implementation of urban development project" shall be construed as "implementation of a project for the motor vehicle service multiple complex", respectively.
  (3) Article 50 through 52 of the Urban Development Act shall apply mutatis mutandis to the inspection of completion of construction of the development project for the motor vehicle service multiple complex; Article 54, 59, and 71 of the Urban Development Act shall apply mutatis mutandis to the cost-bearing, subsidization, or loan and the reduction and exemption of taxes and apportionments in a development project for the motor vehicle service multiple complex; Article 64 and 65 of the Urban Development Act shall apply mutatis mutandis to access to land owned by third persons in order to construct the motor vehicle service multiple complex and the indemnification for loss, etc. incurred by the land-access; Article 66 and 68 of the Urban Development Act shall apply mutatis mutandis to the restriction on disposal of the State-owned and publicly-owned lands within the land for developing the motor vehicle service multiple complex and concerning the reversion of ownership of the public facilities created by the aforementioned development project; and Article 75 of the Urban Development Act shall apply mutatis mutandis to the administrative dispositions against any violation of the relevant statutes committed by a developer of the project for the motor vehicle service multiple complex. In such cases, the term "urban development zone" as "motor vehicle service multiple complex", the term "development plan for urban development zone" as "development plan for the motor vehicle service multiple complex", the term "implementation plan for urban development project" as "implementation plan for the motor vehicle service multiple complex", and the term "implementation of urban development project" as "implementation of a project for the motor vehicle service multiple complex", respectively.
  [This Article Added on Jan 6, 2015]

Article 68-11 (Developers of development projects for motor vehicle service multiple complexes) Notwithstanding Article 11 of the Urban Development Act, the persons who can be designated as a developer of a development project for the motor vehicle service multiple complex shall be the following persons:
  1. The State or a local government;
  2. An institution prescribed by Presidential Decree among the public institutions prescribed in the Act on the Management of Public Institutions;
  3. Local public enterprises prescribed by the Local Public Enterprises Act;
  4. A corporation incorporated pursuant to special statutes;
  5. An owner of the target land for the motor vehicle service multiple complex (the person in receipt holding a reclamation license issued under Article 28 of the Public Waters Management and Reclamation Act shall be deemed the owner of the relevant public waters and the public waters shall be deemed such land; and in cases of the eminent domain or the method of use pursuant to Article 21 of the Urban Development Act, referring to the person who owns at least two thirds of the motor vehicle service multiple complex excluding the State-owned and publicly-owned lands in the multiple complex);
  6. A corporation satisfying the requirements prescribed by Presidential Decree, incorporated by a person prescribed in subparagraphs 1 through 5 who participates in capital contribution for the purpose of developing the motor vehicle service multiple complex.
  [This Article Added on Jan 6, 2015]

Article 68-12 (Expropriation and use of land) Notwithstanding Article 22 of the Urban Development Act, a person who can expropriate or use the land, etc. necessary for the implementation of a project shall be the project developer who falls under subparagraphs 1 through 4 of Article 68-11 or the project developer who falls under subparagraph 6 of Article 68-11 if a person falling under subparagraphs 1 through 4 makes more than 50/100 of the total investment.
  [This Article Added on Jan 6, 2015]

Article 68-13 (Construction permission and approval for use of motor vehicle service multiple complexes) (1) Where a person who intends to construct a motor vehicle-related facility or a supportive motor vehicle service facility within the motor vehicle service multiple complex has obtained construction permission pursuant to Article 11 of the Building Act, he or she shall be deemed to have obtained the following relevant authorization, permission, etc.: <Amended on Jan. 17, 2017; Mar. 31, 2020; Nov. 30, 2021>
  1. Permission for or reports on the construction of temporary buildings pursuant to Article 20(1) and (3) of the Building Act, and reports on construction of the structure prescribed in Article 83 of that Act;
  2. Permission to construct a high-pressure gas storage pursuant to Article 4(3) of the High-Pressure Gas Safety Control Act;
  3. Permission to engage in the activities of development pursuant to Article 56(1)1 of the National Land Planning and Utilization Act; designation of a developer of the urban or military planning facility project pursuant to Article 86 of that Act; authorization of the implementation plan pursuant to Article 88 of that Act;
  4. Permission for or reports on the establishment of discharge facilities pursuant to Article 23 of the Clean Air Conservation Act, Article 33 of the Water Environment Conservation Act, and Article 8 of the Noise and Vibration Control Act;
  5. Permission for the occupation and use of roads pursuant to Article 61 of the Road Act;
  6. Consent to the permission, etc. for construction pursuant to Article 6(1) of the Act on Installation and Control of Firefighting Systems; reports on the construction of firefighting system pursuant to Article 13(1) of the Firefighting System Installation Business Act; permission for the establishment of a factory, etc. pursuant to Article 6(1) of the Act on the Safety Control of Hazardous Substances;
  7. Permission to establish a liquefied petroleum gas storage pursuant to Article 6(1) of the Safety Control and Business of Liquefied Petroleum Gas Act;
  8. Authorization for or reports on the plan to construct an electric facility for private use pursuant to Article 8 of the Electric Utility Act;
  9. Application for registration of the land movement pursuant to Article 64(2) of the Act on the Establishment and Management of Spatial Data;
  10. Permission to construct a temporary storage of explosives pursuant to Article 25(1) of the Act on Control of Guns, Swords and Explosives;
  11. Report on installation of a specific facility subject to the control of soil contamination prescribed in Article 12 of the Soil Environment Conservation Act;
  12. Permission for the occupation and use pursuant to Article 24 of the Sewerage Act; reports on the establishment of draining facilities pursuant to Article 27(3) of that Act; or establishment of a private sewage treatment facility pursuant to Article 34(2) of that Act.
  (2) Where a matter falling under any subparagraph of paragraph (1) falls within the jurisdiction of the administrative institution other than the relevant Mayor/Do Governor or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu, the relevant Mayor/Do Governor or the head of the Si/Gun/Gu shall pre-consult with the head of the aforementioned administrative institution.
  (3) Where a person who has constructed a motor vehicle-related facility or a supportive motor vehicle service facility within the motor vehicle service multiple complex in accordance with paragraph (1), or a person who has constructed a motor vehicle-related facility or a supportive motor vehicle service facility after obtaining deemed permission for construction in accordance with the authorization of an implementation plan formulated under Article 17 of the Urban Development Act, obtains approval for use pursuant to Article 22 of the Building Act with respect to the relevant facility, he or she shall be deemed to have undergone or made the following inspection or report, etc.: <Amended on Jan. 17, 2017; Mar. 31, 2020>
  1. Inspection upon completion of constructing the facility or container, etc. for manufacturing, storing, selling, importing the high-pressure gas prescribed in Article 16(3) of the High-Pressure Gas Safety Control Act; inspection upon completion of the facility for use of the specified high-pressure gas prescribed in Article 20 of that Act;
  2. Inspection upon completion of construction pursuant to Article 62(1) of the National Land Planning and Utilization Act; inspection upon completion of construction pursuant to Article 98(1) of that Act;
  3. Reports on commencing operation of the discharge and prevention facilities pursuant to Article 30 of the Clean Air Conservation Act and Article 37 of the Water Environment Conservation Act;
  4. Inspection upon completion of construction pursuant to Article 14 of the Firefighting System Installation Business Act, inspection upon completion of constructing the factory, etc. prescribed in Article 9 of the Act on the Safety Control of Hazardous Substances;
  5. Inspection upon completion of the construction of the storage facility or gas supplies-manufacturing facility prescribed in Article 18(2) of the Safety Control and Business of Liquefied Petroleum Gas Act;
  6. Inspection prior to using the electric facilities for private use under Article 9 of the Electrical Safety Management Act;
  7. Inspection prior to use pursuant to Article 36 of the Information and Communications Construction Business Act;
  8. Inspection upon completion pursuant to Article 43 of the Act on Control of Guns, Swords and Explosives;
  9. Inspection upon completion of construction of the private sewage treatment facility pursuant to Article 37 of the Sewerage Act.
  (4) The head of the central administrative agency which governs the statutes and regulations regarding the matters falling under any subparagraph of paragraph (1) shall notify the relevant standards for disposition to the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. The same shall also apply to any modification of the aforementioned standards.
  (5) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport receives notification of the standards for disposition pursuant to paragraph (4), he or she shall consolidate them and give a public notice thereof. The same shall also apply where he or she is notified of any modification of the publicly-notified standards for disposition.
  [This Article Added on Jan 6, 2015]

CHAPTER VII-4 MUTUAL AID ASSOCIATION FOR MOTOR VEHICLE TRANSACTION BUSINESS

Article 68-14 (Incorporation of mutual aid association) (1) Motor vehicle dealers may incorporate a mutual aid association (hereinafter referred to as the "mutual aid association") after obtaining authorization thereof from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, in order to promote autonomous economic activities through a mutual cooperative organization, provide various guarantees and loans necessary to conduct motor vehicle transaction business, and compensate for damage related to the sale and purchase of motor vehicles.
  (2) The mutual aid association shall be a corporation and come into existence upon the registration for incorporation at the location of its main office.
  (3) A motor vehicle dealer may join the mutual aid association, as prescribed by its articles of incorporation.
  (4) Matters necessary for qualifications as a member, and executive officers, of the mutual aid association, investments made in and loans provided by the association, its operation, etc. shall be prescribed by its articles of incorporation.
  (5) Matters necessary for the standards and procedures for authorizing the incorporation of the mutual aid association, matters to be included in its articles of incorporation, supervision over the association, etc. shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Added on Mar. 28, 2023]

Article 68-15 (Steering committee of mutual aid association) (1) The mutual aid association shall have a steering committee to deliberate and resolve on the matters regarding its business prescribed in Article 68-17 and to supervise the implementation of its business affairs.
  (2) The members of the steering committee shall be composed of the members of the mutual aid association, experts in the fields of motor vehicle transaction business, finance, insurance, accounting, and law, relevant public officials, and interested parties related to motor vehicle transaction business; and the number of the members shall not exceed 25.
  (3) Matters necessary for the composition, functions, and operation of the steering committee shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Added on Mar. 28, 2023]

Article 68-16 (Grounds for disqualification of members of steering committee) (1) None of the following persons shall serve as a member specified in Article 68-15(2):
  1. A minor, a person under adult guardianship, or a person under limited guardianship;
  2. A person declared bankrupt and not yet reinstated;
  3. A person for whom five years have not elapsed since his or her imprisonment without labor or heavier punishment declared by a court for violating this Act or other finance-related statutes or regulations prescribed by Presidential Decree (including foreign finance-related statutes or regulations), such as the Insurance Business Act, was completely executed (including where the execution is deemed completed) or exempted;
  4. A person who is under suspension of the execution of his or her imprisonment without labor or heavier punishment declared by a court for violating this Act or other finance-related statutes or regulations prescribed by Presidential Decree (including foreign finance-related statutes or regulations), such as the Insurance Business Act;
  5. A person for whom five years have not elapsed since a fine or heavier punishment declared by a court in relation to the business affairs of the mutual aid association under this Act was completely executed (including where the execution is deemed completed) or exempted;
  6. A person who is under suspension of the execution of a fine or heavier punishment in relation to the business affairs of the mutual aid association under this Act;
  7. A person for whom three years have not elapsed since he or she was subject to disciplinary action and dismissal under Article 68-23.
  (2) A member specified in Article 68-15(2) who falls under any subparagraph of paragraph (1) shall lose his or her membership on the date he or she falls thereunder.
  (3) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may request the Commissioner General of the Korean National Police Agency to inquire about criminal history records specified in paragraph (1)3 through 6 and may provide such records to the mutual aid association.
  [This Article Added on Mar. 28, 2023]

Article 68-17 (Business of mutual aid association) (1) The mutual aid association shall perform the following business:
  1. A tender guarantee, a contract guarantee, a damage compensation guarantee, an advance payment guarantee, a loan guarantee, and other guarantees prescribed by Presidential Decree that are necessary for members to conduct motor vehicle transaction business;
  2. Mutual aid business to improve the welfare of persons employed as members and to compensate for loss caused by an occupational accident;
  3. Mutual aid business to compensate for damage caused in relation to the sale and purchase of motor vehicles, which is necessary for the business conducted by members;
  4. Providing and arranging loans necessary for members to conduct motor vehicle transaction business;
  5. Research and education related to motor vehicle transaction business;
  6. Establishing and operating facilities jointly used by members and improving the convenience of members;
  7. Processing information on motor vehicle transaction business conducted by members;
  8. Business entrusted by the State, local governments, or public organizations prescribed by the articles of incorporation;
  9. Business prescribed by the articles of incorporation, which is incidental to the business specified in subparagraphs 1 through 8.
  (2) The Insurance Business Act (excluding Article 193) shall not apply to the business of the mutual aid association prescribed in paragraph (1)2 and 3.
  [This Article Added on Mar. 28, 2023]

Article 68-18 (Regulations on guarantees and mutual aid) (1) Where the mutual aid association intends to conduct business related to the guarantees specified in Article 68-17(1)1 and the mutual aid business prescribed in Article 68-17(1)2 and 3, it shall prepare regulations on guarantees and mutual aid necessary for such business and obtain authorization of such regulations from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. The same shall also apply where any modification is to be made to authorized matters.
  (2) Matters to be included in regulations on guarantees and mutual aid under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Added on Mar. 28, 2023]

Article 68-19 (Transfer of equity in mutual aid association) (1) A current or former member may transfer his or her equity to another member or a person who intends to be a member, as prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (2) A person who takes over equity under paragraph (1) shall succeed to the relevant transferor's rights and obligations accompanying such equity.
  (3) The transfer of equity and establishment of a pledge shall be in the same manner as equity is transferred and a pledge is established under the Commercial Act.
  (4) The provisional seizure or seizure of equity under the procedures for civil execution or the procedures for compulsorily collecting national taxes, etc. or making a disposition for delinquency shall be in the same manner as designated bonds are provisionally seized or seized under Article 233 of the Civil Execution Act.
  [This Article Added on Mar. 28, 2023]

Article 68-20 (Acquisition of equity by mutual aid association) (1) The mutual aid association may acquire equity held by a current or former member in any of the following cases; provided, it shall acquire such equity in cases falling under subparagraph 1 or 3:
  1. Where the mutual aid association intends to reduce the amount of investments;
  2. Where it is necessary for the mutual aid association to exercise a security right against a member;
  3. Where a person who has made investments in the mutual aid association requests it to transfer equity to the person in order to recover his or her investments;
  4. Where any fractional account occurs at the time reserve funds are transferred into investments;
  5. Where two years have elapsed since a member withdrew from the mutual aid association.
  (2) Upon acquiring equity under paragraph (1), the mutual aid association shall implement the following measures without delay:
  1. Where the mutual aid association acquires the equity on the ground specified in paragraph (1)1: Procedures for reducing its investments;
  2. Where the mutual aid association acquires the equity on any ground specified in paragraph (1)2 through 5: Disposing of the equity to another member or a person who intends to be a member; provided, the amount of investments equivalent to the equity not disposed of may be reduced, as prescribed by the articles of incorporation.
  (3) No equity of a member shall be the object of a pledge unless it is provided as security for any obligation to the mutual aid association.
  (4) Where the mutual aid association acquires equity under paragraph (1), it shall without delay pay a current or former member the amount to be paid.
  (5) Where the mutual aid association acquires equity under paragraph (1), a current or former member's right to claim liquidation money shall expire by prescription unless it is exercised for five years from the date of acquisition of the equity.
  [This Article Added on Mar. 28, 2023]

Article 68-21 (Maintenance of financial soundness) (1) The mutual aid association shall satisfy the financial soundness standards prescribed by Presidential Decree regarding the following so as to ensure its solvency and managerial soundness:
  1. Matters regarding appropriateness of capital;
  2. Matters regarding soundness of assets;
  3. Matters regarding ensuring liquidity.
  (2) Where the mutual aid association is deemed likely to undermine its managerial soundness for the failure to satisfy the standards prescribed in paragraph (1), the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may order the association to increase its capital or may restrict it from holding stocks or other risky assets, as prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Added on Mar. 28, 2023]

Article 68-22 (Orders to improve business affairs of mutual aid association) Where the mutual aid association is deemed likely to undermine the rights and interests of persons who participate in mutual aid, motor vehicle buyers, or other relevant persons due to the inappropriate management of its business affairs and poor asset status, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may order the association to take the following measures:
  1. Changing the methods of performing business affairs;
  2. Changing asset depository institutions;
  3. Changing the book value of assets;
  4. Holding reserves for bad assets;
  5. Writing off assets deemed to be of no remaining value as losses.
  [This Article Added on Mar. 28, 2023]

Article 68-23 (Sanctions against executive officers or employees of mutual aid association) Where an executive officer or employee of the mutual aid association who falls under any of the following is deemed to fail to conduct mutual aid business, etc. in a sound manner or deemed likely to undermine the rights and interests of buyers of motor vehicles or other relevant persons, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may request the association to take disciplinary actions against, or dismiss, the executive officer or employee or may order it to correct the relevant violation:
  1. Where he or she conducts business affairs, in violation of the regulations on guarantees and mutual aid under Article 68-18;
  2. Where he or she fails to satisfy the financial soundness standards specified in Article 68-21;
  3. Where he or she fails to comply with an order for improvement issued under Article 68-22.
  [This Article Added on Mar. 28, 2023]

Article 68-24 (Investigation and inspection) (1) If deemed necessary to maintain the financial soundness of the mutual aid association and to protect the rights and interests of motor vehicle buyers or other relevant persons, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may order the association to report matters regarding its business affairs or to submit data and may require public officials under his or her control to investigate the status of its business affairs or accounting or to inspect books or other documents.
  (2) If the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport intends to conduct an investigation or inspection under paragraph (1), he or she shall notify the mutual aid association of a plan for such investigation or inspection, including the details to be investigated or inspected, and the date and time of, and reasons for, the investigation or inspection; provided, this shall not apply in case of an emergency or where it is deemed that prior notification makes it impossible to achieve the purpose of the investigation due to the destruction of evidence, etc.
  (3) A public official who conducts an investigation or inspection under paragraph (1) shall carry identification verifying his or her authority and present it to relevant persons.
  (4) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may request the Governor of the Financial Supervisory Service under the Act on the Establishment of Financial Services Commission to conduct an investigation or inspection with regard to mutual aid business.
  (5) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall determine and publicly notify the guidelines necessary for supervision after consultation with the Chairperson of the Financial Services Commission in order to promote guarantee business and mutual aid business prescribed in Article 68-17(1)1 through 3 in a sound manner and to protect contractors.
  [This Article Added on Mar. 28, 2023]

Article 68-25 (Application mutatis mutandis of other statutes) Except as provided in this Act, the provisions of the Civil Act governing incorporated associations and the provisions of the Commercial Act governing accounting of stock companies shall apply mutatis mutandis to the mutual aid association.
  [This Article Added on Mar. 28, 2023]

CHAPTER VIII SUPPLEMENTARY PROVISIONS

Article 69 (Electronic processing of motor vehicle management affairs) (1) If deemed necessary for the efficient management of motor vehicles, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may handle affairs prescribed by this Act using an electronic information processing organization, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (2) A person who intends to use data processed by the electronic information processing organization as prescribed in paragraph (1) (hereinafter referred to as "electronic data") shall obtain approval from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport after deliberation by the head of the relevant central administrative agency, as prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (3) Upon receipt of a request for approval under paragraph (2), the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may grant approval thereof, only where it is deemed that there is no harm when the motor vehicle management affairs are performed and it does not violate the privacy and freedom of a motor vehicle owner. In such cases, he or she may grant approval, limiting such use. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (4) Matters regarding the range of the use of electronic data and the standards for deliberation and approval, etc. thereof, as prescribed in paragraphs (2) and (3) shall be determined by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]

Article 69-2 (Provision of information on management of motor vehicle records) (1) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may provide a motor vehicle owner, etc. with the comprehensive records of his or her motor vehicle, such as the manufacturing, registering, inspecting, maintaining, and scraping of the motor vehicle (hereinafter referred to as "information on management of motor vehicle records").
  (2) A person who intends to obtain the information on management of motor vehicle records pursuant to paragraph (1) shall request, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport for provision of the information through the electronic civil petition window under Article 12-2(2) of the Civil Petitions Treatment Act. <Amended on Jan. 11, 2022>
  (3) When the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport provides information to any persons other than a motor vehicle owner pursuant to paragraph (2), he or she shall obtain the motor vehicle owner’s consent and take other measures necessary to protect the motor vehicle owner’s personal information.
  (4) Matters necessary for taking measures to protect personal information pursuant to paragraph (3), details of the information available for provision, recipients of information to be provided, and methods of providing information shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Added on Jan 6, 2015]

Article 69-3 (Development and management of professional auto mechanics) (1) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport or a Mayor/Do Governor may formulate and implement a policy for the development and management of human resources who have techniques or skills for maintenance of motor vehicles (hereinafter referred to as "professional auto mechanics").
  (2) The policy formulated pursuant to paragraph (1) shall include the following matters:
  1. Supply and utilization of professional auto mechanics;
  2. Development and training of professional auto mechanics;
  3. Management and certification of work experience records of professional auto mechanics;
  4. Other matters prescribed by Presidential Decree as those necessary for the development and management of professional auto mechanics.
  (3) If necessary to implement the policy under paragraph (1), the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport or a Mayor/Do Governor may provide support to organizations, associations, universities, etc. related to professional auto mechanics, as prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (4) Matters necessary for the development and management of, and support for, professional auto mechanics under paragraphs (1) through (3) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Added on Nov. 26, 2019]

Article 69-4 (Monitoring of online labeling and advertising for motor vehicles) (1) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may monitor whether online labeling or advertising for motor vehicles complies with Article 57(3)2, 57-2(2), and 58(4). <Amended on Nov. 15, 2022>
  (2) When necessary for monitoring under paragraph (1), the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may request a provider of information and communications services (referring to a provider of information and communications services defined in Article 2(1)3 of the Act on Promotion of Information and Communications Network Utilization and Information Protection; hereinafter the same shall apply) to submit relevant data. In such cases, the provider of information and communications services requested to submit relevant data shall comply with such request without good cause. <Amended on Dec. 2, 2025>
  (3) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may request a provider of information and communications services to take necessary measures, such as verification of labels or advertisements suspected of violating this Act or posting of additional information, based on the result of the monitoring conducted pursuant to paragraph (1). In such cases, the provider of information and communications services requested to take necessary measures shall comply with such request without good cause.
  (4) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may entrust affairs related to monitoring specified in paragraph (1) to an institution prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (5) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may provide an institution entrusted with affairs under paragraph (4) with subsidies to help it cover the expenses incurred in performing the entrusted affairs, within the budget.
  (6) Matters necessary for the details of monitoring under paragraph (1), methods and procedures therefor, etc. shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport.
  [This Article Added on Jun. 10, 2022]

Article 69-5 (Obligations of providers of information and communications services) (1) A provider of information and communications services who intends to label or advertise a motor vehicle owned by another person for the purpose of selling such motor vehicle or intermediating the sales thereof [excluding motor vehicle dealers (including their employees and workers)] may publish such labelling or advertisements regarding the motor vehicle only if they have obtained the prior consent of the owner of the motor vehicle in accordance with Article 57-2(3).
  (2) Where a provider of information and communications services publishes a label or advertisement under paragraph (1), it shall indicate whether the owner of the motor vehicle has given prior consent.
  [This Article Added on Dec. 2, 2025]

Article 70 (Special cases concerning motor vehicle management) (1) Notwithstanding the provisions of this Act, registration (referring to a report on use in cases of motorcycles), self-authentication of motor vehicles, self-authentication of parts, checkup, maintenance, inspection, scrapping, and registration license plates (referring to motorcycle license plates in cases of motorcycles) of the following motor vehicles shall be governed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Feb. 20, 2024; Mar. 19, 2024>
  1. Motor vehicle owned by a diplomat stationed in the Republic of Korea;
  2. Private motor vehicle used by a member or civilian personnel of the United States Army stationed in the Republic of Korea or their families;
  3. Motor vehicle owned by an employee of the United Nations or international organizations equivalent thereto;
  4. Motor vehicle operated within the Korean territory from among motor vehicles owned by the people of the Party to the Convention On Road Traffic (excluding natives) and motor vehicles operated in a member country of the Party to the Convention on Road Traffic (excluding the Republic of Korea) from among motor vehicles registered in the Korean territory;
  5. Motor vehicle temporarily imported under the condition that it will be re-exported under the Customs Act;
  6. Motor vehicle deemed especially necessary for the maintenance of national security and public safety, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport;
  7. Motor vehicle used only at a place other than roads (referring to roads and other places used for general traffic, as prescribed by the Road Act);
  8. Motor vehicle manufactured and assembled for export.
  (2) Matters concerning self-authentication of autonomous driving motor vehicles that intend to obtain performance certification under Article 40 of the Act on Promotion of and Support for Commercialization of Autonomous Driving Motor Vehicles and the checkup, maintenance, and inspection of autonomous driving motor vehicles approved for compliance under Article 41 of that Act shall be separately prescribed by Act. <Added on Mar. 19, 2024>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]

Article 71 (Prohibition on illegal use) (1) A person shall neither fabricate, alter, or use illegally a motor vehicle registration certificate, a document certifying the fact of motor vehicle scrapping, a registration license plate, a temporary operation permit, a temporary operation permit license plate, a mark of self-certification of motor vehicles or motor vehicle parts, a carving or mark of inspection of pressure-resistant containers, a carving or mark of re-examination of pressure-resistant containers, a certificate of new inspection, a motorcycle license plate, a mark of a motor vehicle chassis, and a mark of an engine model, as prescribed by this Act, nor sell, intermediate the sale of, give, receive, or use one fabricated or altered. <Amended on May 24, 2011>
  (2) No person shall manipulate the odometer of a motor vehicle; provided, this shall not apply where it is necessary to change the odometer due to malfunction, destruction, or any other unavoidable cause prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]

Article 72 (Reporting and inspections) (1) If deemed necessary for motor vehicle management, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment (only pertaining to services related to comprehensive inspections and motorcycle inspections), the Mayor/Do Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu may require the following persons to report on such management or services relating thereto: <Amended on May 24, 2011; Mar. 23, 2013; Oct. 24, 2017; Jun. 12, 2018; Nov. 15, 2022; Aug. 16, 2023; Sep. 14, 2023; Jan. 30, 2024; Oct. 1, 2025; Feb. 27, 2026>
  1. A user of a motor vehicle;
  2. An agent of a registration license plate issuance;
  3. A person who marks the identification number of a motor vehicle and the number of an engine model under Article 22 (including cases applied mutatis mutandis in Article 52);
  4. A manufacturer, etc. of a motor vehicle;
  5. A manufacturer, etc. of motor vehicle parts;
  5-2. A manufacturer, etc. of pressure-resistant containers;
  6. A manufacturer, etc. of machines and apparatuses;
  7. A motor vehicle inspection agent;
  8. A comprehensive inspection agent;
  8-2. A performance evaluation agent;
  9. A designated maintenance business entity;
  10. A designated maintenance business entity for comprehensive inspection;
  11. A specialized taximeter verification institution;
  11-2. Motorcycle Inspection Agents;
  11-3. Designated Maintenance Business Entity for Motorcycles;
  12. A motor vehicle management business entity;
  12-2. A specialized educational institution designated under Article 64-2(2);
  13. An agency certifying replacement parts;
  13-2. An agency certifying tuning parts;
  14. An online information provider for motor vehicles for sale;
  15. A person to whom the authority over approval of tuning work for motor vehicles is entrusted under Article 77(8);
  16. A motor vehicle performance and condition checkup provider.
  (2) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment (only pertaining to services related to comprehensive inspections and motorcycle inspections), the Mayor/Do Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu may require a competent public official to access the facilities, equipment, motor vehicle, place of business, or business office of a person falling under any subparagraph of paragraph (1) and inspect the relevant motor vehicle, accounting books, documents, and other relevant items or inquire related persons where deemed necessary. In such cases, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall make sure the entrance and inspection by persons who conduct comprehensive inspections and motorcycle inspections are jointly made and conducted. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Sep. 14, 2023; Oct. 1, 2025>
  (3) When inspection is to be conducted under paragraph (2), a person subject to such inspection shall be notified of the inspection plan, including the date, purpose and details of the inspection, etc., by no later than seven days beforehand; provided, in the case of urgent inspection or where it is deemed that the purpose of inspection cannot be achieved by notifying the plan of inspection in advance, the person who conducts inspection may not give a prior notice, and shall prepare an ex-post facto report immediately after such inspection.
  (4) A public official making entrance and conducting inspection under paragraph (2) shall carry a certificate indicating his or her authority to do so, and present it to related persons.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]

Article 72-2 (Request for data) (1) Where necessary for inspection of motor vehicles (including motorcycles) and for efficient operation of managerial affairs, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport or the Mayor/Do Governor (including the person who is entrusted/sub-entrusted or delegated by the Minister or the Mayor/Do Governor pursuant to Article 77(1) through (4) and 77(9)) may request the State institutions, local governments, the public institutions under the Act on the Management of Public Institutions, the insurance companies and the quantum institutions for calculating insurance premium rate under the Insurance Business Act and other relevant institutions, etc. to submit necessary data.
  (2) A person requested to provide data pursuant to paragraph (1) shall comply without good cause.
  [This Article Added on Aug. 11, 2015]

Article 73 (Prohibition measures on violations) (1) In any of the following cases, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, the Mayor/Do Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu may require a relevant public official to order prohibition on such violation, investigate and identify the instrument or facilities used therefor, or make other necessary dispositions (hereinafter referred to as "control"): <Amended on Dec. 18, 2012; Mar. 23, 2013; Dec. 26, 2017; Nov. 15, 2022; Sep. 14, 2023; Feb. 13, 2024>
  1. Dismantling or manipulating any device of a motor vehicle without permission in violation of Article 35(1) (including cases applied mutatis mutandis in Article 52);
  1-2. Where he or she arbitrarily alters, installs, adds, or deletes software that may affect the safe operation of motor vehicles, in violation of Article 35(2);
  2. Maintaining a motor vehicle in violation of Article 36;
  3. Operating the motor vehicle management business without making registration in violation of Article 53(1);
  4. Conducting a checkup on the performance and condition of a motor vehicle without filing a report, in violation of Article 58(2).
  (2) Where a relevant public official maintains control as prescribed in paragraph (1), he or she shall immediately deliver a document detailing such control to a person who is kept under control.
  (3) If it is necessary for control as prescribed in paragraph (1), the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, the Mayor/Do Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu may request cooperation from an association, etc. established under Article 67 and a federation established under Article 68. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (4) Article 72(4) shall apply mutatis mutandis to relevant public officials who maintain control under paragraph (1).
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]

Article 73-2 (Inspections of compliance with motor vehicle safety standards) (1) In order to ascertain whether any motor vehicle not in compliance with the safety standards for motor vehicles is operated in violation of Article 29(1), the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may instruct relevant public officials or safety controllers for motor vehicles (referring to the persons in charge of safety control of motor vehicles, among executive officers and employees of the Korea Transportation Safety Authority; hereinafter the same shall apply) to inspect motor vehicles in operation.
  (2) No owner or operator of a motor vehicle in operation shall refuse, interfere with, or evade an inspection conducted under paragraph (1), without good cause.
  (3) Article 72(4) shall apply mutatis mutandis to relevant public officials or safety controllers for motor vehicles, conducting an inspection under paragraph (1).
  [This Article Added on Dec. 26, 2017]

Article 74 (Imposition of penalty surcharges) (1) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, the Mayor/Do Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu shall render a disposition suspending affairs or business (hereinafter referred to as "disposition of suspension") to an agent of a registration license plate issuance, a performance evaluation agent, a motor vehicle inspection agent, a comprehensive inspection agent, an official approving institution specialized in a taximeter, a motorcycle inspection agent, a motorcycle designated maintenance business entity, or a motor vehicle management business entity where he or she falls under Article 21, 35-14(2), 45-3(1), 47(5), 51-4(1), or 66(1), he or she may impose a penalty surcharge not exceeding 10 million won on such person or institution, in lieu of the disposition of suspension thereto, as prescribed by Presidential Decree, where such disposition of suspension may cause substantial inconvenience to general users or impair public interests; provided, he or she may impose a penalty surcharge not exceeding 50 million won, in cases of replacing the disposition of suspension for a comprehensive inspection agent in relation to the comprehensive inspection. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Sep. 14, 2023; Feb. 27, 2026>
  (2) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may impose a penalty surcharge on any of the following persons within the scope not exceeding 3/100 of the sales of the relevant motor vehicle or motor vehicle part. <Added on Feb. 4, 2020; Feb. 13, 2024>
  1. A person who has concealed or reduced defects, or disclosed defects to the public by falsity, or has failed to correct such defects without delay after he or she became aware of the defects, in violation of Article 31(1) (including cases applied mutatis mutandis in Article 52);
  2. A person who fails to comply with a corrective order under Article 34-6(4);
  (3) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may impose on any of the following persons a penalty surcharge not exceeding 2/100 of the turnover of relevant motor vehicles or motor vehicle parts (if it exceeds 10 billion won, it shall be deemed 10 billion won): <Amended on May 24, 2011; Mar. 23, 2013; Dec. 29, 2015; Feb. 4, 2020; Apr. 13, 2021; Feb. 13, 2024; Feb. 27, 2026>
  1. Any person who has sold a motor vehicle which does not satisfy the safety standards for motor vehicles, in violation of Article 30(1) (including cases applied mutatis mutandis in Article 52);
  2. Any person who has sold the motor vehicle parts not in compliance with the safety standards for motor vehicle parts, in violation of Article 30-2(1) (including cases applied mutatis mutandis in Article 52);
  3. Deleted; <Feb. 4, 2020>
  4. Deleted; <Feb. 4, 2020>
  5. Any person who has sold defective motor vehicles or motor vehicle parts without correcting such defects, in violation of Article 31-4(1) (including cases applied mutatis mutandis in Article 52) (excluding cases falling under subparagraph 1 or 2);
  6. A person who fails to comply with matters to be observed, in violation of Article 34-5(1)1 through 3, and implements an update.
  7. A person who has sold electric vehicles, etc. that failed the pre-distribution safety inspection, in violation of Article 35-16(2).
  (4) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may impose a penalty surcharge not exceeding 1/100 of the turnover of relevant pressure-resistant containers (if it exceeds 10 billion won, it shall be deemed 10 billion won) on any person who has sold pressure-resistant containers that failed to pass the test for pressure-resistant containers, in violation of Article 35-6(1). <Added on Feb. 4, 2020>
  (5) The amount of penalty surcharges, depending on the type and severity of an offense subject to penalty surcharges under paragraphs (1) through (4), and other necessary matters shall be determined by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Feb. 4, 2020>
  (6) Where the penalty surcharge prescribed in paragraphs (1) through (4) is not paid by the due date, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, the Mayor/Do Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu shall collect it in the same manner as delinquent national taxes are collected or under the Act on the Collection of Local Administrative Penalty Charges, as prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Aug. 6, 2013; Feb. 4, 2020; Mar. 24, 2020>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]

Article 74-2 (Compensation for damage) (1) A motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. or a parts manufacturer, etc. shall be liable for any damage to the life, body and property of any person caused by a defect under Article 31(1) (excluding damage incurred only to the relevant motor vehicle or motor vehicle part). <Amended on Feb. 4, 2020>
  (2) Notwithstanding paragraph (1), if a motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. or a parts manufacturer, etc. who has caused serious damage to the life, body or property of any person by knowingly concealing or understating a defect, making a false representation on a defect or failing to correct a defect without delay in accordance with Article 31(1), such motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. or such manufacturer, etc. shall pay damages not exceeding five times the loss inflicted on the person. <Added on Feb. 4, 2020>
  (3) If a person who has sustained any damage under paragraph (1) or (2) proves the following facts, it shall be presumed that the relevant motor vehicle or motor vehicle part has a defect under Article 31(1) and that such damage was caused by the defect: <Added on Feb. 4, 2020>
  1. The fact that the damage under paragraph (1) or (2) occurred while the relevant motor vehicle or motor vehicle part was used in normal conditions;
  2. The fact that the damage under subparagraph 1 is attributable to a cause under actual control of the motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. or the parts manufacturer, etc.;
  3. The fact that the damage under subparagraph 1 would not usually occur without any defect in the relevant motor vehicle or motor vehicle part.
  (4) In determining the amount of damages under paragraph (2), the court shall take the following matters into consideration: <Added on Feb. 4, 2020>
  1. The degree of intentionality;
  2. The seriousness of the damage caused by the relevant defect;
  3. Economic benefits acquired by selling the relevant motor vehicle or motor vehicle part;
  4. If the motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. or the parts manufacturer, etc. was subjected to a criminal punishment or administrative action due to the relevant defect, the severity thereof;
  5. The period during which the supply of relevant motor vehicles or motor vehicle parts continued and the scale of supply;
  6. The financial standing of the motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. or the parts manufacturer, etc.;
  7. The degree of efforts made by the motor vehicle manufacturer, etc. or the parts manufacturer, etc. to remedy damage.
  (5) A person who files a claim for damages under paragraph (1) or (2) may request the performance testing agent to provide information about the investigations under Article 30-3(2), 31(4) and 31-3(1). The performance testing agent shall comply with such request without good cause. <Added on Feb. 4, 2020>
  (6) When a claim for damages is filed pursuant to paragraph (1) or (2), the court may require the performance testing agent to submit the findings from the investigations under Article 30-3(2), 31(4), and 31-3(1), where necessary. <Added on Feb. 4, 2020>
  (7) Article 5 and 6 of the Product Liability Act shall apply to joint and several liability and restrictions on special agreement on indemnification. <Added on Feb. 4, 2020>
  [This Article Added on Dec. 29, 2015]

Article 75 (Hearings) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, the Mayor/Do Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu shall hold a hearing to make any of the following dispositions: <Amended on May 24, 2011; Mar. 23, 2013; Dec. 29, 2015; Oct. 24, 2017; Feb. 4, 2020; Nov. 15, 2022; Aug. 16, 2023; Sep. 14, 2023; Feb. 13, 2024; Feb. 27, 2026>
  1. Revocation of designation under Articles 21, 30-6(1), 35-14(2), 45-3(1), 47(5), and 51-4(1);
  2. Revocation of registration as prescribed in Article 30(6) or 30-2(4);
  3. Orders to suspend manufacturing, assembly, import or sale pursuant to Article 30-3(1) (including cases applied mutatis mutandis in Article 52);
  3-2. Revocation of safety certification under Article 30-7(4) (including cases applied mutatis mutandis in Article 52);
  3-3. Orders to suspend manufacture, assembly, import, or sale pursuant to Article 30-7(4) (including cases applied mutatis mutandis in Article 52);
  3-4. Revocation or suspension of validity of certification of a cyber security management system for motor vehicles under Article 30-11(1);
  4. Orders to correct defects of motor vehicles or motor vehicle parts pursuant to the main clause of Article 31(3) (including cases applied mutatis mutandis in Article 52);
  5. Revocation of certification pursuant to Article 32(2);
  5-2. Corrective orders for updates under Article 34-6(4);
  5-3. Orders to suspend manufacturing, import or sale pursuant to Article 35-9(1) (excluding cases falling under Article 35-10(3) among the cases falling under Article 35-9(1)2);
  5-4. Orders to recall pressure-resistant containers, etc. pursuant to Article 35-10(2);
  6. Revocation of registration as prescribed in Article 54(2) (including cases applied mutatis mutandis in Article 65-2(6)) and 66;
  6-2. Closure of places of business under Article 66(5);
  7. Imposition of the penalty surcharge under Article 74(2) through (4).
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 6, 2009]

Article 76 (Fees) Any of the following persons shall pay fees to the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport (referring to Joint Ministerial Decrees in cases of an application for designation as a designated maintenance business entity for comprehensive inspections or a designated maintenance business entity for motorcycles or for a comprehensive inspection and inspection of motorcycles), the Mayor/Do Governor, the head of a Si/Gun/Gu, or a performance testing agent; provided, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, the Mayor/Do Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu shall conduct technical reviews and safety inspections of motor vehicles under Article 30(3) (including cases applied mutatis mutandis in Article 52), safety performance tests under Article 30-8(1) (including cases applied mutatis mutandis in Article 52); the confirmation of safety performance test facilities and safety performance tests under Article 30-8(3) (including cases applied mutatis mutandis in Article 52); the conformity inspection under Article 30-8(4) (including cases applied mutatis mutandis in Article 52), affairs concerning certification, etc. of a cyber security management system for motor vehicles under the subparagraphs of Article 30-9(5), a performance test of parts or devices of a motor vehicle under Article 32(3) (including cases applied mutatis mutandis in Article 52), verification of data under Article 34-5(3), a test for pressure-resistant containers under Article 35-6(1), an installation test for pressure-resistant containers under Article 35-7(1), the re-examination of pressure-resistant containers under Article 35-8, a performance evaluation under Article 35-13(1), a pre-distribution safety inspection under Article 35-16(1), the motor vehicle inspection under Article 44(1), the comprehensive inspection under Article 44-2(1), the regular inspection under Article 45(1), the comprehensive inspection under Article 45-2(1), the verification of a taximeter under Article 47(2), the affairs of taking as a proxy the measures necessary for revocation of an attachment, the exemption from self-certification, a precision test for machines and apparatuses and the provision of information on management of motor vehicle records pursuant to Article 77(6), the affairs concerning the registration requested electronically (including the electronic and digitalized documents under the Electronic Government Act; hereinafter the same shall apply) pursuant to Article 77(7), the approval of tuning of a motor vehicle under Article 77(8), and vicarious execution or entrustment of affairs on installation or operation of the electronic information processing organization under Article 77(10), a fee determined by a person who performs the respective affairs as an agent or a person entrusted with the respective affairs shall be paid to him or her. <Amended on May 24, 2011; Mar. 23, 2013; Jan. 7, 2014; Jan. 6, 2015; Aug. 16, 2023; Sep. 14, 2023; Feb. 13, 2024; Feb. 20, 2024; Feb. 27, 2026>
  1. A person who makes an application for perusal of the register or delivery of its certified copy or abridged copy under Article 7(4);
  2. A person who makes an application for registration under Article 8(1), 11(1), 12(1), and 13(1);
  2-2. A person who requests taking the measures necessary for revocation of an attachment pursuant to Article 14-2 and who intends to revoke registration of an attachment;
  3. A person who makes an application for re-issuance of a motor vehicle registration certificate under Article 18(2);
  4. A person for whom the delivery of a registration license plate or a motorcycle license plate is made under Articles 19 and 49;
  5. A person who requests designation of a registration license plate issuance agent, a motor vehicle inspection agent, a comprehensive inspection agent, a designated maintenance business entity, a designated maintenance business entity for comprehensive inspection, or a specialized taximeter verification institution, a motorcycle inspection agent, or a designated motorcycle maintenance business entity under Article 20, 44, 44-2, 45, 45-2, 47, 51-2, or 51-3;
  6. A person who requests temporary operation permission for a motor vehicle under Article 27;
  7. A person who requests the technology review and safety inspection of a motor vehicle under Article 30(3) (including cases applied mutatis mutandis in Article 52);
  8. A person who requests the exemption of self-certification under Article 30-4 (including cases applied mutatis mutandis in Article 52);
  8-2. A person who files an application for a safety performance test under Article 30-8(1) (including cases applied mutatis mutandis in Article 52);
  8-3. A person who obtains the confirmation of a safety performance test facility or a safety performance test under Article 30-8(3) (including cases applied mutatis mutandis in Article 52);
  8-4. A person who undergoes a conformity inspection under Article 30-8(4) (including cases applied mutatis mutandis in Article 52);
  8-5. A person who applies for certification of a cyber security management system for motor vehicles pursuant to Article 30-9(1);
  8-6. A person who applies for certification of modification pursuant to Article 30-9(2);
  9. A person who undergoes the performance test for parts or devices used on a motor vehicle under Article 32 (including cases applied mutatis mutandis in Article 52);
  10. A person who requests the approval of tuning of a motor vehicle or a motorcycle under Article 34 (including cases applied mutatis mutandis in Article 52);
  10-2. A person who files an application for verification of data under Article 34-5(3);
  10-3. A person who requests the test for pressure-resistant containers pursuant to Article 35-6(1);
  10-4. A person who requests the installation test for pressure-resistant containers pursuant to Article 35-7(1);
  10-5. A person who requests the re-examination of pressure-resistant containers pursuant to Article 35-8(1);
  10-6. A person who intends to undergo a performance evaluation in accordance with Article 35-13(1) or (2);
  10-7. A person who intends to undergo a pre-distribution safety inspection in accordance with Article 35-16(1);
  11. A person who requests the precision test for machines and apparatuses under Article 40;
  12. A person who requests the motor vehicle inspection or comprehensive inspection under Article 43 or 43-2;
  13. A person who requests the verification of taximeter under Article 47;
